{"name":"Alchemist","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"alchemist","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Alchemist; Alchemy Companion pp.79–86 — Core-Rules Alchemist lists"},"compendiumSourceKey":"alchemist","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-essence","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Alchimiste","system":{"description":"<h1>Alchimiste</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Alchimiste</strong> est un utilisateur pur de l’<strong>Essence</strong> spécialisé dans l’intégration de la magie au monde matériel. Son domaine est la création d’objets, de préparations, de dispositifs et de supports capables de conserver ou transmettre un effet magique.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il correspond à l’artisan savant, au maître des runes, au créateur d’objets de pouvoir et au chercheur capable d’associer connaissance de la matière et maîtrise de l’Essence. Un Noldo peut développer des techniques très anciennes ; un érudit dúnadan du Gondor travailler sur des objets hérités de Númenor ; un maître-artisan d’Umbar conserver des traditions dérivées des Númenóréens Noirs.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales de l’Alchimiste sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong> et <strong>Raisonnement (Re)</strong>. L’Empathie est la caractéristique de royaume de l’Essence ; le Raisonnement soutient analyse, conception et reproduction précise des procédés.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Alchimiste favorise Listes de base, Artisanat, connaissances magiques et techniques, Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Perception de la Puissance et compétences utiles à la création.</p>\n<h2>L’art de fixer la magie</h2>\n<p>Le cœur de la Profession consiste à transformer un effet magique fugitif en une œuvre durable : arme, armure, bijou, potion, bâton, baguette, rune, matériau ou objet utilitaire. La fabrication associe artisanat, connaissance magique et incantation.</p>\n<h2>Création et artisanat</h2>\n<p>Un Noldo peut associer forge, métaux et Essence ; un Alchimiste du Gondor créer objets de protection ou outils savants ; à Umbar, certains ateliers peuvent produire des objets destinés à la guerre, à l’intimidation ou à la domination. La qualité matérielle de l’objet reste importante.</p>\n<h2>Analyse des objets de pouvoir</h2>\n<p>L’Alchimiste peut rechercher fonction, nature de l’enchantement, royaume utilisé, conditions d’activation, procédés de fabrication et limitations d’un objet. Une arme trouvée dans les ruines d’Arnor, un artefact de Númenor ou une œuvre elfique exigent des connaissances différentes.</p>\n<h2>Potions et préparations</h2>\n<p>Liquides, poudres, huiles et substances préparées peuvent servir de support à la magie. Un Alchimiste dorwinadan peut associer son art aux vins, huiles et plantes locales ; un érudit du Gondor travailler à partir d’anciens textes númenóréens.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p>L’Alchimiste dispose traditionnellement d’un ensemble plus large de traditions et en choisit six comme Listes de base. Parmi elles : <strong>Enchantements d’Armures (Armour Enchantments)</strong>, <strong>Enchantements Généraux (General Enchantments)</strong>, <strong>Compétences de Matière Inorganique (Inorganic Skills)</strong>, <strong>Analyse des Objets (Item Analysis)</strong>, <strong>Maîtrise des Liquides et des Gaz (Liquid/Gas Skills)</strong>, <strong>Compétences de Matière Organique (Organic Skills)</strong>, <strong>Imprégnation d’un Autre Royaume (Other Realm Imbedding)</strong>, <strong>Imprégnation de son Propre Royaume (Own Realm Imbedding)</strong> et <strong>Enchantements d’Armes (Weapon Enchantments)</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Déclin des savoirs</h2>\n<p>Au Premier ou au Deuxième Âge, certains artisans peuvent disposer de maîtres, matériaux et ateliers devenus introuvables plus tard. Au Troisième Âge, connaître le principe d’un enchantement ne garantit pas la capacité de reproduire une œuvre ancienne.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis de l’Alchimiste RMU</strong> et son royaume d’Essence, avec <strong>Empathie et Raisonnement</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU développe fortement son identité de créateur d’objets de pouvoir et impose des limites de rareté, transmission, atelier et matériaux. La création magique devient un projet réel plutôt qu’une simple dépense mécanique.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Alchemist","system":{"description":"<h1>Alchemist</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Alchemist</strong> is a pure <strong>Essence</strong> spell-user specializing in the integration of magic with the material world. The Profession’s domain is the <strong>creation of objects, preparations, devices, and media capable of retaining or transmitting a magical effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Alchemist is already defined as a maker of magical items, potions, runes, staves, and wands. The Alchemist manipulates matter, enchants objects, and can embed spells from the character’s own realm or from other magical traditions.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Profession corresponds to learned artisans, rune-masters, makers of objects of power, and researchers able to combine knowledge of matter with mastery of Essence. A <strong>Noldo</strong> craftsman may develop very ancient enchanting techniques; a learned <strong>Dúnadan of Gondor</strong> may study objects inherited from Númenor; a master artisan of <strong>Umbar</strong> may preserve traditions derived from the Black Númenóreans.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Empathy</strong> is the realm stat of Essence and reflects sensitivity to the magical structures that permeate matter. <strong>Reasoning</strong> represents analysis, understanding of complex processes, the ability to design an object or preparation, and the precision required to reproduce a magical procedure.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist is a <strong>pure Essence spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, <strong>Craft</strong>, magical and technical lore, <strong>Power Manipulation</strong>, Power Point Development, Power Perception, sciences useful to creation, and analysis of objects of power.</p>\n<h2>The Art of Fixing Magic into Matter</h2>\n<p>The heart of the Profession is the ability to transform a fleeting magical effect into something enduring. An Alchemist may work upon a weapon, armour, jewel, potion, staff, wand, rune, raw material, or practical object.</p>\n<p>This approach is especially well suited to Middle-earth, where objects of power occupy a central place. An ancient blade, enchanted jewel, or rune-carved stone may survive for centuries and become the lasting testimony of a vanished art.</p>\n<h2>Creation and Craftsmanship</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist remains a craftsman as much as a spell-user. A Noldo may combine smithcraft, metalworking, and Essence to produce an exceptional blade. An Alchemist of Gondor may specialize in protective items or devices inspired by Númenórean traditions. In Umbar, an artisan tied to ancient Númenórean houses may create objects intended for war, intimidation, or domination.</p>\n<h2>Analysis of Objects of Power</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist can determine an object’s function, the nature of its enchantment, the realm used, its conditions of activation, the way an effect was embedded into matter, and any limitations or dangers.</p>\n<p>In a Third Age campaign, this ability is invaluable: an object found in the ruins of Arnor, a weapon recovered from an old tomb of Gondor, a relic of Númenor, or an Elven work may require very different bodies of knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Potions and Preparations</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist can create magical preparations in the form of liquids, powders, oils, or other prepared substances. In Middle-earth, these practices remain discreet: strengthening draughts, protective oils, travel preparations, or substances with unusual properties.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> Alchemist may combine the art with knowledge of the wines, oils, and plants of Dorwinion; a scholar of Gondor may work from preparations described in old Númenórean texts.</p>\n<h2>Spell Imbedding</h2>\n<p>One of the Alchemist’s major abilities is to embed a spell in an object. If the Alchemist knows the spell it may be supplied directly; otherwise another spell-user, a rune, or another magical source may provide the effect. This naturally encourages cooperation with Magicians, Animists, Lay Healers, and other traditions.</p>\n<h2>Objects of Power and Rarity</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, creating magical objects requires time, suitable materials, an appropriate workshop or tools, access to the necessary Lists, and often rare components. A blade forged from ore of the <strong>Blue Mountains</strong>, a stone cut according to a technique of <strong>Númenor</strong>, or an object made in an ancient Elven workshop may permit results impossible with ordinary materials.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Weapon Enchanter.</strong> Creation and improvement of weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Armour Enchanter.</strong> Protection and reinforcement of armour.</p>\n<p><strong>Potion Maker.</strong> Substances, preparations, and effects retained in liquid form.</p>\n<p><strong>Rune-master.</strong> Techniques that fix magical effects into signs and inscriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Artefact Scholar.</strong> Item analysis, magical lore, history, and craftsmanship.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>Traditionally, the Alchemist has access to an expanded set of professional Lists and chooses <strong>six</strong> as Base Spell Lists. The principal traditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Armour Enchantments</strong></li>\n<li><strong>General Enchantments</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Item Analysis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Organic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weapon Enchantments</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Decline of Lore</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist illustrates especially well the <strong>decline of magic through the Ages</strong>. In the First or Second Age, some craftsmen had access to lore, materials, and masters that are almost impossible to find in the Third Age. A late human Alchemist may understand the principle behind an ancient work yet remain unable to reproduce it for lack of materials, tools, or complementary knowledge.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>RMU retains the Alchemist as a pure spell-user specialized in the creation of magical objects. For MERP-RMU we use the <strong>Essence Alchemist chassis</strong>, with <strong>Empathy and Reasoning</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>We develop the Profession’s traditional identity as a maker of objects of power, with particular emphasis on enchantment, potions, analysis, and spell imbedding. The Middle-earth adaptation mainly adds limits of <strong>rarity, transmission, and availability of lore</strong>. Creation requires a tradition, workshop, materials, and time. The Alchemist therefore represents magic fixed into enduring works, bound to craft, materials, and knowledge whose secrets become rarer with each passing Age.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Essence"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Essence"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-essence"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP / RMSS — adaptation RMU","profession":"Alchemist","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Essence","description":"<h1>Alchemist</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Alchemist</strong> is a pure <strong>Essence</strong> spell-user specializing in the integration of magic with the material world. The Profession’s domain is the <strong>creation of objects, preparations, devices, and media capable of retaining or transmitting a magical effect</strong>.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Alchemist is already defined as a maker of magical items, potions, runes, staves, and wands. The Alchemist manipulates matter, enchants objects, and can embed spells from the character’s own realm or from other magical traditions.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Profession corresponds to learned artisans, rune-masters, makers of objects of power, and researchers able to combine knowledge of matter with mastery of Essence. A <strong>Noldo</strong> craftsman may develop very ancient enchanting techniques; a learned <strong>Dúnadan of Gondor</strong> may study objects inherited from Númenor; a master artisan of <strong>Umbar</strong> may preserve traditions derived from the Black Númenóreans.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Empathy</strong> is the realm stat of Essence and reflects sensitivity to the magical structures that permeate matter. <strong>Reasoning</strong> represents analysis, understanding of complex processes, the ability to design an object or preparation, and the precision required to reproduce a magical procedure.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist is a <strong>pure Essence spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, <strong>Craft</strong>, magical and technical lore, <strong>Power Manipulation</strong>, Power Point Development, Power Perception, sciences useful to creation, and analysis of objects of power.</p>\n<h2>The Art of Fixing Magic into Matter</h2>\n<p>The heart of the Profession is the ability to transform a fleeting magical effect into something enduring. An Alchemist may work upon a weapon, armour, jewel, potion, staff, wand, rune, raw material, or practical object.</p>\n<p>This approach is especially well suited to Middle-earth, where objects of power occupy a central place. An ancient blade, enchanted jewel, or rune-carved stone may survive for centuries and become the lasting testimony of a vanished art.</p>\n<h2>Creation and Craftsmanship</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist remains a craftsman as much as a spell-user. A Noldo may combine smithcraft, metalworking, and Essence to produce an exceptional blade. An Alchemist of Gondor may specialize in protective items or devices inspired by Númenórean traditions. In Umbar, an artisan tied to ancient Númenórean houses may create objects intended for war, intimidation, or domination.</p>\n<h2>Analysis of Objects of Power</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist can determine an object’s function, the nature of its enchantment, the realm used, its conditions of activation, the way an effect was embedded into matter, and any limitations or dangers.</p>\n<p>In a Third Age campaign, this ability is invaluable: an object found in the ruins of Arnor, a weapon recovered from an old tomb of Gondor, a relic of Númenor, or an Elven work may require very different bodies of knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Potions and Preparations</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist can create magical preparations in the form of liquids, powders, oils, or other prepared substances. In Middle-earth, these practices remain discreet: strengthening draughts, protective oils, travel preparations, or substances with unusual properties.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> Alchemist may combine the art with knowledge of the wines, oils, and plants of Dorwinion; a scholar of Gondor may work from preparations described in old Númenórean texts.</p>\n<h2>Spell Imbedding</h2>\n<p>One of the Alchemist’s major abilities is to embed a spell in an object. If the Alchemist knows the spell it may be supplied directly; otherwise another spell-user, a rune, or another magical source may provide the effect. This naturally encourages cooperation with Magicians, Animists, Lay Healers, and other traditions.</p>\n<h2>Objects of Power and Rarity</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, creating magical objects requires time, suitable materials, an appropriate workshop or tools, access to the necessary Lists, and often rare components. A blade forged from ore of the <strong>Blue Mountains</strong>, a stone cut according to a technique of <strong>Númenor</strong>, or an object made in an ancient Elven workshop may permit results impossible with ordinary materials.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Weapon Enchanter.</strong> Creation and improvement of weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Armour Enchanter.</strong> Protection and reinforcement of armour.</p>\n<p><strong>Potion Maker.</strong> Substances, preparations, and effects retained in liquid form.</p>\n<p><strong>Rune-master.</strong> Techniques that fix magical effects into signs and inscriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Artefact Scholar.</strong> Item analysis, magical lore, history, and craftsmanship.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>Traditionally, the Alchemist has access to an expanded set of professional Lists and chooses <strong>six</strong> as Base Spell Lists. The principal traditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Armour Enchantments</strong></li>\n<li><strong>General Enchantments</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Item Analysis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Organic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weapon Enchantments</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Decline of Lore</h2>\n<p>The Alchemist illustrates especially well the <strong>decline of magic through the Ages</strong>. In the First or Second Age, some craftsmen had access to lore, materials, and masters that are almost impossible to find in the Third Age. A late human Alchemist may understand the principle behind an ancient work yet remain unable to reproduce it for lack of materials, tools, or complementary knowledge.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>RMU retains the Alchemist as a pure spell-user specialized in the creation of magical objects. For MERP-RMU we use the <strong>Essence Alchemist chassis</strong>, with <strong>Empathy and Reasoning</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>We develop the Profession’s traditional identity as a maker of objects of power, with particular emphasis on enchantment, potions, analysis, and spell imbedding. The Middle-earth adaptation mainly adds limits of <strong>rarity, transmission, and availability of lore</strong>. Creation requires a tradition, workshop, materials, and time. The Alchemist therefore represents magic fixed into enduring works, bound to craft, materials, and knowledge whose secrets become rarer with each passing Age.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"2/3"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Attunement"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Runes"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Spell Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Materials Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","skillName":"Grace"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Metalcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Stonecraft"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Engineering"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Locks"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Crafting"}]},"_id":"ShFrQz1clFbvww21"}
{"name":"Animist","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"animist","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Animist; RMU Core Law — Druid mechanical profile"},"compendiumSourceKey":"animist","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Animiste","system":{"description":"<h1>Animiste</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Animiste</strong> est un utilisateur pur de la <strong>Théurgie</strong> spécialisé dans les forces qui animent le monde vivant. Sa magie porte sur les animaux, les plantes, les herbes, les phénomènes naturels, les sens et la protection offerte par l’environnement.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, le terme Animiste désigne une famille de traditions. Chez les Hommes des Bois du Rhovanion, les femmes exerçant cette fonction sont appelées <strong>Wita</strong> ; chez certains Hommes du Nord et Gramuz, elles peuvent être connues comme <strong>Urdharkona</strong>. Chaque peuple donne à cette magie une expression propre.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales de l’Animiste sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong>. L’Intuition est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Mémoire porte le savoir accumulé sur plantes, animaux, saisons, maladies et remèdes.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Animiste favorise Listes de base, Animaux, Environnement, Herboristerie, Premiers soins, Perception, connaissances générales et magiques, Développement de la Puissance et Manipulation de la Puissance. Ses compétences ordinaires restent importantes même lorsque la magie n’est pas utilisée.</p>\n<h2>Une magie du monde vivant</h2>\n<p>La magie permet d’apaiser les animaux, trouver et renforcer les herbes, lire les informations du terrain, améliorer les sens, résister aux dangers naturels et stimuler les plantes. Elle peut rester très discrète et se confondre avec une connaissance exceptionnelle de la nature.</p>\n<h2>Animaux et herbes</h2>\n<p>Chez les Hommes des Bois, cette aptitude concerne les créatures de la Forêt Noire ; chez les Béornides, elle s’inscrit dans une tradition respectueuse des animaux des vallées de l’Anduin ; chez les Lossoth, elle porte sur les espèces adaptées aux régions glacées. Les herbes complètent naturellement cette relation au vivant.</p>\n<h2>Variantes culturelles</h2>\n<p>Les <strong>Wita</strong> des Hommes des Bois sont chamans, voyantes, guérisseuses et guides spirituelles, avec une tradition chantée ou psalmodiée. Les <strong>Urdharkona</strong> des Hommes du Nord urbanisés et des Gramuz peuvent être davantage liées à l’élevage, aux saisons et aux communautés agricoles. Certaines traditions particulières remplacent ou ajoutent des Listes selon leur Culture.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Animaux (Animal Mastery).</strong> Apaise, approche, comprend et influence les animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Herbes (Herb Mastery).</strong> Trouve, prépare et renforce les herbes et remèdes naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance de la Nature (Nature’s Lore).</strong> Tire des informations de l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Mouvement et Sens de la Nature (Nature’s Movement/Senses).</strong> Améliore capacités physiques et sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection de la Nature (Nature’s Protection).</strong> Protège contre les dangers naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Plantes (Plant Mastery).</strong> Stimule, restaure et influence les végétaux.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>L’Animiste s’intègre naturellement aux communautés vivant en relation étroite avec leur environnement : Hommes des Bois, Béornides, Lossoth et autres peuples ruraux ou forestiers. Dans les grandes cités du Gondor, il sera plus rare et pourra apparaître comme herboriste ou guérisseur spécialisé.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons l’Animiste comme <strong>pur utilisateur de Théurgie</strong> et rétablissons <strong>Intuition et Mémoire</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales. Les six Listes historiques restent la référence de la Profession.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU développe les variantes culturelles et lie l’accès aux Listes à la transmission réelle du savoir et au déclin de la magie selon les Âges.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Animist","system":{"description":"<h1>Animist</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Animist</strong> is a pure <strong>Channeling</strong> spell-user specializing in the forces that animate the living world. The Profession’s magic concerns animals, plants, herbs, natural phenomena, the senses, and the protection offered by the environment. The Animist’s relationship with nature is founded on knowledge, observation, and harmony with living things.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Animist already occupied this role as a specialist in the living world. In Middle-earth, the term Animist is a broad category: among the <strong>Woodmen of Rhovanion</strong>, women who fulfil this role are called <strong>Wita</strong> and also serve as seers, healers, and spiritual guides; among some <strong>Urban Northmen</strong> and the <strong>Gramuz</strong>, Animists are known as <strong>Urdharkona</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Animist’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Memory (Me)</strong>. Intuition is also the realm stat of Channeling. Memory represents the breadth of accumulated knowledge concerning plants, animals, seasons, diseases, remedies, and traditions.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Animist is a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, skills related to <strong>Animals</strong> and the <strong>Environment</strong>, flora and fauna, Herb Lore, First Aid, Perception, general and magical lore, Power Point Development, and Power Manipulation.</p>\n<h2>Magic of the Living World</h2>\n<p>The Animist’s magic can calm or influence animals, find and prepare herbs, draw information from the environment, improve senses and physical abilities, resist natural hazards, and stimulate or restore plants.</p>\n<p>This magic often appears subtle. An Animist may walk through a forest and sense that a large group passed recently, recognize the plant capable of treating a wound, calm a frightened horse, or help a damaged tree recover its vigour.</p>\n<h2>Animals</h2>\n<p>Among the <strong>Woodmen</strong>, this ability may concern deer, birds, and the creatures of Mirkwood. Among the <strong>Beornings</strong>, it belongs to a tradition of particular respect for the animals of the Anduin vales. Among the <strong>Lossoth</strong>, an Animist may possess deep knowledge of creatures adapted to frozen lands.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Herb Mastery</strong> allows the Animist to find and prepare herbs and improve the effectiveness of natural remedies. A <strong>Wita</strong> of the Woodmen uses the plants of Mirkwood and the Anduin vales; a <strong>Dúnadan</strong> Animist may know remedies inherited from Númenórean traditions; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> Animist may combine this knowledge with the oils and cultivated plants of Dorwinion.</p>\n<h2>Knowledge of the Environment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong> draws information from terrain, plants, animals, and natural phenomena. In Mirkwood, it can reveal areas where vegetation or animals react to the influence of the Shadow; in Eriador, it can identify traces left by travellers or predators.</p>\n<h2>Protection, Movement, and Senses</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong> improves resistance to natural hazards. <strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong> enhances physical and perceptive abilities. Together these traditions reinforce the Animist’s identity as a character able to thrive in difficult environments through a combination of knowledge and magic.</p>\n<h2>Cultural Variants</h2>\n<h3>Wita of the Woodmen</h3>\n<p>The Wita are women regarded as “knowers.” They fulfil the roles of shaman, seer, healer, and spiritual guide. Their spells are traditionally sung or chanted.</p>\n<h3>Urdharkona</h3>\n<p>Among the Urban Northmen and the Gramuz, an Animist may bear the title <strong>Urdharkona</strong>. The tradition may be more closely structured around farming communities, livestock, the seasons, and protection of cultivated land.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>Combat is secondary. A Wita of the Woodmen may carry a staff, dagger, or bow; a Beorning Animist a spear or axe; a Dúnadan Animist may have received sword training. Light armour is generally the most compatible with the Profession’s way of life and magic.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Forest Guardian.</strong> Animals, plants, Perception, and natural protection.</p>\n<p><strong>Herbalist.</strong> Herb Mastery, plant lore, and healing.</p>\n<p><strong>Animal Master.</strong> Animal Mastery, Riding, and knowledge of fauna.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Adviser.</strong> Natural magic, Influence, religious traditions, and cultural lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilderness Traveller.</strong> Movement, senses, Survival, and environmental skills.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Animist has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Animal Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plant Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the fundamental concept of the Animist as a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user specializing in the living world</strong>. The Prime Stats remain <strong>Intuition and Memory</strong>, with Intuition also serving as the realm stat. The six traditional Lists remain the Profession’s reference.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation also develops cultural variants such as the <strong>Wita</strong>, <strong>Urdharkona</strong>, and other local traditions. The Animist’s place depends directly on the decline of magic and the transmission of lore. The more modest abilities may appear simply as extraordinary healing skill or knowledge of nature, while greater manifestations remain rare signs of an ancient tradition.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Théurgie"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU","profession":"Animist","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling","description":"<h1>Animist</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Animist</strong> is a pure <strong>Channeling</strong> spell-user specializing in the forces that animate the living world. The Profession’s magic concerns animals, plants, herbs, natural phenomena, the senses, and the protection offered by the environment. The Animist’s relationship with nature is founded on knowledge, observation, and harmony with living things.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Animist already occupied this role as a specialist in the living world. In Middle-earth, the term Animist is a broad category: among the <strong>Woodmen of Rhovanion</strong>, women who fulfil this role are called <strong>Wita</strong> and also serve as seers, healers, and spiritual guides; among some <strong>Urban Northmen</strong> and the <strong>Gramuz</strong>, Animists are known as <strong>Urdharkona</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Animist’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Memory (Me)</strong>. Intuition is also the realm stat of Channeling. Memory represents the breadth of accumulated knowledge concerning plants, animals, seasons, diseases, remedies, and traditions.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Animist is a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, skills related to <strong>Animals</strong> and the <strong>Environment</strong>, flora and fauna, Herb Lore, First Aid, Perception, general and magical lore, Power Point Development, and Power Manipulation.</p>\n<h2>Magic of the Living World</h2>\n<p>The Animist’s magic can calm or influence animals, find and prepare herbs, draw information from the environment, improve senses and physical abilities, resist natural hazards, and stimulate or restore plants.</p>\n<p>This magic often appears subtle. An Animist may walk through a forest and sense that a large group passed recently, recognize the plant capable of treating a wound, calm a frightened horse, or help a damaged tree recover its vigour.</p>\n<h2>Animals</h2>\n<p>Among the <strong>Woodmen</strong>, this ability may concern deer, birds, and the creatures of Mirkwood. Among the <strong>Beornings</strong>, it belongs to a tradition of particular respect for the animals of the Anduin vales. Among the <strong>Lossoth</strong>, an Animist may possess deep knowledge of creatures adapted to frozen lands.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Herb Mastery</strong> allows the Animist to find and prepare herbs and improve the effectiveness of natural remedies. A <strong>Wita</strong> of the Woodmen uses the plants of Mirkwood and the Anduin vales; a <strong>Dúnadan</strong> Animist may know remedies inherited from Númenórean traditions; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> Animist may combine this knowledge with the oils and cultivated plants of Dorwinion.</p>\n<h2>Knowledge of the Environment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong> draws information from terrain, plants, animals, and natural phenomena. In Mirkwood, it can reveal areas where vegetation or animals react to the influence of the Shadow; in Eriador, it can identify traces left by travellers or predators.</p>\n<h2>Protection, Movement, and Senses</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong> improves resistance to natural hazards. <strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong> enhances physical and perceptive abilities. Together these traditions reinforce the Animist’s identity as a character able to thrive in difficult environments through a combination of knowledge and magic.</p>\n<h2>Cultural Variants</h2>\n<h3>Wita of the Woodmen</h3>\n<p>The Wita are women regarded as “knowers.” They fulfil the roles of shaman, seer, healer, and spiritual guide. Their spells are traditionally sung or chanted.</p>\n<h3>Urdharkona</h3>\n<p>Among the Urban Northmen and the Gramuz, an Animist may bear the title <strong>Urdharkona</strong>. The tradition may be more closely structured around farming communities, livestock, the seasons, and protection of cultivated land.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>Combat is secondary. A Wita of the Woodmen may carry a staff, dagger, or bow; a Beorning Animist a spear or axe; a Dúnadan Animist may have received sword training. Light armour is generally the most compatible with the Profession’s way of life and magic.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Forest Guardian.</strong> Animals, plants, Perception, and natural protection.</p>\n<p><strong>Herbalist.</strong> Herb Mastery, plant lore, and healing.</p>\n<p><strong>Animal Master.</strong> Animal Mastery, Riding, and knowledge of fauna.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Adviser.</strong> Natural magic, Influence, religious traditions, and cultural lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilderness Traveller.</strong> Movement, senses, Survival, and environmental skills.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Animist has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Animal Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plant Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the fundamental concept of the Animist as a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user specializing in the living world</strong>. The Prime Stats remain <strong>Intuition and Memory</strong>, with Intuition also serving as the realm stat. The six traditional Lists remain the Profession’s reference.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation also develops cultural variants such as the <strong>Wita</strong>, <strong>Urdharkona</strong>, and other local traditions. The Animist’s place depends directly on the decline of magic and the transmission of lore. The more modest abilities may appear simply as extraordinary healing skill or knowledge of nature, while greater manifestations remain rare signs of an ancient tradition.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"2/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"3/4"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Woodcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Navigation"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Creature Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","skillName":"Grace"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Concealment"}]},"_id":"I8zeXj7LUv6E43Rp"}
{"name":"Astrologer","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"astrologer","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Astrologer p.153; Mentalism Companion pp.127-132"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"astrologer","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Sorcerer","document":{"name":"Astrologue","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"astrologer","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Astrologer p.153; Mentalism Companion pp.127-132"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / Mentalism Companion","profession":"Astrologer","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Astrologue</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Astrologue</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>Théurgie et de Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans l’observation, l’interprétation et la transmission de l’information. Son art associe étude des astres, perceptions mentales, communion, présages et communication à distance.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être savant-magicien, interprète des signes célestes ou conseiller. Un Dúnadan du Gondor peut associer astronomie et traditions númenóréennes ; un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar utiliser les mêmes connaissances pour navigation, stratégie ou politique ; un Dorwinadan observer les cycles saisonniers et routes orientales.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Intuition est la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Présence celle du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient l’observation, l’interprétation et la concentration.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue est un <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Vigilance, Astronomie, connaissances, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Autocontrôle, Communication et Influence.</p>\n<h2>Observer le ciel et interpréter les présages</h2>\n<p>Le ciel fournit configurations, cycles et phénomènes à interpréter. Une pratique astrologique donne tendances, avertissements et moments favorables plutôt que certitudes absolues. Au Gondor, elle peut servir un conseiller ; à Umbar, un capitaine ou une maison marchande ; dans le Rhovanion, un clan ou une communauté.</p>\n<h2>La lumière des étoiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> manipule la lumière et fournit certains effets offensifs. Dans la Terre du Milieu, la lumière stellaire possède une résonance particulière, surtout chez les Elfes et les Dúnedain.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voix Lointaine (Far Voice).</strong> Communication mentale et projection de la voix.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Sacrée (Holy Vision).</strong> Connaissance obtenue par rêves et communion spirituelle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights).</strong> Manipule la lumière et certaines attaques lumineuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Étoiles (Starlore).</strong> Construction et interprétation des horoscopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Perception Stellaire (Starsense).</strong> Détections mentales et modifications sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Voix (Way of the Voice).</strong> Communication, influence et certaines formes de contrôle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risque et observation de l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>Chercher des informations sur Dol Guldur, un serviteur du Roi-Sorcier ou Sauron peut exposer l’Astrologue. Communications et détections puissantes sont concernées par le <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue a été reconstruit comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline, Intuition et Présence</strong>. Ses six Listes historiques restent la référence.</p>\n<p>Cinq traditions s’appuient sur des mécaniques RMU existantes ; <strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> a nécessité une intégration spécifique MERP-RMU. Les anciennes entrées erronées Startlight/Startlights sont exclues.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Astrologue","system":{"description":"<h1>Astrologue</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Astrologue</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>Théurgie et de Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans l’observation, l’interprétation et la transmission de l’information. Son art associe étude des astres, perceptions mentales, communion, présages et communication à distance.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être savant-magicien, interprète des signes célestes ou conseiller. Un Dúnadan du Gondor peut associer astronomie et traditions númenóréennes ; un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar utiliser les mêmes connaissances pour navigation, stratégie ou politique ; un Dorwinadan observer les cycles saisonniers et routes orientales.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Intuition est la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Présence celle du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient l’observation, l’interprétation et la concentration.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue est un <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Vigilance, Astronomie, connaissances, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Autocontrôle, Communication et Influence.</p>\n<h2>Observer le ciel et interpréter les présages</h2>\n<p>Le ciel fournit configurations, cycles et phénomènes à interpréter. Une pratique astrologique donne tendances, avertissements et moments favorables plutôt que certitudes absolues. Au Gondor, elle peut servir un conseiller ; à Umbar, un capitaine ou une maison marchande ; dans le Rhovanion, un clan ou une communauté.</p>\n<h2>La lumière des étoiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> manipule la lumière et fournit certains effets offensifs. Dans la Terre du Milieu, la lumière stellaire possède une résonance particulière, surtout chez les Elfes et les Dúnedain.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voix Lointaine (Far Voice).</strong> Communication mentale et projection de la voix.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Sacrée (Holy Vision).</strong> Connaissance obtenue par rêves et communion spirituelle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights).</strong> Manipule la lumière et certaines attaques lumineuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Étoiles (Starlore).</strong> Construction et interprétation des horoscopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Perception Stellaire (Starsense).</strong> Détections mentales et modifications sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Voix (Way of the Voice).</strong> Communication, influence et certaines formes de contrôle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risque et observation de l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>Chercher des informations sur Dol Guldur, un serviteur du Roi-Sorcier ou Sauron peut exposer l’Astrologue. Communications et détections puissantes sont concernées par le <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue a été reconstruit comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline, Intuition et Présence</strong>. Ses six Listes historiques restent la référence.</p>\n<p>Cinq traditions s’appuient sur des mécaniques RMU existantes ; <strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> a nécessité une intégration spécifique MERP-RMU. Les anciennes entrées erronées Startlight/Startlights sont exclues.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Astrologer","system":{"description":"<h1>Astrologer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Astrologer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in observation, interpretation, and the transmission of information. The Profession combines study of the heavens, mental perception, communion, omens, and communication at a distance. The movements of the sky are used to recognize favourable configurations, interpret events, and reinforce certain magical practices.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Astrologer may be a learned magician, an interpreter of celestial signs, or an adviser versed in omens. A <strong>Dúnadan scholar of Gondor</strong> may combine astronomical observation with Númenórean traditions; a <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may use the same knowledge to guide an expedition; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> scholar may watch seasonal cycles and eastern routes; among the <strong>Elves</strong>, a related tradition may take a more contemplative form.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling, Presence that of Mentalism, and Self Discipline represents the patience and concentration required for observation and interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, <strong>Awareness</strong>, general and magical lore, Astronomy, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Self Control, Communication, and selected Influence skills.</p>\n<h2>Observing the Heavens</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer studies the movements of stars, constellations, celestial cycles, and unusual phenomena. An Astrologer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may maintain precise tables established in Minas Tirith or Dol Amroth; a Dorwinadan scholar may compare observations made along the roads of Rhovanion; in <strong>Umbar</strong>, astronomy also has immediate practical value for sea navigation.</p>\n<h2>Astrology and Omens</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlore</strong> helps construct and interpret horoscopes. A configuration may indicate a dangerous period, an auspicious moment, an important encounter, an approaching threat, or a coming change. Results take the form of tendencies and warnings rather than absolute certainty.</p>\n<h2>Starlight</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlights</strong> manipulates light and provides certain offensive effects. In Middle-earth, starlight has strong symbolic resonance. Among the Elves, the association may carry a spiritual dimension; among the Dúnedain, a scholarly one; for a practitioner of Umbar, a more technical and pragmatic interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Perception and Communication</h2>\n<p><strong>Starsense</strong> develops mental and sensory detection. <strong>Far Voice</strong> provides mental communication and projection of the voice. <strong>Way of the Voice</strong> gathers techniques of communication, influence, and certain forms of control.</p>\n<h2>Communion and Dreams</h2>\n<p><strong>Holy Vision</strong> obtains knowledge through communion and dreams. In Middle-earth, this List often appears as a lucid dream, symbolic image, omen, or unusual feeling of certainty. A Dúnadan Astrologer may interpret such dreams within traditions inherited from the Faithful of Númenor; an Elf may understand them as a form of harmony with the Powers of Arda.</p>\n<h2>Astrologer and Seer</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> explores past, present, and future directly through Mentalism. The <strong>Astrologer</strong> builds practice around the heavens, omens, communion, and communication, using hybrid magic. The former excels at psychic vision; the latter combines astrology, detection, dreams, light, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Court Astrologer.</strong> Starlore, Influence, Communication, and political knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Celestial Observer.</strong> Astronomy, sciences, Perception, and Starlights.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpreter of Dreams.</strong> Holy Vision, spiritual lore, and analysis of omens.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Messenger.</strong> Far Voice and Way of the Voice.</p>\n<p><strong>Military Adviser.</strong> Astrology, detection, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Voice</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Holy Vision</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlights</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starsense</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Way of the Voice</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Observation of the Shadow</h2>\n<p>Watching signs associated with Dol Guldur, mentally seeking the activities of a servant of the Witch-king, or attempting a vision linked to Sauron exposes the Astrologer to more than simple misinterpretation. Communication and detection can themselves become perceptible. Such uses remain subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is one of the Professions that had to be <strong>reconstructed more extensively for RMU</strong>. We preserve its historical identity as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>, its three Prime Stats, and its six traditional Lists.</p>\n<p>For the Foundry implementation, five of these traditions use Lists already available in the RMU ecosystem. <strong>Starlights</strong> required a specific MERP-RMU integration. We also preserve the dependence of certain spells upon actual starlight, a defining feature of the Profession.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"astrologer","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Astrologue","system":{"description":"<h1>Astrologue</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Astrologue</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>Théurgie et de Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans l’observation, l’interprétation et la transmission de l’information. Son art associe étude des astres, perceptions mentales, communion, présages et communication à distance.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être savant-magicien, interprète des signes célestes ou conseiller. Un Dúnadan du Gondor peut associer astronomie et traditions númenóréennes ; un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar utiliser les mêmes connaissances pour navigation, stratégie ou politique ; un Dorwinadan observer les cycles saisonniers et routes orientales.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Intuition est la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Présence celle du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient l’observation, l’interprétation et la concentration.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue est un <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Vigilance, Astronomie, connaissances, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Autocontrôle, Communication et Influence.</p>\n<h2>Observer le ciel et interpréter les présages</h2>\n<p>Le ciel fournit configurations, cycles et phénomènes à interpréter. Une pratique astrologique donne tendances, avertissements et moments favorables plutôt que certitudes absolues. Au Gondor, elle peut servir un conseiller ; à Umbar, un capitaine ou une maison marchande ; dans le Rhovanion, un clan ou une communauté.</p>\n<h2>La lumière des étoiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> manipule la lumière et fournit certains effets offensifs. Dans la Terre du Milieu, la lumière stellaire possède une résonance particulière, surtout chez les Elfes et les Dúnedain.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voix Lointaine (Far Voice).</strong> Communication mentale et projection de la voix.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Sacrée (Holy Vision).</strong> Connaissance obtenue par rêves et communion spirituelle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights).</strong> Manipule la lumière et certaines attaques lumineuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Étoiles (Starlore).</strong> Construction et interprétation des horoscopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Perception Stellaire (Starsense).</strong> Détections mentales et modifications sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Voix (Way of the Voice).</strong> Communication, influence et certaines formes de contrôle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risque et observation de l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>Chercher des informations sur Dol Guldur, un serviteur du Roi-Sorcier ou Sauron peut exposer l’Astrologue. Communications et détections puissantes sont concernées par le <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>L’Astrologue a été reconstruit comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline, Intuition et Présence</strong>. Ses six Listes historiques restent la référence.</p>\n<p>Cinq traditions s’appuient sur des mécaniques RMU existantes ; <strong>Lumières Stellaires (Starlights)</strong> a nécessité une intégration spécifique MERP-RMU. Les anciennes entrées erronées Startlight/Startlights sont exclues.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Astrologer","system":{"description":"<h1>Astrologer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Astrologer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in observation, interpretation, and the transmission of information. The Profession combines study of the heavens, mental perception, communion, omens, and communication at a distance. The movements of the sky are used to recognize favourable configurations, interpret events, and reinforce certain magical practices.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Astrologer may be a learned magician, an interpreter of celestial signs, or an adviser versed in omens. A <strong>Dúnadan scholar of Gondor</strong> may combine astronomical observation with Númenórean traditions; a <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may use the same knowledge to guide an expedition; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> scholar may watch seasonal cycles and eastern routes; among the <strong>Elves</strong>, a related tradition may take a more contemplative form.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling, Presence that of Mentalism, and Self Discipline represents the patience and concentration required for observation and interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, <strong>Awareness</strong>, general and magical lore, Astronomy, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Self Control, Communication, and selected Influence skills.</p>\n<h2>Observing the Heavens</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer studies the movements of stars, constellations, celestial cycles, and unusual phenomena. An Astrologer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may maintain precise tables established in Minas Tirith or Dol Amroth; a Dorwinadan scholar may compare observations made along the roads of Rhovanion; in <strong>Umbar</strong>, astronomy also has immediate practical value for sea navigation.</p>\n<h2>Astrology and Omens</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlore</strong> helps construct and interpret horoscopes. A configuration may indicate a dangerous period, an auspicious moment, an important encounter, an approaching threat, or a coming change. Results take the form of tendencies and warnings rather than absolute certainty.</p>\n<h2>Starlight</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlights</strong> manipulates light and provides certain offensive effects. In Middle-earth, starlight has strong symbolic resonance. Among the Elves, the association may carry a spiritual dimension; among the Dúnedain, a scholarly one; for a practitioner of Umbar, a more technical and pragmatic interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Perception and Communication</h2>\n<p><strong>Starsense</strong> develops mental and sensory detection. <strong>Far Voice</strong> provides mental communication and projection of the voice. <strong>Way of the Voice</strong> gathers techniques of communication, influence, and certain forms of control.</p>\n<h2>Communion and Dreams</h2>\n<p><strong>Holy Vision</strong> obtains knowledge through communion and dreams. In Middle-earth, this List often appears as a lucid dream, symbolic image, omen, or unusual feeling of certainty. A Dúnadan Astrologer may interpret such dreams within traditions inherited from the Faithful of Númenor; an Elf may understand them as a form of harmony with the Powers of Arda.</p>\n<h2>Astrologer and Seer</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> explores past, present, and future directly through Mentalism. The <strong>Astrologer</strong> builds practice around the heavens, omens, communion, and communication, using hybrid magic. The former excels at psychic vision; the latter combines astrology, detection, dreams, light, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Court Astrologer.</strong> Starlore, Influence, Communication, and political knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Celestial Observer.</strong> Astronomy, sciences, Perception, and Starlights.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpreter of Dreams.</strong> Holy Vision, spiritual lore, and analysis of omens.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Messenger.</strong> Far Voice and Way of the Voice.</p>\n<p><strong>Military Adviser.</strong> Astrology, detection, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Voice</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Holy Vision</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlights</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starsense</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Way of the Voice</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Observation of the Shadow</h2>\n<p>Watching signs associated with Dol Guldur, mentally seeking the activities of a servant of the Witch-king, or attempting a vision linked to Sauron exposes the Astrologer to more than simple misinterpretation. Communication and detection can themselves become perceptible. Such uses remain subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is one of the Professions that had to be <strong>reconstructed more extensively for RMU</strong>. We preserve its historical identity as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>, its three Prime Stats, and its six traditional Lists.</p>\n<p>For the Foundry implementation, five of these traditions use Lists already available in the RMU ecosystem. <strong>Starlights</strong> required a specific MERP-RMU integration. We also preserve the dependence of certain spells upon actual starlight, a defining feature of the Profession.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Hybrides","Théurgie + Mentalisme"],"en":["Hybrid Spell-user Professions","Channeling + Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-hybrides","professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / Mentalism Companion","profession":"Astrologer","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Astrologer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Astrologer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in observation, interpretation, and the transmission of information. The Profession combines study of the heavens, mental perception, communion, omens, and communication at a distance. The movements of the sky are used to recognize favourable configurations, interpret events, and reinforce certain magical practices.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Astrologer may be a learned magician, an interpreter of celestial signs, or an adviser versed in omens. A <strong>Dúnadan scholar of Gondor</strong> may combine astronomical observation with Númenórean traditions; a <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may use the same knowledge to guide an expedition; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> scholar may watch seasonal cycles and eastern routes; among the <strong>Elves</strong>, a related tradition may take a more contemplative form.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling, Presence that of Mentalism, and Self Discipline represents the patience and concentration required for observation and interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, <strong>Awareness</strong>, general and magical lore, Astronomy, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Self Control, Communication, and selected Influence skills.</p>\n<h2>Observing the Heavens</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer studies the movements of stars, constellations, celestial cycles, and unusual phenomena. An Astrologer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may maintain precise tables established in Minas Tirith or Dol Amroth; a Dorwinadan scholar may compare observations made along the roads of Rhovanion; in <strong>Umbar</strong>, astronomy also has immediate practical value for sea navigation.</p>\n<h2>Astrology and Omens</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlore</strong> helps construct and interpret horoscopes. A configuration may indicate a dangerous period, an auspicious moment, an important encounter, an approaching threat, or a coming change. Results take the form of tendencies and warnings rather than absolute certainty.</p>\n<h2>Starlight</h2>\n<p><strong>Starlights</strong> manipulates light and provides certain offensive effects. In Middle-earth, starlight has strong symbolic resonance. Among the Elves, the association may carry a spiritual dimension; among the Dúnedain, a scholarly one; for a practitioner of Umbar, a more technical and pragmatic interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Perception and Communication</h2>\n<p><strong>Starsense</strong> develops mental and sensory detection. <strong>Far Voice</strong> provides mental communication and projection of the voice. <strong>Way of the Voice</strong> gathers techniques of communication, influence, and certain forms of control.</p>\n<h2>Communion and Dreams</h2>\n<p><strong>Holy Vision</strong> obtains knowledge through communion and dreams. In Middle-earth, this List often appears as a lucid dream, symbolic image, omen, or unusual feeling of certainty. A Dúnadan Astrologer may interpret such dreams within traditions inherited from the Faithful of Númenor; an Elf may understand them as a form of harmony with the Powers of Arda.</p>\n<h2>Astrologer and Seer</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> explores past, present, and future directly through Mentalism. The <strong>Astrologer</strong> builds practice around the heavens, omens, communion, and communication, using hybrid magic. The former excels at psychic vision; the latter combines astrology, detection, dreams, light, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Court Astrologer.</strong> Starlore, Influence, Communication, and political knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Celestial Observer.</strong> Astronomy, sciences, Perception, and Starlights.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpreter of Dreams.</strong> Holy Vision, spiritual lore, and analysis of omens.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Messenger.</strong> Far Voice and Way of the Voice.</p>\n<p><strong>Military Adviser.</strong> Astrology, detection, and communication.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Voice</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Holy Vision</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlights</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starlore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starsense</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Way of the Voice</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Observation of the Shadow</h2>\n<p>Watching signs associated with Dol Guldur, mentally seeking the activities of a servant of the Witch-king, or attempting a vision linked to Sauron exposes the Astrologer to more than simple misinterpretation. Communication and detection can themselves become perceptible. Such uses remain subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Astrologer is one of the Professions that had to be <strong>reconstructed more extensively for RMU</strong>. We preserve its historical identity as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Mentalism spell-user</strong>, its three Prime Stats, and its six traditional Lists.</p>\n<p>For the Foundry implementation, five of these traditions use Lists already available in the RMU ecosystem. <strong>Starlights</strong> required a specific MERP-RMU integration. We also preserve the dependence of certain spells upon actual starlight, a defining feature of the Profession.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"gK7MVK2bQNs2rhYZ"}
{"name":"Barbarian","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"barbarian","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Barbarian; RMU-compatible adaptation"},"compendiumSourceKey":"barbarian","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-non-magiques","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Barbare","system":{"description":"<h1>Barbare</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Barbare</strong> est un combattant des terres sauvages, façonné par une vie rude, un environnement exigeant et des sociétés où la survie dépend largement de la force, de l’endurance et de l’expérience pratique. Sa formation est plus étroite que celle du Guerrier mais peut atteindre une efficacité remarquable dans les domaines qu’il choisit de maîtriser.</p>\n<p>Le terme ne décrit pas un individu dépourvu de culture. Il représente un combattant issu d’une société peu urbanisée ou d’une tradition où la chasse, la guerre, le voyage et la résistance physique structurent la vie quotidienne. Un Variag de Khand, un Homme des Collines du Rhudaur, un Beffraen ou un guerrier d’une communauté nordique isolée peuvent tous suivre cette Profession avec des traditions très différentes.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales du Barbare sont <strong>Force (St)</strong> et <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong>. La Force traduit sa puissance physique ; la Constitution représente sa capacité à supporter fatigue, blessures, privations et conditions naturelles difficiles.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Barbare est un <strong>non-utilisateur de sorts</strong>. Il privilégie Développement corporel, Armes, Survie, activités de plein air, Discrétion, Perception et aptitudes physiques. Il excelle dans quelques domaines directement liés à son mode de vie et peut y atteindre une très grande efficacité.</p>\n<h2>Combat et équipement</h2>\n<p>Le Barbare privilégie généralement les armes de sa Culture : un Variag peut combattre à cheval avec arc, lance ou lame ; un Homme des Collines peut employer lance, hache ou arc ; un Beffraen peut favoriser des armes robustes adaptées à une vie rude et à des ressources plus limitées.</p>\n<p>Les protections légères ou moyennes s’accordent souvent mieux avec sa mobilité. Le recours aux armures lourdes dépend fortement de la Culture, des ressources disponibles et de la tradition martiale locale.</p>\n<h2>Vie dans les terres sauvages</h2>\n<p>Survie, recherche de nourriture, observation du climat, équitation, connaissance du terrain et endurance sont naturellement importants. Le Barbare survit grâce à l’expérience, à sa robustesse et aux traditions de sa communauté plutôt qu’à une spécialisation comparable à celle du Rôdeur.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Guerrier tribal.</strong> Arme de mêlée, Développement corporel et compétences nécessaires aux guerres de clan.</li>\n<li><strong>Chasseur.</strong> Armes à distance, Perception, Survie et connaissance des animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Cavalier nomade.</strong> Équitation, armes de jet ou de tir et grande mobilité.</li>\n<li><strong>Berserker.</strong> Puissance offensive et résistance aux blessures.</li>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur des terres sauvages.</strong> Discrétion, Perception, déplacement et connaissance du terrain.</li>\n<li><strong>Champion de clan.</strong> Spécialisation martiale représentant la communauté dans les affrontements importants.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p><strong>Aucune.</strong> La magie ne fait pas partie de sa formation professionnelle normale.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le socle martial et physique de RMU tout en réaffirmant l’identité traditionnelle du Barbare : <strong>Force et Constitution</strong>, robustesse élevée, spécialisation et forte relation à la Culture.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU insiste sur le fait que la Profession n’est pas un niveau de civilisation. Un Variag, un Homme des Collines, un Beffraen ou un guerrier d’une société nordique possède une histoire, des croyances et des usages précis qui donnent tout son sens au châssis mécanique.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Barbarian","system":{"description":"<h1>Barbarian</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Barbarian</strong> is a warrior of the wild lands, shaped by a harsh life, a demanding environment, and societies in which survival depends first on strength, endurance, and direct mastery of combat. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Barbarian is a non-spell-user: less broadly versatile than the Fighter in the range of martial techniques, but exceptionally effective in the areas he chooses to master.</p>\n<p>The term does not imply a lack of culture. It represents a warrior from a less urbanized society, or from a way of life in which hunting, warfare, movement through difficult country, and physical resilience lie at the heart of existence. A <strong>Variag of Khand</strong> may be a fierce and mobile horse-warrior; a <strong>Hillman</strong> may favour ambush, spear, and axe; a <strong>Beffraen</strong> may combine endurance, simple weapons, and intimate knowledge of a demanding environment.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian’s Prime Stats are <strong>Strength (St)</strong> and <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Strength</strong> reflects physical power: the ability to wield demanding weapons, wrestle, carry, push, or break. <strong>Constitution</strong> represents endurance and resistance to wounds, fatigue, privation, and harsh natural conditions.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. He has no professional Base Spell Lists, and magical development remains particularly costly.</p>\n<p>Development strongly favours <strong>Body Development</strong>, <strong>Weapons</strong>, outdoor activities, <strong>Survival</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, Perception, physical skills, and knowledge of terrain and animals. Mechanically, the Barbarian is defined less by breadth than the Fighter: he excels in a smaller number of areas directly linked to his way of life and can become extremely capable in them.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian generally favours direct combat and weapons suited to the traditions of his Culture. He may be a melee fighter, javelin thrower, archer, spearman, or two-handed warrior. The choice depends above all on his origin.</p>\n<p>Traditionally, Barbarians avoid heavy armour and prefer light or medium protection in order to preserve mobility and endurance. Among the <strong>Variags</strong>, this mobility naturally combines with riding; among the <strong>Hillmen</strong>, it suits fighting in broken terrain; among some <strong>Beffraens</strong>, it reflects warrior traditions adapted to limited resources and frequent travel.</p>\n<h2>Life in the Wild</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian is usually at home in a natural environment. Survival, foraging, weather sense, riding, and knowledge of the land are natural fields of expertise. He survives above all through practical experience, toughness, and the traditions of his community.</p>\n<h2>Physical Strength and Endurance</h2>\n<p>Physical resilience is one of the Profession’s defining traits. The Barbarian develops <strong>Body Development</strong> very efficiently and has a remarkable ability to endure wounds and fatigue. This toughness does not necessarily imply ponderousness: a Variag rider or hill-country hunter may remain highly mobile while possessing exceptional stamina.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Tribal Warrior.</strong> Focuses on a melee weapon, Body Development, and the abilities needed for clan warfare.</p>\n<p><strong>Hunter.</strong> Combines ranged weapons, Perception, Survival, and animal lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Nomad Rider.</strong> Favours Riding, thrown or missile weapons, and mobility; especially appropriate for the Variags of Khand.</p>\n<p><strong>Berserker.</strong> Seeks maximum offensive power and the ability to continue fighting despite wounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilderness Scout.</strong> Develops Stealth, Perception, movement, and environmental knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Clan Champion.</strong> Concentrates training on a particular weapon and becomes a representative warrior of the community.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Barbarian has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magical training remains possible in exceptional circumstances, but it is always costly and foreign to the Profession’s normal training.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a warrior specialized in physical and martial disciplines while reinforcing the traditional identity of the <strong>MERP and Rolemaster Barbarian</strong>. Strength and Constitution remain the Prime Stats, and the Profession continues to represent an exceptionally robust fighter who is less broadly trained than the Fighter but particularly effective in the areas that match his way of life.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation also emphasizes the relationship with <strong>Culture</strong>. The Barbarian is not a universal personality type: fighting style, weapons, clothing, beliefs, and skills arise from the character’s people of origin. The distinction from the Fighter remains clear: the Fighter represents broad, structured martial training; the Barbarian relies more heavily on toughness, specialization, practical experience, and adaptation to a demanding environment.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions non magiques"],"en":["Non-magical Professions"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-non-magiques"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP / RMSS — adaptation RMU","profession":"Barbarian","spellCastingGroup":"Non","numberOfBaseLists":0,"realms":"","description":"<h1>Barbarian</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Barbarian</strong> is a warrior of the wild lands, shaped by a harsh life, a demanding environment, and societies in which survival depends first on strength, endurance, and direct mastery of combat. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Barbarian is a non-spell-user: less broadly versatile than the Fighter in the range of martial techniques, but exceptionally effective in the areas he chooses to master.</p>\n<p>The term does not imply a lack of culture. It represents a warrior from a less urbanized society, or from a way of life in which hunting, warfare, movement through difficult country, and physical resilience lie at the heart of existence. A <strong>Variag of Khand</strong> may be a fierce and mobile horse-warrior; a <strong>Hillman</strong> may favour ambush, spear, and axe; a <strong>Beffraen</strong> may combine endurance, simple weapons, and intimate knowledge of a demanding environment.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian’s Prime Stats are <strong>Strength (St)</strong> and <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Strength</strong> reflects physical power: the ability to wield demanding weapons, wrestle, carry, push, or break. <strong>Constitution</strong> represents endurance and resistance to wounds, fatigue, privation, and harsh natural conditions.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. He has no professional Base Spell Lists, and magical development remains particularly costly.</p>\n<p>Development strongly favours <strong>Body Development</strong>, <strong>Weapons</strong>, outdoor activities, <strong>Survival</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, Perception, physical skills, and knowledge of terrain and animals. Mechanically, the Barbarian is defined less by breadth than the Fighter: he excels in a smaller number of areas directly linked to his way of life and can become extremely capable in them.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian generally favours direct combat and weapons suited to the traditions of his Culture. He may be a melee fighter, javelin thrower, archer, spearman, or two-handed warrior. The choice depends above all on his origin.</p>\n<p>Traditionally, Barbarians avoid heavy armour and prefer light or medium protection in order to preserve mobility and endurance. Among the <strong>Variags</strong>, this mobility naturally combines with riding; among the <strong>Hillmen</strong>, it suits fighting in broken terrain; among some <strong>Beffraens</strong>, it reflects warrior traditions adapted to limited resources and frequent travel.</p>\n<h2>Life in the Wild</h2>\n<p>The Barbarian is usually at home in a natural environment. Survival, foraging, weather sense, riding, and knowledge of the land are natural fields of expertise. He survives above all through practical experience, toughness, and the traditions of his community.</p>\n<h2>Physical Strength and Endurance</h2>\n<p>Physical resilience is one of the Profession’s defining traits. The Barbarian develops <strong>Body Development</strong> very efficiently and has a remarkable ability to endure wounds and fatigue. This toughness does not necessarily imply ponderousness: a Variag rider or hill-country hunter may remain highly mobile while possessing exceptional stamina.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Tribal Warrior.</strong> Focuses on a melee weapon, Body Development, and the abilities needed for clan warfare.</p>\n<p><strong>Hunter.</strong> Combines ranged weapons, Perception, Survival, and animal lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Nomad Rider.</strong> Favours Riding, thrown or missile weapons, and mobility; especially appropriate for the Variags of Khand.</p>\n<p><strong>Berserker.</strong> Seeks maximum offensive power and the ability to continue fighting despite wounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilderness Scout.</strong> Develops Stealth, Perception, movement, and environmental knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Clan Champion.</strong> Concentrates training on a particular weapon and becomes a representative warrior of the community.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Barbarian has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magical training remains possible in exceptional circumstances, but it is always costly and foreign to the Profession’s normal training.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a warrior specialized in physical and martial disciplines while reinforcing the traditional identity of the <strong>MERP and Rolemaster Barbarian</strong>. Strength and Constitution remain the Prime Stats, and the Profession continues to represent an exceptionally robust fighter who is less broadly trained than the Fighter but particularly effective in the areas that match his way of life.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation also emphasizes the relationship with <strong>Culture</strong>. The Barbarian is not a universal personality type: fighting style, weapons, clothing, beliefs, and skills arise from the character’s people of origin. The distinction from the Fighter remains clear: the Fighter represents broad, structured martial training; the Barbarian relies more heavily on toughness, specialization, practical experience, and adaptation to a demanding environment.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"3/5"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"1/3"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"2/4"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"3/5"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"2/3"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Observation"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Fortitude"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Foraging"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Weather Watching"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Melee Weapons"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Unarmed"}]},"_id":"R0PmJzcbid5aS7B7"}
{"name":"Bard","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"bard","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"Compendium.rmu.core — Bard; RMU Spell Law 7.3"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"bard","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Bard","document":{"name":"Barde","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"bard","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"Compendium.rmu.core — Bard; RMU Spell Law 7.3"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Bard","spellCastingGroup":"Semi","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Essence","description":"<h1>Barde</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Barde</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique qui associe performance, savoir, influence sociale et magie à une formation suffisamment polyvalente pour voyager, enquêter et se défendre. Sa puissance agit à travers la parole, le chant, la musique, le récit et la résonance du son.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être ménestrel du Gondor, gardien de mémoire chez les Dúnedain, poète guerrier du Rohan, conteur dorwinadan ou chanteur hobbit. Chez les Elfes, musique, mémoire et pouvoir peuvent être encore plus intimement liés.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. La Mémoire soutient récits, langues, légendes et généalogies ; la Présence gouverne l’impact de la performance et la relation à l’auditoire.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Barde est un <strong>semi-utilisateur d’Essence</strong> dans RMU. Il favorise Arts de la Performance, Composition, Connaissances, Langues, Communication, Influence, Perception, certaines compétences martiales et ses Listes de base.</p>\n<h2>Performance et magie</h2>\n<p>Certaines Listes nécessitent une véritable Manœuvre de Performance après le lancement. Chant, instrument, récit, déclamation, danse ou théâtre peuvent servir de support. La qualité de la performance influence durée, portée ou résistance selon le sort.</p>\n<h2>Chants et récits dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Au Rohan, le Barde peut entretenir la mémoire des rois et des batailles ; chez les Dúnedain, transmettre l’histoire de Númenor et des royaumes perdus ; chez les Hobbits, utiliser chansons, poèmes et généalogies sous une forme beaucoup plus familière ; chez les Eldar, le chant peut atteindre une profondeur que les Hommes qualifieraient spontanément de magique.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chants de Contrôle (Controlling Songs).</strong> Influence un auditoire par une performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Arts du Divertissement (Entertaining Ways).</strong> Soutient et améliore les performances.</li>\n<li><strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs).</strong> Renforce et encourage les personnes qui assistent à la performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Objets (Item Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations sur les objets et leur nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Contrôle du Son (Sound Control).</strong> Manipule intensité et propriétés du son.</li>\n<li><strong>Projection du Son (Sound Projection).</strong> Étend, déplace ou renforce les sons.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Savoirs et évolution RMU</h2>\n<p>Les anciennes traditions possédaient <strong>Savoirs (Lores)</strong> comme Liste de base. RMU la remplace par <strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs)</strong>. MERP-RMU conserve cette évolution tout en préservant le rôle du Barde comme gardien de mémoire grâce à ses excellents coûts en Connaissances et à sa Mémoire primordiale.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Barde RMU</strong>, semi-magique d’Essence, et ses six Listes modernes. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Mémoire et Présence</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>La Profession reste profondément liée au langage, au souvenir et à l’art. Ses performances peuvent rester discrètes, ce qui s’accorde particulièrement bien avec les limites de la magie en Terre du Milieu.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Barde","system":{"description":"<h1>Barde</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Barde</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique qui associe performance, savoir, influence sociale et magie à une formation suffisamment polyvalente pour voyager, enquêter et se défendre. Sa puissance agit à travers la parole, le chant, la musique, le récit et la résonance du son.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être ménestrel du Gondor, gardien de mémoire chez les Dúnedain, poète guerrier du Rohan, conteur dorwinadan ou chanteur hobbit. Chez les Elfes, musique, mémoire et pouvoir peuvent être encore plus intimement liés.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. La Mémoire soutient récits, langues, légendes et généalogies ; la Présence gouverne l’impact de la performance et la relation à l’auditoire.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Barde est un <strong>semi-utilisateur d’Essence</strong> dans RMU. Il favorise Arts de la Performance, Composition, Connaissances, Langues, Communication, Influence, Perception, certaines compétences martiales et ses Listes de base.</p>\n<h2>Performance et magie</h2>\n<p>Certaines Listes nécessitent une véritable Manœuvre de Performance après le lancement. Chant, instrument, récit, déclamation, danse ou théâtre peuvent servir de support. La qualité de la performance influence durée, portée ou résistance selon le sort.</p>\n<h2>Chants et récits dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Au Rohan, le Barde peut entretenir la mémoire des rois et des batailles ; chez les Dúnedain, transmettre l’histoire de Númenor et des royaumes perdus ; chez les Hobbits, utiliser chansons, poèmes et généalogies sous une forme beaucoup plus familière ; chez les Eldar, le chant peut atteindre une profondeur que les Hommes qualifieraient spontanément de magique.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chants de Contrôle (Controlling Songs).</strong> Influence un auditoire par une performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Arts du Divertissement (Entertaining Ways).</strong> Soutient et améliore les performances.</li>\n<li><strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs).</strong> Renforce et encourage les personnes qui assistent à la performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Objets (Item Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations sur les objets et leur nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Contrôle du Son (Sound Control).</strong> Manipule intensité et propriétés du son.</li>\n<li><strong>Projection du Son (Sound Projection).</strong> Étend, déplace ou renforce les sons.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Savoirs et évolution RMU</h2>\n<p>Les anciennes traditions possédaient <strong>Savoirs (Lores)</strong> comme Liste de base. RMU la remplace par <strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs)</strong>. MERP-RMU conserve cette évolution tout en préservant le rôle du Barde comme gardien de mémoire grâce à ses excellents coûts en Connaissances et à sa Mémoire primordiale.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Barde RMU</strong>, semi-magique d’Essence, et ses six Listes modernes. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Mémoire et Présence</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>La Profession reste profondément liée au langage, au souvenir et à l’art. Ses performances peuvent rester discrètes, ce qui s’accorde particulièrement bien avec les limites de la magie en Terre du Milieu.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Bard","system":{"description":"<h1>Bard</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Bard</strong> is a semi-spell-user who combines <strong>performance, lore, social influence, and magic</strong> with enough broad training to travel, investigate, and defend himself. The Profession’s power lies in shaping emotion, conveying ideas, manipulating sound, uncovering knowledge, and strengthening others through art.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Bard traditionally combined the fields of <strong>Weapons</strong> and <strong>Mentalism</strong>. RMU has revised this concept by placing the Bard’s Base Spell Lists within <strong>Essence</strong>. We retain that RMU development while preserving the historical identity of the Profession.</p>\n<p>A <strong>minstrel of Gondor</strong> may be historian, musician, and messenger of a noble house; a <strong>Dúnadan of the North</strong> may preserve orally the tales of vanished kingdoms; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> may combine music, diplomacy, and knowledge of trade routes; a <strong>Hobbit</strong> may be storyteller, singer, or chronicler without ever thinking of himself as a practitioner of magic.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Bard’s Prime Stats are <strong>Memory (Me)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Memory represents the ability to retain tales, genealogies, songs, languages, and legends; Presence reflects charisma and the ability to hold an audience.</p>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard’s magical realm is <strong>Essence</strong>, whose realm stat is Empathy. Realm stat and Prime Stats therefore serve different functions here.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Bard is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Essence</strong>. Development favours <strong>Performance Arts</strong>, <strong>Lore</strong>, Languages, Influence, Perception, Composition, social skills, selected martial skills, Base Spell Lists, and Power Manipulation.</p>\n<h2>Performance and Magic</h2>\n<p>Bardic magic uses <strong>performance</strong> directly: song, instrumental music, storytelling, recitation, dance, juggling, theatre, or another mastered form of expression. <strong>Controlling Songs</strong> and <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong> require a genuine performance after the spell has been cast successfully. The quality of that performance influences duration and certain parameters of the effect.</p>\n<p>This mechanic ties the character’s artistic skill directly to magic.</p>\n<h2>Songs and Tales in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>Among the <strong>Sindar</strong> or <strong>Noldor</strong>, a Bard may preserve stories reaching back to the First Age and employ forms of song whose power seems almost natural. Among the <strong>Dúnedain</strong>, the role may be that of a keeper of memory. In <strong>Rohan</strong>, a Bard may be a court singer or poet charged with preserving the memory of kings and battles. Among the <strong>Hobbits</strong>, popular songs, family stories, and long genealogies may become the vehicle for gentle and almost invisible magic.</p>\n<h2>Influence and Emotion</h2>\n<p>A song may strengthen courage, calm a crowd, kindle enthusiasm, divert attention, or create a feeling of trust. A Bard of Rohan may support warriors with a song of their ancestors; a minstrel of Minas Tirith may soothe an assembly; in Umbar, a court performer may subtly influence a reception or negotiation.</p>\n<h2>Lore and Memory</h2>\n<p>The Bard gathers histories, legends, genealogies, old songs, languages, local traditions, and information concerning remarkable objects. RMU replaces the historical <strong>Lores</strong> List with <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong>, while preserving a strong orientation toward Lore skills and <strong>Item Lore</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Mastery of Sound</h2>\n<p><strong>Sound Control</strong> and <strong>Sound Projection</strong> can alter, move, strengthen, or exploit sound: project a voice, increase the reach of a song, mask a noise, or create a tactical acoustic effect.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Minstrel.</strong> Music, Singing, Composition, Influence, and magical Songs.</p>\n<p><strong>Storyteller.</strong> Narrative performance, historical lore, languages, and oral arts.</p>\n<p><strong>Skald or Warrior-poet.</strong> Weapons, heroic songs, and inspirational magic; especially suitable for Rohan.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Scholar.</strong> Lore, languages, Item Lore, and investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Diplomat.</strong> Influence, social awareness, languages, and emotional magic.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard has the following six modern Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controlling Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Entertaining Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inspiring Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Item Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Control</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Projection</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Magic and Discretion</h2>\n<p>A song or tale may appear perfectly ordinary to an observer. The supernatural manifestation may emerge only through the emotions of the audience or the unnatural reach of a voice. This magic fits especially well with our rule for <strong>Discreet Use of Magic</strong>, while still being subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong> when its scale and power become significant.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>Historically, the Bard is a semi-spell-user combining <strong>Weapons and Mentalism</strong>, with <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. RMU retains the semi-magical status and artistic identity but moves the Base Lists to <strong>Essence</strong>.</p>\n<p>For MERP-RMU, we retain <strong>the RMU chassis and its Essence realm</strong> in order to benefit from the new performance-based bardic rules, while explicitly restoring <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> and preserving the MERP identity of the Bard as keeper of lore, storyteller, traveller, and bearer of memory.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"bard","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-semi-essence","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Barde","system":{"description":"<h1>Barde</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Barde</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique qui associe performance, savoir, influence sociale et magie à une formation suffisamment polyvalente pour voyager, enquêter et se défendre. Sa puissance agit à travers la parole, le chant, la musique, le récit et la résonance du son.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, il peut être ménestrel du Gondor, gardien de mémoire chez les Dúnedain, poète guerrier du Rohan, conteur dorwinadan ou chanteur hobbit. Chez les Elfes, musique, mémoire et pouvoir peuvent être encore plus intimement liés.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. La Mémoire soutient récits, langues, légendes et généalogies ; la Présence gouverne l’impact de la performance et la relation à l’auditoire.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Barde est un <strong>semi-utilisateur d’Essence</strong> dans RMU. Il favorise Arts de la Performance, Composition, Connaissances, Langues, Communication, Influence, Perception, certaines compétences martiales et ses Listes de base.</p>\n<h2>Performance et magie</h2>\n<p>Certaines Listes nécessitent une véritable Manœuvre de Performance après le lancement. Chant, instrument, récit, déclamation, danse ou théâtre peuvent servir de support. La qualité de la performance influence durée, portée ou résistance selon le sort.</p>\n<h2>Chants et récits dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Au Rohan, le Barde peut entretenir la mémoire des rois et des batailles ; chez les Dúnedain, transmettre l’histoire de Númenor et des royaumes perdus ; chez les Hobbits, utiliser chansons, poèmes et généalogies sous une forme beaucoup plus familière ; chez les Eldar, le chant peut atteindre une profondeur que les Hommes qualifieraient spontanément de magique.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chants de Contrôle (Controlling Songs).</strong> Influence un auditoire par une performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Arts du Divertissement (Entertaining Ways).</strong> Soutient et améliore les performances.</li>\n<li><strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs).</strong> Renforce et encourage les personnes qui assistent à la performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance des Objets (Item Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations sur les objets et leur nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Contrôle du Son (Sound Control).</strong> Manipule intensité et propriétés du son.</li>\n<li><strong>Projection du Son (Sound Projection).</strong> Étend, déplace ou renforce les sons.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Savoirs et évolution RMU</h2>\n<p>Les anciennes traditions possédaient <strong>Savoirs (Lores)</strong> comme Liste de base. RMU la remplace par <strong>Chants d’Inspiration (Inspiring Songs)</strong>. MERP-RMU conserve cette évolution tout en préservant le rôle du Barde comme gardien de mémoire grâce à ses excellents coûts en Connaissances et à sa Mémoire primordiale.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Barde RMU</strong>, semi-magique d’Essence, et ses six Listes modernes. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Mémoire et Présence</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>La Profession reste profondément liée au langage, au souvenir et à l’art. Ses performances peuvent rester discrètes, ce qui s’accorde particulièrement bien avec les limites de la magie en Terre du Milieu.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Bard","system":{"description":"<h1>Bard</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Bard</strong> is a semi-spell-user who combines <strong>performance, lore, social influence, and magic</strong> with enough broad training to travel, investigate, and defend himself. The Profession’s power lies in shaping emotion, conveying ideas, manipulating sound, uncovering knowledge, and strengthening others through art.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Bard traditionally combined the fields of <strong>Weapons</strong> and <strong>Mentalism</strong>. RMU has revised this concept by placing the Bard’s Base Spell Lists within <strong>Essence</strong>. We retain that RMU development while preserving the historical identity of the Profession.</p>\n<p>A <strong>minstrel of Gondor</strong> may be historian, musician, and messenger of a noble house; a <strong>Dúnadan of the North</strong> may preserve orally the tales of vanished kingdoms; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> may combine music, diplomacy, and knowledge of trade routes; a <strong>Hobbit</strong> may be storyteller, singer, or chronicler without ever thinking of himself as a practitioner of magic.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Bard’s Prime Stats are <strong>Memory (Me)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Memory represents the ability to retain tales, genealogies, songs, languages, and legends; Presence reflects charisma and the ability to hold an audience.</p>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard’s magical realm is <strong>Essence</strong>, whose realm stat is Empathy. Realm stat and Prime Stats therefore serve different functions here.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Bard is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Essence</strong>. Development favours <strong>Performance Arts</strong>, <strong>Lore</strong>, Languages, Influence, Perception, Composition, social skills, selected martial skills, Base Spell Lists, and Power Manipulation.</p>\n<h2>Performance and Magic</h2>\n<p>Bardic magic uses <strong>performance</strong> directly: song, instrumental music, storytelling, recitation, dance, juggling, theatre, or another mastered form of expression. <strong>Controlling Songs</strong> and <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong> require a genuine performance after the spell has been cast successfully. The quality of that performance influences duration and certain parameters of the effect.</p>\n<p>This mechanic ties the character’s artistic skill directly to magic.</p>\n<h2>Songs and Tales in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>Among the <strong>Sindar</strong> or <strong>Noldor</strong>, a Bard may preserve stories reaching back to the First Age and employ forms of song whose power seems almost natural. Among the <strong>Dúnedain</strong>, the role may be that of a keeper of memory. In <strong>Rohan</strong>, a Bard may be a court singer or poet charged with preserving the memory of kings and battles. Among the <strong>Hobbits</strong>, popular songs, family stories, and long genealogies may become the vehicle for gentle and almost invisible magic.</p>\n<h2>Influence and Emotion</h2>\n<p>A song may strengthen courage, calm a crowd, kindle enthusiasm, divert attention, or create a feeling of trust. A Bard of Rohan may support warriors with a song of their ancestors; a minstrel of Minas Tirith may soothe an assembly; in Umbar, a court performer may subtly influence a reception or negotiation.</p>\n<h2>Lore and Memory</h2>\n<p>The Bard gathers histories, legends, genealogies, old songs, languages, local traditions, and information concerning remarkable objects. RMU replaces the historical <strong>Lores</strong> List with <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong>, while preserving a strong orientation toward Lore skills and <strong>Item Lore</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Mastery of Sound</h2>\n<p><strong>Sound Control</strong> and <strong>Sound Projection</strong> can alter, move, strengthen, or exploit sound: project a voice, increase the reach of a song, mask a noise, or create a tactical acoustic effect.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Minstrel.</strong> Music, Singing, Composition, Influence, and magical Songs.</p>\n<p><strong>Storyteller.</strong> Narrative performance, historical lore, languages, and oral arts.</p>\n<p><strong>Skald or Warrior-poet.</strong> Weapons, heroic songs, and inspirational magic; especially suitable for Rohan.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Scholar.</strong> Lore, languages, Item Lore, and investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Diplomat.</strong> Influence, social awareness, languages, and emotional magic.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard has the following six modern Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controlling Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Entertaining Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inspiring Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Item Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Control</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Projection</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Magic and Discretion</h2>\n<p>A song or tale may appear perfectly ordinary to an observer. The supernatural manifestation may emerge only through the emotions of the audience or the unnatural reach of a voice. This magic fits especially well with our rule for <strong>Discreet Use of Magic</strong>, while still being subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong> when its scale and power become significant.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>Historically, the Bard is a semi-spell-user combining <strong>Weapons and Mentalism</strong>, with <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. RMU retains the semi-magical status and artistic identity but moves the Base Lists to <strong>Essence</strong>.</p>\n<p>For MERP-RMU, we retain <strong>the RMU chassis and its Essence realm</strong> in order to benefit from the new performance-based bardic rules, while explicitly restoring <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> and preserving the MERP identity of the Bard as keeper of lore, storyteller, traveller, and bearer of memory.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Semi-Magiques","Essence"],"en":["Semi Spell-user Professions","Essence"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-semi-magiques","professions-semi-essence"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Bard","spellCastingGroup":"Semi","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Essence","description":"<h1>Bard</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Bard</strong> is a semi-spell-user who combines <strong>performance, lore, social influence, and magic</strong> with enough broad training to travel, investigate, and defend himself. The Profession’s power lies in shaping emotion, conveying ideas, manipulating sound, uncovering knowledge, and strengthening others through art.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Bard traditionally combined the fields of <strong>Weapons</strong> and <strong>Mentalism</strong>. RMU has revised this concept by placing the Bard’s Base Spell Lists within <strong>Essence</strong>. We retain that RMU development while preserving the historical identity of the Profession.</p>\n<p>A <strong>minstrel of Gondor</strong> may be historian, musician, and messenger of a noble house; a <strong>Dúnadan of the North</strong> may preserve orally the tales of vanished kingdoms; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> may combine music, diplomacy, and knowledge of trade routes; a <strong>Hobbit</strong> may be storyteller, singer, or chronicler without ever thinking of himself as a practitioner of magic.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Bard’s Prime Stats are <strong>Memory (Me)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Memory represents the ability to retain tales, genealogies, songs, languages, and legends; Presence reflects charisma and the ability to hold an audience.</p>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard’s magical realm is <strong>Essence</strong>, whose realm stat is Empathy. Realm stat and Prime Stats therefore serve different functions here.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Bard is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Essence</strong>. Development favours <strong>Performance Arts</strong>, <strong>Lore</strong>, Languages, Influence, Perception, Composition, social skills, selected martial skills, Base Spell Lists, and Power Manipulation.</p>\n<h2>Performance and Magic</h2>\n<p>Bardic magic uses <strong>performance</strong> directly: song, instrumental music, storytelling, recitation, dance, juggling, theatre, or another mastered form of expression. <strong>Controlling Songs</strong> and <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong> require a genuine performance after the spell has been cast successfully. The quality of that performance influences duration and certain parameters of the effect.</p>\n<p>This mechanic ties the character’s artistic skill directly to magic.</p>\n<h2>Songs and Tales in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>Among the <strong>Sindar</strong> or <strong>Noldor</strong>, a Bard may preserve stories reaching back to the First Age and employ forms of song whose power seems almost natural. Among the <strong>Dúnedain</strong>, the role may be that of a keeper of memory. In <strong>Rohan</strong>, a Bard may be a court singer or poet charged with preserving the memory of kings and battles. Among the <strong>Hobbits</strong>, popular songs, family stories, and long genealogies may become the vehicle for gentle and almost invisible magic.</p>\n<h2>Influence and Emotion</h2>\n<p>A song may strengthen courage, calm a crowd, kindle enthusiasm, divert attention, or create a feeling of trust. A Bard of Rohan may support warriors with a song of their ancestors; a minstrel of Minas Tirith may soothe an assembly; in Umbar, a court performer may subtly influence a reception or negotiation.</p>\n<h2>Lore and Memory</h2>\n<p>The Bard gathers histories, legends, genealogies, old songs, languages, local traditions, and information concerning remarkable objects. RMU replaces the historical <strong>Lores</strong> List with <strong>Inspiring Songs</strong>, while preserving a strong orientation toward Lore skills and <strong>Item Lore</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Mastery of Sound</h2>\n<p><strong>Sound Control</strong> and <strong>Sound Projection</strong> can alter, move, strengthen, or exploit sound: project a voice, increase the reach of a song, mask a noise, or create a tactical acoustic effect.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Minstrel.</strong> Music, Singing, Composition, Influence, and magical Songs.</p>\n<p><strong>Storyteller.</strong> Narrative performance, historical lore, languages, and oral arts.</p>\n<p><strong>Skald or Warrior-poet.</strong> Weapons, heroic songs, and inspirational magic; especially suitable for Rohan.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Scholar.</strong> Lore, languages, Item Lore, and investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Diplomat.</strong> Influence, social awareness, languages, and emotional magic.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In our RMU adaptation, the Bard has the following six modern Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controlling Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Entertaining Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inspiring Songs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Item Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Control</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sound Projection</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Magic and Discretion</h2>\n<p>A song or tale may appear perfectly ordinary to an observer. The supernatural manifestation may emerge only through the emotions of the audience or the unnatural reach of a voice. This magic fits especially well with our rule for <strong>Discreet Use of Magic</strong>, while still being subject to the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong> when its scale and power become significant.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>Historically, the Bard is a semi-spell-user combining <strong>Weapons and Mentalism</strong>, with <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. RMU retains the semi-magical status and artistic identity but moves the Base Lists to <strong>Essence</strong>.</p>\n<p>For MERP-RMU, we retain <strong>the RMU chassis and its Essence realm</strong> in order to benefit from the new performance-based bardic rules, while explicitly restoring <strong>Memory and Presence</strong> and preserving the MERP identity of the Bard as keeper of lore, storyteller, traveller, and bearer of memory.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"SCfSkXaByc7Dzrxq"}
{"name":"Drughan","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"drughan","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Druid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Drughân, p.151"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"drughan","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Ranger","document":{"name":"Drughân","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"drughan","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Druid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Drughân, p.151"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Drughân","spellCastingGroup":"Semi","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling","description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Drughân</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique de <strong>Théurgie</strong>, exclusivement issu de la culture <strong>Drughu</strong>. Il appartient aux Drughôr-ghan, les « Grands Frères », fraternité chamanique dont les membres deviennent guérisseurs, conseillers spirituels et gardiens des traditions.</p>\n<p>Chaque clan Drughu possède normalement un Drughân. Dans la Forêt de Drúadan, il peut être gardien des chemins secrets, connaisseur des plantes et protecteur d’un petit clan. Aux Âges anciens, la même Profession peut représenter une tradition héritée des premiers peuples humains de l’Ouest.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong>. L’Intuition est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Mémoire soutient la transmission orale des plantes, animaux, rites, lieux sacrés et traditions du peuple.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Animaux, Environnement, Connaissances, Vigilance, Perception, Développement corporel, Artisanat, Discrétion et Listes professionnelles.</p>\n<h2>Guérisseur et gardien du territoire</h2>\n<p>Sa guérison repose largement sur les herbes et la connaissance du monde naturel. Il lit l’environnement, interprète les signes, protège le clan et peut utiliser sa magie pour renforcer ses aptitudes ordinaires.</p>\n<h2>Les tatouages sacrés</h2>\n<p>Chaque Drughân reçoit un tatouage sacré lors de son passage à l’âge adulte, tracé selon les traditions Drughu. Les motifs peuvent accorder camouflage, robustesse, relation privilégiée avec une espèce animale, puissance accrue, ténacité ou changement de forme. Le corps devient lui-même support de l’enchantement.</p>\n<h2>Artisanat des tatouages</h2>\n<p>L’<strong>Artisanat des Tatouages (Tattoo Crafts)</strong> permet d’enchanter des tatouages et d’y intégrer des sorts de Théurgie. Ces traditions constituent l’une des expressions magiques les plus spécifiques aux Drughu.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Animaux (Animal Mastery).</strong> Apaise, approche et influence les animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Herbes (Herb Mastery).</strong> Trouve, prépare et renforce les herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance de la Nature (Nature’s Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations de l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Mouvement et Sens de la Nature (Nature’s Movement/Senses).</strong> Améliore capacités physiques et sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection de la Nature (Nature’s Protection).</strong> Protège contre les dangers naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Plantes (Plant Mastery).</strong> Fait croître, restaure et influence les végétaux.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Échange avec les Listes du Rôdeur</h2>\n<p>Une particularité traditionnelle permet au Drughân de remplacer <strong>deux</strong> de ses six Listes par deux Listes professionnelles du Rôdeur. Ce choix représente une orientation culturelle précise et ne donne pas un accès libre à l’ensemble des Listes du Rôdeur.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est construit sur un <strong>châssis semi-magique de Théurgie</strong> proche du Rôdeur, avec <strong>Intuition et Mémoire</strong>. Les six Listes naturelles et l’échange possible de deux Listes sont conservés.</p>\n<p>L’élément le plus spécifique à MERP reste le système des tatouages sacrés et de l’Artisanat des Tatouages, qui devra être représenté par des Talents et mécanismes adaptés.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Drughân","system":{"description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Drughân</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique de <strong>Théurgie</strong>, exclusivement issu de la culture <strong>Drughu</strong>. Il appartient aux Drughôr-ghan, les « Grands Frères », fraternité chamanique dont les membres deviennent guérisseurs, conseillers spirituels et gardiens des traditions.</p>\n<p>Chaque clan Drughu possède normalement un Drughân. Dans la Forêt de Drúadan, il peut être gardien des chemins secrets, connaisseur des plantes et protecteur d’un petit clan. Aux Âges anciens, la même Profession peut représenter une tradition héritée des premiers peuples humains de l’Ouest.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong>. L’Intuition est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Mémoire soutient la transmission orale des plantes, animaux, rites, lieux sacrés et traditions du peuple.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Animaux, Environnement, Connaissances, Vigilance, Perception, Développement corporel, Artisanat, Discrétion et Listes professionnelles.</p>\n<h2>Guérisseur et gardien du territoire</h2>\n<p>Sa guérison repose largement sur les herbes et la connaissance du monde naturel. Il lit l’environnement, interprète les signes, protège le clan et peut utiliser sa magie pour renforcer ses aptitudes ordinaires.</p>\n<h2>Les tatouages sacrés</h2>\n<p>Chaque Drughân reçoit un tatouage sacré lors de son passage à l’âge adulte, tracé selon les traditions Drughu. Les motifs peuvent accorder camouflage, robustesse, relation privilégiée avec une espèce animale, puissance accrue, ténacité ou changement de forme. Le corps devient lui-même support de l’enchantement.</p>\n<h2>Artisanat des tatouages</h2>\n<p>L’<strong>Artisanat des Tatouages (Tattoo Crafts)</strong> permet d’enchanter des tatouages et d’y intégrer des sorts de Théurgie. Ces traditions constituent l’une des expressions magiques les plus spécifiques aux Drughu.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Animaux (Animal Mastery).</strong> Apaise, approche et influence les animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Herbes (Herb Mastery).</strong> Trouve, prépare et renforce les herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance de la Nature (Nature’s Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations de l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Mouvement et Sens de la Nature (Nature’s Movement/Senses).</strong> Améliore capacités physiques et sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection de la Nature (Nature’s Protection).</strong> Protège contre les dangers naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Plantes (Plant Mastery).</strong> Fait croître, restaure et influence les végétaux.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Échange avec les Listes du Rôdeur</h2>\n<p>Une particularité traditionnelle permet au Drughân de remplacer <strong>deux</strong> de ses six Listes par deux Listes professionnelles du Rôdeur. Ce choix représente une orientation culturelle précise et ne donne pas un accès libre à l’ensemble des Listes du Rôdeur.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est construit sur un <strong>châssis semi-magique de Théurgie</strong> proche du Rôdeur, avec <strong>Intuition et Mémoire</strong>. Les six Listes naturelles et l’échange possible de deux Listes sont conservés.</p>\n<p>L’élément le plus spécifique à MERP reste le système des tatouages sacrés et de l’Artisanat des Tatouages, qui devra être représenté par des Talents et mécanismes adaptés.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Drughan","system":{"description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Drughân</strong> is a semi-spell-user of <strong>Channeling</strong> found exclusively within <strong>Drughu</strong> culture. The Profession belongs to the great shamanic brotherhood of the <strong>Drughôr-ghan</strong>, the “Great Brothers,” whose members are chosen and trained from youth to become the <strong>healers, spiritual advisers, and keepers of tradition</strong> of their people.</p>\n<p>The role combines knowledge of nature, herbal medicine, relationships with animals, protection of the clan, spiritual interpretation of the world, and transmission of ancient lore. A Drughân living in the <strong>Drúadan Forest</strong> may be keeper of secret paths, master of plants, and protector of a small isolated clan.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Drughân’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Memory (Me)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Memory reflects the oral transmission of knowledge: plants, animals, rites, sacred places, histories, and ritual tattoos.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. Development favours skills related to Animals and the Environment, Lore, Awareness, Perception, Body Development, Craft, Stealth, magical skills, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>The Drughôr-ghan</h2>\n<p>The Drughôr-ghan form a shamanic brotherhood extending beyond any single clan. A young Drughu chosen to become a Drughân learns traditions, plants, animals, rites, Channeling, the making and meaning of tattoos, and the responsibilities of the future role.</p>\n<h2>Healing, Animals, and Herbs</h2>\n<p>Healing rests primarily on knowledge of the natural world. <strong>Herb Mastery</strong> finds, prepares, and strengthens remedies. <strong>Animal Mastery</strong> calms, approaches, and influences different creatures. In the Drúadan Forest, these abilities are grounded in intimate knowledge of every season and every part of the land.</p>\n<h2>Reading the Environment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong> interprets vegetation, animal tracks, changes in bird behaviour, disturbances in the ground, and signs of weather. <strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong> enhances physical and sensory abilities. <strong>Nature’s Protection</strong> helps resist natural hazards.</p>\n<h2>Sacred Tattoos</h2>\n<p>At adulthood, every Drughân receives <strong>a sacred tattoo</strong> drawn with the <em>colouring stone</em>. Each spiral design has a unique pattern and colour. The tattoo marks membership in the brotherhood, passage into adulthood, and a personal magical capacity.</p>\n<p>Traditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of the Wilds</strong>, camouflage adapted to a chosen environment;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Hardiness</strong>, natural protection;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Friendliness</strong>, a magical bond with an animal species;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Power</strong>, a substantial increase in the reserve of Power;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Toughness</strong>, improved resistance to wounds;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Being</strong>, transformation into a chosen animal, avian, or plant form.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tattoo Craft</h2>\n<p>Drughu traditions also develop a true <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong> discipline capable of enchanting tattoos and embedding Channeling spells within them. The body itself becomes the medium of enchantment.</p>\n<h2>Drughân and Animist</h2>\n<p>The Drughân and Animist share many Lists. The Animist is a pure spell-user specializing in the living world; the Drughân is a semi-spell-user integrated into a specific society, combining natural magic, physical resilience, craft, and communal responsibility. Sacred tattoos and membership in the Drughôr-ghan give the Profession its distinct identity.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Herb Mastery, First Aid, and natural lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Forest Guardian.</strong> Nature’s Lore, Nature’s Protection, Stealth, and Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Animal Master.</strong> Animal Mastery, Animal Handling, and knowledge of fauna.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of Tradition.</strong> Lore, rites, and Tattoo Crafts.</p>\n<p><strong>Shape-changer.</strong> Tattoo of Being and an identity built around the chosen form.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Animal Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plant Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A traditional option allows the Drughân to replace <strong>two of these six Lists</strong> with two professional Lists of the Ranger.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We build the adaptation from a <strong>semi-spell-user Channeling chassis</strong>, close to the Ranger in overall balance, while preserving the Drughu identity and <strong>Intuition and Memory</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The six natural Lists remain the reference, using corresponding modern mechanics where possible. The most distinctive element remains the system of <strong>sacred tattoos</strong> and <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong>, represented through adapted Talents and mechanics.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"drughan","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-semi-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Drughân","system":{"description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Drughân</strong> est un utilisateur semi-magique de <strong>Théurgie</strong>, exclusivement issu de la culture <strong>Drughu</strong>. Il appartient aux Drughôr-ghan, les « Grands Frères », fraternité chamanique dont les membres deviennent guérisseurs, conseillers spirituels et gardiens des traditions.</p>\n<p>Chaque clan Drughu possède normalement un Drughân. Dans la Forêt de Drúadan, il peut être gardien des chemins secrets, connaisseur des plantes et protecteur d’un petit clan. Aux Âges anciens, la même Profession peut représenter une tradition héritée des premiers peuples humains de l’Ouest.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong>. L’Intuition est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Mémoire soutient la transmission orale des plantes, animaux, rites, lieux sacrés et traditions du peuple.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Animaux, Environnement, Connaissances, Vigilance, Perception, Développement corporel, Artisanat, Discrétion et Listes professionnelles.</p>\n<h2>Guérisseur et gardien du territoire</h2>\n<p>Sa guérison repose largement sur les herbes et la connaissance du monde naturel. Il lit l’environnement, interprète les signes, protège le clan et peut utiliser sa magie pour renforcer ses aptitudes ordinaires.</p>\n<h2>Les tatouages sacrés</h2>\n<p>Chaque Drughân reçoit un tatouage sacré lors de son passage à l’âge adulte, tracé selon les traditions Drughu. Les motifs peuvent accorder camouflage, robustesse, relation privilégiée avec une espèce animale, puissance accrue, ténacité ou changement de forme. Le corps devient lui-même support de l’enchantement.</p>\n<h2>Artisanat des tatouages</h2>\n<p>L’<strong>Artisanat des Tatouages (Tattoo Crafts)</strong> permet d’enchanter des tatouages et d’y intégrer des sorts de Théurgie. Ces traditions constituent l’une des expressions magiques les plus spécifiques aux Drughu.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Animaux (Animal Mastery).</strong> Apaise, approche et influence les animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Herbes (Herb Mastery).</strong> Trouve, prépare et renforce les herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance de la Nature (Nature’s Lore).</strong> Obtient des informations de l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Mouvement et Sens de la Nature (Nature’s Movement/Senses).</strong> Améliore capacités physiques et sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection de la Nature (Nature’s Protection).</strong> Protège contre les dangers naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Plantes (Plant Mastery).</strong> Fait croître, restaure et influence les végétaux.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Échange avec les Listes du Rôdeur</h2>\n<p>Une particularité traditionnelle permet au Drughân de remplacer <strong>deux</strong> de ses six Listes par deux Listes professionnelles du Rôdeur. Ce choix représente une orientation culturelle précise et ne donne pas un accès libre à l’ensemble des Listes du Rôdeur.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Le Drughân est construit sur un <strong>châssis semi-magique de Théurgie</strong> proche du Rôdeur, avec <strong>Intuition et Mémoire</strong>. Les six Listes naturelles et l’échange possible de deux Listes sont conservés.</p>\n<p>L’élément le plus spécifique à MERP reste le système des tatouages sacrés et de l’Artisanat des Tatouages, qui devra être représenté par des Talents et mécanismes adaptés.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Drughan","system":{"description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Drughân</strong> is a semi-spell-user of <strong>Channeling</strong> found exclusively within <strong>Drughu</strong> culture. The Profession belongs to the great shamanic brotherhood of the <strong>Drughôr-ghan</strong>, the “Great Brothers,” whose members are chosen and trained from youth to become the <strong>healers, spiritual advisers, and keepers of tradition</strong> of their people.</p>\n<p>The role combines knowledge of nature, herbal medicine, relationships with animals, protection of the clan, spiritual interpretation of the world, and transmission of ancient lore. A Drughân living in the <strong>Drúadan Forest</strong> may be keeper of secret paths, master of plants, and protector of a small isolated clan.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Drughân’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Memory (Me)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Memory reflects the oral transmission of knowledge: plants, animals, rites, sacred places, histories, and ritual tattoos.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. Development favours skills related to Animals and the Environment, Lore, Awareness, Perception, Body Development, Craft, Stealth, magical skills, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>The Drughôr-ghan</h2>\n<p>The Drughôr-ghan form a shamanic brotherhood extending beyond any single clan. A young Drughu chosen to become a Drughân learns traditions, plants, animals, rites, Channeling, the making and meaning of tattoos, and the responsibilities of the future role.</p>\n<h2>Healing, Animals, and Herbs</h2>\n<p>Healing rests primarily on knowledge of the natural world. <strong>Herb Mastery</strong> finds, prepares, and strengthens remedies. <strong>Animal Mastery</strong> calms, approaches, and influences different creatures. In the Drúadan Forest, these abilities are grounded in intimate knowledge of every season and every part of the land.</p>\n<h2>Reading the Environment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong> interprets vegetation, animal tracks, changes in bird behaviour, disturbances in the ground, and signs of weather. <strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong> enhances physical and sensory abilities. <strong>Nature’s Protection</strong> helps resist natural hazards.</p>\n<h2>Sacred Tattoos</h2>\n<p>At adulthood, every Drughân receives <strong>a sacred tattoo</strong> drawn with the <em>colouring stone</em>. Each spiral design has a unique pattern and colour. The tattoo marks membership in the brotherhood, passage into adulthood, and a personal magical capacity.</p>\n<p>Traditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of the Wilds</strong>, camouflage adapted to a chosen environment;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Hardiness</strong>, natural protection;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Friendliness</strong>, a magical bond with an animal species;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Power</strong>, a substantial increase in the reserve of Power;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Toughness</strong>, improved resistance to wounds;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Being</strong>, transformation into a chosen animal, avian, or plant form.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tattoo Craft</h2>\n<p>Drughu traditions also develop a true <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong> discipline capable of enchanting tattoos and embedding Channeling spells within them. The body itself becomes the medium of enchantment.</p>\n<h2>Drughân and Animist</h2>\n<p>The Drughân and Animist share many Lists. The Animist is a pure spell-user specializing in the living world; the Drughân is a semi-spell-user integrated into a specific society, combining natural magic, physical resilience, craft, and communal responsibility. Sacred tattoos and membership in the Drughôr-ghan give the Profession its distinct identity.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Herb Mastery, First Aid, and natural lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Forest Guardian.</strong> Nature’s Lore, Nature’s Protection, Stealth, and Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Animal Master.</strong> Animal Mastery, Animal Handling, and knowledge of fauna.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of Tradition.</strong> Lore, rites, and Tattoo Crafts.</p>\n<p><strong>Shape-changer.</strong> Tattoo of Being and an identity built around the chosen form.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Animal Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plant Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A traditional option allows the Drughân to replace <strong>two of these six Lists</strong> with two professional Lists of the Ranger.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We build the adaptation from a <strong>semi-spell-user Channeling chassis</strong>, close to the Ranger in overall balance, while preserving the Drughu identity and <strong>Intuition and Memory</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The six natural Lists remain the reference, using corresponding modern mechanics where possible. The most distinctive element remains the system of <strong>sacred tattoos</strong> and <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong>, represented through adapted Talents and mechanics.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Semi-Magiques","Théurgie"],"en":["Semi Spell-user Professions","Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-semi-magiques","professions-semi-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Drughân","spellCastingGroup":"Semi","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling","description":"<h1>Drughân</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Drughân</strong> is a semi-spell-user of <strong>Channeling</strong> found exclusively within <strong>Drughu</strong> culture. The Profession belongs to the great shamanic brotherhood of the <strong>Drughôr-ghan</strong>, the “Great Brothers,” whose members are chosen and trained from youth to become the <strong>healers, spiritual advisers, and keepers of tradition</strong> of their people.</p>\n<p>The role combines knowledge of nature, herbal medicine, relationships with animals, protection of the clan, spiritual interpretation of the world, and transmission of ancient lore. A Drughân living in the <strong>Drúadan Forest</strong> may be keeper of secret paths, master of plants, and protector of a small isolated clan.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Drughân’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Memory (Me)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Memory reflects the oral transmission of knowledge: plants, animals, rites, sacred places, histories, and ritual tattoos.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. Development favours skills related to Animals and the Environment, Lore, Awareness, Perception, Body Development, Craft, Stealth, magical skills, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>The Drughôr-ghan</h2>\n<p>The Drughôr-ghan form a shamanic brotherhood extending beyond any single clan. A young Drughu chosen to become a Drughân learns traditions, plants, animals, rites, Channeling, the making and meaning of tattoos, and the responsibilities of the future role.</p>\n<h2>Healing, Animals, and Herbs</h2>\n<p>Healing rests primarily on knowledge of the natural world. <strong>Herb Mastery</strong> finds, prepares, and strengthens remedies. <strong>Animal Mastery</strong> calms, approaches, and influences different creatures. In the Drúadan Forest, these abilities are grounded in intimate knowledge of every season and every part of the land.</p>\n<h2>Reading the Environment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong> interprets vegetation, animal tracks, changes in bird behaviour, disturbances in the ground, and signs of weather. <strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong> enhances physical and sensory abilities. <strong>Nature’s Protection</strong> helps resist natural hazards.</p>\n<h2>Sacred Tattoos</h2>\n<p>At adulthood, every Drughân receives <strong>a sacred tattoo</strong> drawn with the <em>colouring stone</em>. Each spiral design has a unique pattern and colour. The tattoo marks membership in the brotherhood, passage into adulthood, and a personal magical capacity.</p>\n<p>Traditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of the Wilds</strong>, camouflage adapted to a chosen environment;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Hardiness</strong>, natural protection;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Friendliness</strong>, a magical bond with an animal species;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Power</strong>, a substantial increase in the reserve of Power;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Toughness</strong>, improved resistance to wounds;</li>\n<li><strong>Tattoo of Being</strong>, transformation into a chosen animal, avian, or plant form.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tattoo Craft</h2>\n<p>Drughu traditions also develop a true <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong> discipline capable of enchanting tattoos and embedding Channeling spells within them. The body itself becomes the medium of enchantment.</p>\n<h2>Drughân and Animist</h2>\n<p>The Drughân and Animist share many Lists. The Animist is a pure spell-user specializing in the living world; the Drughân is a semi-spell-user integrated into a specific society, combining natural magic, physical resilience, craft, and communal responsibility. Sacred tattoos and membership in the Drughôr-ghan give the Profession its distinct identity.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Herb Mastery, First Aid, and natural lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Forest Guardian.</strong> Nature’s Lore, Nature’s Protection, Stealth, and Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Animal Master.</strong> Animal Mastery, Animal Handling, and knowledge of fauna.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of Tradition.</strong> Lore, rites, and Tattoo Crafts.</p>\n<p><strong>Shape-changer.</strong> Tattoo of Being and an identity built around the chosen form.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Animal Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Lore</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Movement/Senses</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Protection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Plant Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A traditional option allows the Drughân to replace <strong>two of these six Lists</strong> with two professional Lists of the Ranger.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Drughân has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We build the adaptation from a <strong>semi-spell-user Channeling chassis</strong>, close to the Ranger in overall balance, while preserving the Drughu identity and <strong>Intuition and Memory</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The six natural Lists remain the reference, using corresponding modern mechanics where possible. The most distinctive element remains the system of <strong>sacred tattoos</strong> and <strong>Tattoo Crafts</strong>, represented through adapted Talents and mechanics.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"8CKiOZWT3zSQdNrK"}
{"name":"Fighter","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"fighter","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-core-law-adaptation","reference":"RMU Core Law — Fighter; MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Fighter"},"compendiumSourceKey":"fighter","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-non-magiques","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Guerrier","system":{"description":"<h1>Guerrier</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Guerrier</strong> est un combattant professionnel. Il consacre l’essentiel de son entraînement au maniement des armes, au port de l’armure et aux techniques qui permettent de survivre et de vaincre dans une confrontation directe. Dans les versions précédentes du jeu de rôle des Terres du Milieu et de Rolemaster, il représente déjà le spécialiste martial capable de développer plusieurs catégories d’armes et d’utiliser efficacement les protections lourdes.</p>\n<p>La Profession peut représenter un chevalier du Gondor, un cavalier du Rohan, un garde de cité, un mercenaire, un champion de clan, un archer, un combattant khazâd ou un vétéran. La Culture donne sa forme concrète au personnage : un Rohir privilégiera souvent lance, équitation et combat monté ; un Khazad pourra favoriser hache, bouclier et armure ; un Sinda pourra rechercher mobilité, arc et épée.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales du Guerrier sont <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> et <strong>Force (St)</strong>. La Constitution soutient l’endurance, la résistance aux blessures et aux contraintes prolongées. La Force intervient dans de nombreux entraînements martiaux, le maniement des armes et les efforts physiques liés au combat.</p>\n<p>Ces deux caractéristiques doivent recevoir une attention particulière lors de la création du personnage.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Guerrier est un <strong>non-utilisateur de sorts</strong>. Il ne possède aucune Liste de base. Son développement privilégie les Armes de mêlée, les Armes à distance, le Bouclier, le Combat à mains nues, les Manœuvres en armure, le Combat monté, le Développement corporel, la Protection, le Désarmement, les Attaques multiples, la Course et le Commandement.</p>\n<p>Il peut devenir très spécialisé dans une seule arme ou construire une formation martiale plus large. La Profession donne surtout la capacité de consacrer une part importante du développement à plusieurs disciplines de combat sans sacrifier sa robustesse générale.</p>\n<h2>Combat et équipement</h2>\n<p>Le Guerrier est naturellement à l’aise avec les armes et l’armure. Un soldat du Gondor peut apprendre la discipline de formation, le bouclier et les protections lourdes ; un cavalier du Rohan associera l’équitation à la lance et à l’épée ; un Khazad des Longues-Barbes pourra privilégier hache, marteau, bouclier et cotte de mailles ; un Corsaire d’Umbar pourra combiner lame, arc et techniques adaptées au combat naval.</p>\n<p>Le choix du style de combat relève donc de la Culture, de l’histoire personnelle et de l’équipement disponible plutôt que d’une école unique imposée par la Profession.</p>\n<h2>Profils de Guerriers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Guerrier lourd.</strong> Arme de mêlée, Bouclier, Manœuvres en armure et Développement corporel.</li>\n<li><strong>Archer.</strong> Armes à distance, Perception et mobilité.</li>\n<li><strong>Cavalier.</strong> Équitation, Combat monté et arme adaptée au combat à cheval.</li>\n<li><strong>Champion.</strong> Spécialisation poussée dans une arme et techniques de combat avancées.</li>\n<li><strong>Garde.</strong> Protection, Bouclier, Observation et éventuellement Commandement.</li>\n<li><strong>Capitaine.</strong> Compétences martiales, Commandement et aptitudes sociales nécessaires pour diriger une troupe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p><strong>Aucune.</strong> Le Guerrier ne possède pas de Liste de base professionnelle. Il peut théoriquement apprendre certaines formes de magie, mais cet apprentissage reste particulièrement coûteux et exceptionnel.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis mécanique du Fighter RMU</strong> : ses coûts de développement, ses groupes d’entraînement au combat et ses compétences professionnelles constituent la base de la Profession.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU rétablit explicitement les caractéristiques primordiales <strong>Constitution et Force</strong> et replace l’identité du Guerrier dans les traditions militaires de la Terre du Milieu. Sa Race et sa Culture déterminent les armes, les protections et la forme concrète de son entraînement. Son absence de magie reste volontairement nette afin de conserver la distinction traditionnelle entre combattants professionnels et professions magiques.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Fighter","system":{"description":"<h1>Fighter</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Fighter</strong> is a professional combatant. Most of the profession’s training is devoted to weapons, armour, and the techniques needed to survive and prevail in direct confrontation. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Fighter already represents the martial specialist able to develop several weapon categories and use heavy protection effectively.</p>\n<p>The Profession may represent a knight of Gondor, a rider of Rohan, a city guard, mercenary, clan champion, archer, Khazâd warrior, or veteran. Culture gives the character a concrete form: a Rohir will often favour spear, riding, and mounted combat; a Khazad may favour axe, shield, and armour; a Sinda may seek mobility, bow, and sword.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Fighter’s Prime Stats are <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> and <strong>Strength (St)</strong>. Constitution supports endurance, resistance to wounds, and prolonged physical hardship. Strength naturally contributes to many martial disciplines, weapon use, and strenuous physical effort.</p>\n<p>These two Stats deserve particular attention during character creation.</p>\n<h2>Technical orientation</h2>\n<p>The Fighter is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong> and has no Base Lists. Development favours Melee Weapons, Ranged Weapons, Shield, Unarmed Combat, Maneuvering in Armor, Mounted Combat, Body Development, Protect, Disarm, Multiple Attacks, Running, and Leadership.</p>\n<p>A Fighter may become highly specialized in one weapon or build a broader martial training. The Profession’s main strength is the ability to invest heavily in several combat disciplines without giving up overall physical resilience.</p>\n<h2>Combat and equipment</h2>\n<p>Fighters are naturally comfortable with weapons and armour. A Gondorian soldier may learn formation discipline, shield, and heavy armour; a rider of Rohan may combine riding with spear and sword; a Longbeard Khazad may favour axe, hammer, shield, and mail; a Corsair of Umbar may combine blade, bow, and techniques suited to fighting at sea.</p>\n<p>The choice of fighting style therefore comes from Culture, personal history, and available equipment rather than from a single school imposed by the Profession.</p>\n<h2>Fighter profiles</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Heavy Fighter.</strong> Melee weapon, Shield, Maneuvering in Armor, and Body Development.</li><li><strong>Archer.</strong> Ranged Weapons, Perception, and mobility.</li><li><strong>Rider.</strong> Riding, Mounted Combat, and a weapon suited to fighting from horseback.</li><li><strong>Champion.</strong> Deep specialization in one main weapon and advanced combat techniques.</li><li><strong>Guard.</strong> Protect, Shield, Observation, and possibly Leadership.</li><li><strong>Captain.</strong> Martial skills combined with Leadership and the social abilities needed to command troops.</li></ul>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong> The Fighter has no professional Base Spell List. A Fighter can theoretically learn some forms of magic, but doing so is unusually costly and represents an exceptional path.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>RMU Fighter mechanical chassis</strong>: its development costs, combat training groups, and professional skills remain the foundation of the Profession.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU explicitly restores <strong>Constitution and Strength</strong> as the Prime Stats and places the Fighter within the military traditions of Middle-earth. Race and Culture determine weapons, protection, and the concrete form of training. The deliberate absence of magic preserves the traditional distinction between professional warriors and magical professions.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions non magiques"],"en":["Non-magical Professions"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-non-magiques"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"RMU Core Law","profession":"Fighter","spellCastingGroup":"Non","numberOfBaseLists":0,"realms":"","description":"<h1>Fighter</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Fighter</strong> is a professional combatant. Most of the profession’s training is devoted to weapons, armour, and the techniques needed to survive and prevail in direct confrontation. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Fighter already represents the martial specialist able to develop several weapon categories and use heavy protection effectively.</p>\n<p>The Profession may represent a knight of Gondor, a rider of Rohan, a city guard, mercenary, clan champion, archer, Khazâd warrior, or veteran. Culture gives the character a concrete form: a Rohir will often favour spear, riding, and mounted combat; a Khazad may favour axe, shield, and armour; a Sinda may seek mobility, bow, and sword.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Fighter’s Prime Stats are <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> and <strong>Strength (St)</strong>. Constitution supports endurance, resistance to wounds, and prolonged physical hardship. Strength naturally contributes to many martial disciplines, weapon use, and strenuous physical effort.</p>\n<p>These two Stats deserve particular attention during character creation.</p>\n<h2>Technical orientation</h2>\n<p>The Fighter is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong> and has no Base Lists. Development favours Melee Weapons, Ranged Weapons, Shield, Unarmed Combat, Maneuvering in Armor, Mounted Combat, Body Development, Protect, Disarm, Multiple Attacks, Running, and Leadership.</p>\n<p>A Fighter may become highly specialized in one weapon or build a broader martial training. The Profession’s main strength is the ability to invest heavily in several combat disciplines without giving up overall physical resilience.</p>\n<h2>Combat and equipment</h2>\n<p>Fighters are naturally comfortable with weapons and armour. A Gondorian soldier may learn formation discipline, shield, and heavy armour; a rider of Rohan may combine riding with spear and sword; a Longbeard Khazad may favour axe, hammer, shield, and mail; a Corsair of Umbar may combine blade, bow, and techniques suited to fighting at sea.</p>\n<p>The choice of fighting style therefore comes from Culture, personal history, and available equipment rather than from a single school imposed by the Profession.</p>\n<h2>Fighter profiles</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Heavy Fighter.</strong> Melee weapon, Shield, Maneuvering in Armor, and Body Development.</li><li><strong>Archer.</strong> Ranged Weapons, Perception, and mobility.</li><li><strong>Rider.</strong> Riding, Mounted Combat, and a weapon suited to fighting from horseback.</li><li><strong>Champion.</strong> Deep specialization in one main weapon and advanced combat techniques.</li><li><strong>Guard.</strong> Protect, Shield, Observation, and possibly Leadership.</li><li><strong>Captain.</strong> Martial skills combined with Leadership and the social abilities needed to command troops.</li></ul>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong> The Fighter has no professional Base Spell List. A Fighter can theoretically learn some forms of magic, but doing so is unusually costly and represents an exceptional path.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>RMU Fighter mechanical chassis</strong>: its development costs, combat training groups, and professional skills remain the foundation of the Profession.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU explicitly restores <strong>Constitution and Strength</strong> as the Prime Stats and places the Fighter within the military traditions of Middle-earth. Race and Culture determine weapons, protection, and the concrete form of training. The deliberate absence of magic preserves the traditional distinction between professional warriors and magical professions.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"2/3"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"3/4"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"1/2"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"1/2"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"2/3"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"3/4"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Riding"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Maneuvering in Armor"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Mounted Combat"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","skillName":"Protect"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Body Development"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Fortitude"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","skillName":"Weight Training"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","skillName":"Disarm"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","skillName":"Multiple Attacks"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Melee Weapons"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Ranged Weapons"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Shield"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Metalcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running"},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Leadership"}]},"_id":"IETUcvRptZkMm3dw"}
{"name":"Herutano","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"herutano","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-core-law-adaptation","reference":"RMU Core Law — Alchemist mechanical chassis; RMSS/FRP — Herutano"},"compendiumSourceKey":"herutano","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Herutano","system":{"description":"<h1>Herutano</h1>\n<p>L’<strong>Herutano</strong> — <em>Herutanor</em> au pluriel, « maître artisan » en quenya — est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, exclusivement lié aux cultures elfiques. Sa magie repose sur l’interaction entre sa propre force intérieure, son <em>fairë</em>, et la matière qu’il travaille. Il crée des objets magiques, enchante armes et protections, transforme les matériaux, prépare des potions et fixe des sorts dans des objets.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession convient particulièrement aux Noldor. Un Herutano de Fondcombe peut restaurer des œuvres anciennes ; un artisan des Havres Gris travailler sur des instruments ou objets de voyage ; aux Âges anciens, un maître d’Eregion représente l’expression la plus accomplie de cette tradition.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. La Présence est aussi la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme et représente la puissance intérieure projetée dans l’œuvre. L’Autodiscipline soutient précision, patience et contrôle sur des travaux de très longue durée.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>L’Herutano est un <strong>pur utilisateur de Mentalisme</strong>. Sa formation favorise Artisanat, Listes de base, connaissances magiques et anciennes, Autocontrôle, Perception de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, compétences techniques et Développement corporel.</p>\n<h2>Le fairë et la matière</h2>\n<p>L’Herutano agit sur le monde physique à travers son <em>fairë</em>. Il comprend progressivement la structure d’un matériau et y imprime une intention. Chez les Elfes, art, connaissance et magie forment souvent un seul continuum aux yeux des Hommes.</p>\n<h2>Une tradition elfique</h2>\n<p>Chez les Noldor, la tradition s’exprime naturellement dans forge, gemmes et enchantements. Chez les Sindar, elle peut être davantage liée au bois, aux instruments et aux ornements. Chez les Elfes sylvains, une forme plus rare peut privilégier matières organiques et objets intimement liés à l’environnement.</p>\n<h2>Création d’objets et maîtrise du corps</h2>\n<p>L’Herutano peut créer armes, armures, vêtements, bijoux, outils, potions et objets contenant des sorts. Plusieurs traditions lui donnent également un contrôle accru sur son propre corps, appliquant à sa propre forme la même discipline mentale qu’à la matière extérieure.</p>\n<h2>Neuf traditions, six Listes</h2>\n<p>L’Herutano dispose de <strong>neuf Listes professionnelles</strong> et en choisit exactement <strong>six comme Listes de base</strong>. Les trois restantes conservent un statut plus difficile et ne peuvent pas être ajoutées ensuite comme septième, huitième ou neuvième Liste de base. Ce choix définit la spécialité artisanale initiale du personnage.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise de l’Attaque (Attack Mastery).</strong> Crée des armes magiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise de la Défense (Defense Mastery).</strong> Crée armures et protections magiques par concentration.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentrations Extérieures (External Concentrations).</strong> Modifie la forme des matières organiques et inorganiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Techniques Inorganiques Elfiques (Elven Inorganic Skills).</strong> Travaille les matières inorganiques selon les traditions elfiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Techniques des Liquides et des Gaz (Liquid/Gas Skills).</strong> Permet notamment de créer des potions.</li>\n<li><strong>Guérison Méditative (Meditative Healing).</strong> Soigne les blessures du lanceur et celles d’autres personnes.</li>\n<li><strong>Imprégnation d’un Autre Royaume (Other Realm Imbedding).</strong> Fixe dans un objet un sort d’un autre royaume.</li>\n<li><strong>Imprégnation de son Propre Royaume (Own Realm Imbedding).</strong> Fixe des sorts de Mentalisme dans des objets.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise de la Compréhension (Understanding Mastery).</strong> Crée des objets magiques généraux.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Le déclin des arts</h2>\n<p>Au Premier Âge, les plus grands artisans elfes produisent des œuvres presque inimaginables aux générations ultérieures. Au Deuxième Âge, Eregion devient un centre majeur de l’artisanat magique. Au Troisième Âge, Fondcombe et les Havres Gris préservent encore des connaissances remarquables, mais maîtres, ateliers et matériaux se font plus rares.</p>\n<h2>Artisanat et Ombre</h2>\n<p>La création peut être détournée vers la domination. Les savoirs hérités d’Eregion peuvent conduire à des techniques extraordinaires et dangereuses lorsque leur origine remonte aux enseignements d’Annatar. Les règles de Sorcellerie, Corruption et Risque d’attirer l’Ombre s’appliquent selon la nature exacte de la pratique.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>L’Herutano n’a pas d’équivalent professionnel direct dans RMU. MERP-RMU utilise le <strong>châssis mécanique de l’Alchimiste RMU</strong>, proche par son orientation Artisanat et création, puis le convertit en <strong>pur Mentalisme</strong>. Nous conservons <strong>Présence et Autodiscipline</strong>, la restriction aux cultures elfiques et le choix historique de <strong>six Listes parmi neuf</strong>.</p>\n<p>Le créateur de personnage interdit mécaniquement l’ajout d’une septième Liste de base Herutano. Les trois traditions non choisies restent accessibles seulement selon leur statut historique plus difficile.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Herutano","system":{"description":"<h1>Herutano</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Herutano</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user found exclusively among Elven cultures and specializing in the creation of magical objects through the action of <em>fairë</em>—the spirit or inner power of the maker—upon matter. The Profession represents the Elven craftsman whose discipline reaches beyond ordinary craftsmanship into the shaping of objects through will, understanding, and inner harmony.</p>\n<p>Among the Noldor this tradition can encompass smithcraft, jewel-work, potions, enchanted weapons and armour, restoration of ancient works, and the embedding of spells in objects. The greatest Herutani belong to cultures in which craft and knowledge of the substance of Arda are themselves part of a profound spiritual tradition.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Herutano’s Prime Stats are <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong> and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism and expresses the force of the maker’s inner being; Self Discipline represents the concentration, precision, and mastery of will required to impose a chosen form upon matter without destroying its nature.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Herutano is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Craft, magical lore, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Power Perception, technical skills, and knowledge relating to materials, creation, and enchanted objects.</p>\n<h2>Fairë and Matter</h2>\n<p>The Herutano tradition rests on the idea that true creation is not merely mechanical. The maker’s <em>fairë</em> acts upon the material through knowledge, discipline, and intention. An object therefore bears something of its creator: not necessarily a fragment of the soul, but the mark of a will and understanding brought into harmony with the substance being shaped.</p>\n<h2>An Elven Tradition</h2>\n<p>The Profession belongs above all to cultures with deep traditions of craftsmanship and lore. Among the <strong>Noldor</strong>, it may evoke the great smiths and jewel-makers of the First Age and the craftsmen of <strong>Eregion</strong> in the Second. Other Elven peoples may preserve narrower or more local traditions, but the Profession should remain rare and closely tied to access to masters, workshops, and old knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Creation of Objects</h2>\n<p>The Herutano can create enchanted weapons, armour, protective objects, potions, tools, and works bearing embedded magical effects. Such creation is never reduced to a simple game procedure: appropriate materials, time, craft skill, and access to the relevant tradition remain essential.</p>\n<h2>Mastery of the Body</h2>\n<p><strong>External Concentrations</strong> and <strong>Meditative Healing</strong> represent the application of Mentalism to living form. Through concentration and inner discipline, the Herutano can reinforce control of the body or restore damaged living structures.</p>\n<h2>Enchanted Weapons and Defences</h2>\n<p><strong>Attack Mastery</strong> and <strong>Defense Mastery</strong> support the creation or enhancement of offensive and defensive objects. In an Elven context these traditions may produce blades, bows, armour, shields, or protective items whose qualities arise as much from exquisite craftsmanship as from magic.</p>\n<h2>Transformation of Matter</h2>\n<p><strong>Elven Inorganic Skills</strong> concerns stone, metal, gems, glass, and other inorganic materials as understood through Elven craft traditions. It reflects knowledge of matter at a level that ordinary artisans may perceive simply as impossible skill.</p>\n<h2>Potions and Meditative Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong> can be used, among other things, to create potions and prepared substances. <strong>Meditative Healing</strong> can treat the wounds of the caster and of others. This healing rests on the idea of restoring harmony to a living form through disciplined mental action.</p>\n<h2>Imbedding and Understanding</h2>\n<p><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong> fixes Mentalism spells into objects. <strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong> allows cooperation with a Magician, Animist, or another spell-user. <strong>Understanding Mastery</strong> supports the creation of general magical items.</p>\n<h2>Nine Traditions, Six Lists</h2>\n<p>The Herutano has <strong>nine professional Lists</strong>, of which <strong>six are chosen as Base Spell Lists</strong>. The remaining three become more difficult to develop. This choice allows very different specializations: Noldo smith, master enchanter, potion-maker, healer-craftsman, or restorer of ancient works.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Herutano chooses <strong>six Base Spell Lists from nine</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Attack Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Defense Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>External Concentrations</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Elven Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Meditative Healing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Understanding Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Decline of the Arts</h2>\n<p>The Herutano illustrates the gradual decline of great lore. In the First Age, the greatest Elven craftsmen created works almost unimaginable to later generations. In the Second Age, <strong>Eregion</strong> became a major centre of magical craftsmanship. By the Third Age, Rivendell and the Grey Havens still preserve exceptional knowledge, but the great workshops are gone.</p>\n<p>A late Herutano may know the principle of a technique yet lack the material, master, workshop, or complementary lore that has been lost.</p>\n<h2>Craft and the Shadow</h2>\n<p>Creation can be corrupted. A craftsman who seeks to impose will upon matter, create objects of domination, or reproduce techniques derived from the teaching of <strong>Sauron</strong> may enter the realm of Corruption. Traditions descending from Eregion therefore require particular attention to their exact origin.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Herutano has no direct professional equivalent among standard RMU Professions. We preserve its distinct identity: a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user, restricted to Elven cultures, specializing in the creation of magical objects through the action of fairë upon matter</strong>, with <strong>Presence and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The special organization of the Profession is also preserved: <strong>nine professional traditions, of which only six become Base Spell Lists</strong>. RMU mechanics for craft, item creation, imbedding, and manipulation of matter provide the mechanical foundation where equivalents exist. The Herutano remains the Elven artisan whose mastery reaches the point where the spirit of the maker, the substance being worked, and the form of the finished work become three aspects of a single act of creation.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Mentalisme"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Herutano","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Mentalism","description":"<h1>Herutano</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Herutano</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user found exclusively among Elven cultures and specializing in the creation of magical objects through the action of <em>fairë</em>—the spirit or inner power of the maker—upon matter. The Profession represents the Elven craftsman whose discipline reaches beyond ordinary craftsmanship into the shaping of objects through will, understanding, and inner harmony.</p>\n<p>Among the Noldor this tradition can encompass smithcraft, jewel-work, potions, enchanted weapons and armour, restoration of ancient works, and the embedding of spells in objects. The greatest Herutani belong to cultures in which craft and knowledge of the substance of Arda are themselves part of a profound spiritual tradition.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Herutano’s Prime Stats are <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong> and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism and expresses the force of the maker’s inner being; Self Discipline represents the concentration, precision, and mastery of will required to impose a chosen form upon matter without destroying its nature.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Herutano is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Craft, magical lore, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Power Perception, technical skills, and knowledge relating to materials, creation, and enchanted objects.</p>\n<h2>Fairë and Matter</h2>\n<p>The Herutano tradition rests on the idea that true creation is not merely mechanical. The maker’s <em>fairë</em> acts upon the material through knowledge, discipline, and intention. An object therefore bears something of its creator: not necessarily a fragment of the soul, but the mark of a will and understanding brought into harmony with the substance being shaped.</p>\n<h2>An Elven Tradition</h2>\n<p>The Profession belongs above all to cultures with deep traditions of craftsmanship and lore. Among the <strong>Noldor</strong>, it may evoke the great smiths and jewel-makers of the First Age and the craftsmen of <strong>Eregion</strong> in the Second. Other Elven peoples may preserve narrower or more local traditions, but the Profession should remain rare and closely tied to access to masters, workshops, and old knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Creation of Objects</h2>\n<p>The Herutano can create enchanted weapons, armour, protective objects, potions, tools, and works bearing embedded magical effects. Such creation is never reduced to a simple game procedure: appropriate materials, time, craft skill, and access to the relevant tradition remain essential.</p>\n<h2>Mastery of the Body</h2>\n<p><strong>External Concentrations</strong> and <strong>Meditative Healing</strong> represent the application of Mentalism to living form. Through concentration and inner discipline, the Herutano can reinforce control of the body or restore damaged living structures.</p>\n<h2>Enchanted Weapons and Defences</h2>\n<p><strong>Attack Mastery</strong> and <strong>Defense Mastery</strong> support the creation or enhancement of offensive and defensive objects. In an Elven context these traditions may produce blades, bows, armour, shields, or protective items whose qualities arise as much from exquisite craftsmanship as from magic.</p>\n<h2>Transformation of Matter</h2>\n<p><strong>Elven Inorganic Skills</strong> concerns stone, metal, gems, glass, and other inorganic materials as understood through Elven craft traditions. It reflects knowledge of matter at a level that ordinary artisans may perceive simply as impossible skill.</p>\n<h2>Potions and Meditative Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong> can be used, among other things, to create potions and prepared substances. <strong>Meditative Healing</strong> can treat the wounds of the caster and of others. This healing rests on the idea of restoring harmony to a living form through disciplined mental action.</p>\n<h2>Imbedding and Understanding</h2>\n<p><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong> fixes Mentalism spells into objects. <strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong> allows cooperation with a Magician, Animist, or another spell-user. <strong>Understanding Mastery</strong> supports the creation of general magical items.</p>\n<h2>Nine Traditions, Six Lists</h2>\n<p>The Herutano has <strong>nine professional Lists</strong>, of which <strong>six are chosen as Base Spell Lists</strong>. The remaining three become more difficult to develop. This choice allows very different specializations: Noldo smith, master enchanter, potion-maker, healer-craftsman, or restorer of ancient works.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Herutano chooses <strong>six Base Spell Lists from nine</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Attack Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Defense Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>External Concentrations</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Elven Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liquid/Gas Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Meditative Healing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Understanding Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Decline of the Arts</h2>\n<p>The Herutano illustrates the gradual decline of great lore. In the First Age, the greatest Elven craftsmen created works almost unimaginable to later generations. In the Second Age, <strong>Eregion</strong> became a major centre of magical craftsmanship. By the Third Age, Rivendell and the Grey Havens still preserve exceptional knowledge, but the great workshops are gone.</p>\n<p>A late Herutano may know the principle of a technique yet lack the material, master, workshop, or complementary lore that has been lost.</p>\n<h2>Craft and the Shadow</h2>\n<p>Creation can be corrupted. A craftsman who seeks to impose will upon matter, create objects of domination, or reproduce techniques derived from the teaching of <strong>Sauron</strong> may enter the realm of Corruption. Traditions descending from Eregion therefore require particular attention to their exact origin.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Herutano has no direct professional equivalent among standard RMU Professions. We preserve its distinct identity: a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user, restricted to Elven cultures, specializing in the creation of magical objects through the action of fairë upon matter</strong>, with <strong>Presence and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The special organization of the Profession is also preserved: <strong>nine professional traditions, of which only six become Base Spell Lists</strong>. RMU mechanics for craft, item creation, imbedding, and manipulation of matter provide the mechanical foundation where equivalents exist. The Herutano remains the Elven artisan whose mastery reaches the point where the spirit of the maker, the substance being worked, and the form of the finished work become three aspects of a single act of creation.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"2/3"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Attunement"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Runes"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Spell Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Materials Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","skillName":"Grace"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Metalcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Stonecraft"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Engineering"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Locks"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Crafting"}]},"_id":"IQDFdx0YhJs4W2al"}
{"name":"Prêtre Honnin (Ônu)","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"honnin-priest","collection":"honnin-beta","contentVersion":1,"beta":true,"restriction":{"culture":"Honnin"},"compendiumSourceKey":"honnin-priest","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/honnin-priest-beta-v1.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Théurgie"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU — Bêta","profession":"Honnin Priest","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling","description":"<h1>Prêtre Honnin (Ônu)</h1>\n<p>Les <strong>Ônu</strong> sont les prêtres-chamans des Honnin. Ils servent d’intermédiaires entre leur clan et les puissances qui habitent la forêt : esprits des oiseaux, des bêtes, des plantes, des eaux et des lieux anciens. Leur tradition accorde une place particulière à <strong>Suzush</strong>, déesse de la Lune, à <strong>Nuva-Tu</strong>, dieu de la Nature, et aux innombrables esprits considérés comme leurs enfants.</p>\n<p>Un Ônu n’est pas seulement un lanceur de sorts. Il officie lors des fêtes saisonnières, conserve les récits du clan, interprète les signes du monde vivant, soigne, conseille les matriarches et veille sur les lieux sacrés. Les grands rassemblements tels que le <strong>Pujna-Kasl</strong> sont organisés par les prêtres Honnin, qui y conduisent les cérémonies et entretiennent la mémoire commune.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Pour cette adaptation bêta, les caractéristiques primordiales du Prêtre Honnin sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Mémoire (Me)</strong>. L’Intuition représente sa sensibilité aux signes du monde vivant et constitue la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; la Mémoire traduit la transmission orale des rites, des plantes, des esprits et des traditions claniques.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Prêtre Honnin est un <strong>utilisateur pur de Théurgie</strong>. Son châssis de développement reprend volontairement celui de l’Animiste MERP-RMU pour cette première version bêta. Il favorise les Animaux, l’Environnement, les connaissances, la médecine naturelle, la Manipulation de la Puissance, les Listes de sorts et les rituels.</p>\n<p>Ses Compétences Professionnelles mettent davantage l’accent sur le <strong>Tatouage (Tattooing)</strong>, la connaissance des créatures, l’Herboristerie, la Religion, les rituels magiques, la Survie et le rôle de chaman au sein du clan.</p>\n<h2>Les tatouages sacrés</h2>\n<p>Les tatouages Honnin peuvent marquer une étape de la vie, une appartenance cultuelle, un rang ou une dévotion particulière. Chez certains ordres, les prêtres les plus accomplis portent une grande partie de leur histoire religieuse sur leur propre peau. Les motifs sont complexes, fluides et colorés ; certains sont purement symboliques, tandis que d’autres deviennent de véritables réceptacles de pouvoir.</p>\n<p>La Liste <strong>Artisanat du Tatouage (Tattoo Crafts)</strong> permet de créer ces tatouages magiques. Un tatouage enchanté ne peut contenir qu’une seule capacité, ne peut croiser un autre tatouage magique et une même personne ne peut en porter plus de treize. Seuls des sorts de Théurgie peuvent y être imprégnés.</p>\n<h2>Oiseaux et esprits de la forêt</h2>\n<p>Les oiseaux occupent une place importante dans les traditions religieuses Honnin. La Liste <strong>Voies Aviaires (Avian Ways)</strong> permet de faire taire les oiseaux, d’échapper à leur perception, de comprendre leur langage, d’en apprivoiser certains, de les appeler et, aux plus hauts niveaux, d’emprunter leur forme.</p>\n<p><strong>Communions (Communions)</strong> approfondit cette relation : l’Ônu perçoit les présences, partage les sensations et les émotions des oiseaux, emprunte leur vue ou leur ouïe et étend progressivement sa conscience à la vie qui l’entoure.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base — version bêta</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Animaux (Animal Mastery).</strong> Approche, apaise et influence les animaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Herbes (Herb Mastery).</strong> Recherche, prépare et renforce les remèdes végétaux.</li>\n<li><strong>Connaissance de la Nature (Nature’s Lore).</strong> Interprète le terrain, les phénomènes naturels et les signes du monde vivant.</li>\n<li><strong>Artisanat du Tatouage (Tattoo Crafts).</strong> Crée et enchante les tatouages rituels des Honnin.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Aviaires (Avian Ways).</strong> Communique avec les oiseaux, les appelle et emprunte progressivement leurs capacités et leur forme.</li>\n<li><strong>Communions (Communions).</strong> Établit des liens sensoriels et spirituels avec les oiseaux et les présences environnantes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la société Honnin</h2>\n<p>La Profession est normalement réservée aux <strong>Honnin — Suzamatu</strong>. L’Ônu appartient à une tradition précise, liée à un clan, à un sanctuaire ou à un esprit particulier. Il peut être guérisseur, gardien d’un lieu ancien, officiant saisonnier, interprète des présages ou tatoueur rituel. Certains demeurent toute leur vie dans la Forêt des Larmes ; d’autres peuvent être envoyés au-dehors pour accomplir une mission dont l’importance justifie de rompre l’isolement traditionnel des Honnin.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP — Bêta</h2>\n<p>Le document RMSS/RMFRP décrit les Honnin, leurs prêtres et trois Listes de Théurgie propres à leur tradition, mais ne fournit pas une Profession autonome intitulée « Honnin ». Cette fiche constitue donc une <strong>reconstruction MERP-RMU destinée au bêta-test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Le châssis de l’Animiste a été retenu comme référence mécanique afin de limiter les variables d’équilibrage. Les trois Listes Honnin ont été promues au rang de Listes de base de cette Profession, aux côtés de trois Listes naturelles compatibles avec son rôle. Les retours de jeu devront principalement porter sur le coût réel du Tatouage, la puissance des tatouages permanents, l’utilité des Voies Aviaires et l’équilibre général face à l’Animiste.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"2/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"3/4"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","skillName":"Animal Handling"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Perception"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Tracking"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Tattooing"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","skillName":"Survival"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Creature Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Religion/Philosophy Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"Herbalism"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Service"}]},"_id":"Nky1JVIxSrRZiMOK"}
{"name":"Kekhavra","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"kekhavra","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Kekhavra"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"kekhavra","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Sorcerer","document":{"name":"Kekhavra","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"kekhavra","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Kekhavra"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Kekhavra","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Kekhavra</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong> propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle réunit les fonctions de guérisseuse, herboriste, devineresse et guide spirituelle du clan.</p>\n<p>La tradition s’inscrit dans l’organisation matrilinéaire des Variags. Chaque clan possède normalement une Kekhavra, assistée de femmes plus jeunes qui apprennent auprès d’elle. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient guérison, pratique rituelle et autorité.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, environnement, herbes, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Recherche et compétences sociales.</p>\n<h2>Autorité spirituelle du clan</h2>\n<p>Elle conseille, interprète, soigne et participe à la transmission de la tradition. Son autorité touche maladies, blessures, naissances, morts, décisions de déplacement, conflits internes, cérémonies et présages. Plusieurs apprenties peuvent exister, mais une seule femme porte pleinement le rôle dans le clan.</p>\n<h2>Guérison et herbes</h2>\n<p>Quatre Listes traitent sang, os, traumatismes et muscles. <strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law)</strong> renforce le savoir des plantes dans les terres semi-arides de Khand. Elle reste une guérisseuse capable d’agir sans magie grâce à ses connaissances pratiques.</p>\n<h2>Divination et conseil</h2>\n<p><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways)</strong> permet d’obtenir des informations par divination et communion. La Kekhavra peut chercher un danger, interpréter un signe ou conseiller le clan avant une décision importante.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite hémorragies et blessures liées au sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies des Traumatismes (Concussion’s Ways).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et pertes de points de vie.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Répare les lésions musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law).</strong> Renforce et exploite les propriétés des herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion communautaire.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Les Variags et l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>La culture variag vit sous une forte influence de Mordor. Une Kekhavra peut être fidèle à ses traditions, protectrice pragmatique du clan, consciente de la nature de l’Ombre ou dissidente. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement de la Sorcellerie ; l’apprentissage de pratiques maléfiques ou de pouvoirs liés à Sauron déclenche les règles de Corruption appropriées.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra n’a pas d’équivalent standard complet dans RMU. MERP-RMU la construit comme <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong>, restriction aux femmes variags et six Listes historiques.</p>\n<p>Les mécanismes modernes de guérison servent de socle lorsque possible, tandis que Loi des Herbes et Voies Communales conservent leur identité culturelle propre.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Kekhavra","system":{"description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Kekhavra</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong> propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle réunit les fonctions de guérisseuse, herboriste, devineresse et guide spirituelle du clan.</p>\n<p>La tradition s’inscrit dans l’organisation matrilinéaire des Variags. Chaque clan possède normalement une Kekhavra, assistée de femmes plus jeunes qui apprennent auprès d’elle. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient guérison, pratique rituelle et autorité.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, environnement, herbes, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Recherche et compétences sociales.</p>\n<h2>Autorité spirituelle du clan</h2>\n<p>Elle conseille, interprète, soigne et participe à la transmission de la tradition. Son autorité touche maladies, blessures, naissances, morts, décisions de déplacement, conflits internes, cérémonies et présages. Plusieurs apprenties peuvent exister, mais une seule femme porte pleinement le rôle dans le clan.</p>\n<h2>Guérison et herbes</h2>\n<p>Quatre Listes traitent sang, os, traumatismes et muscles. <strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law)</strong> renforce le savoir des plantes dans les terres semi-arides de Khand. Elle reste une guérisseuse capable d’agir sans magie grâce à ses connaissances pratiques.</p>\n<h2>Divination et conseil</h2>\n<p><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways)</strong> permet d’obtenir des informations par divination et communion. La Kekhavra peut chercher un danger, interpréter un signe ou conseiller le clan avant une décision importante.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite hémorragies et blessures liées au sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies des Traumatismes (Concussion’s Ways).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et pertes de points de vie.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Répare les lésions musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law).</strong> Renforce et exploite les propriétés des herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion communautaire.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Les Variags et l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>La culture variag vit sous une forte influence de Mordor. Une Kekhavra peut être fidèle à ses traditions, protectrice pragmatique du clan, consciente de la nature de l’Ombre ou dissidente. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement de la Sorcellerie ; l’apprentissage de pratiques maléfiques ou de pouvoirs liés à Sauron déclenche les règles de Corruption appropriées.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra n’a pas d’équivalent standard complet dans RMU. MERP-RMU la construit comme <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong>, restriction aux femmes variags et six Listes historiques.</p>\n<p>Les mécanismes modernes de guérison servent de socle lorsque possible, tandis que Loi des Herbes et Voies Communales conservent leur identité culturelle propre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Kekhavra","system":{"description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Kekhavra</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She combines the functions of healer, herbalist, diviner, and above all <strong>spiritual guide of the clan</strong>. Her magic acts upon wounded bodies, medicinal plants, and the gathering of information, while her authority extends far beyond healing.</p>\n<p>The tradition of the Kekhavrathí belongs to the matrilineal organization of Variag society. Each clan normally has <strong>one Kekhavra</strong>, assisted by several younger women who learn from her and may one day succeed her. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Presence that of Mentalism; Self Discipline supports the mastery required for healing and ritual practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, skills involving animals and the environment, magical and religious lore, Herbs, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Research, and social skills useful to a position of authority.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Authority of the Clan</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra advises, interprets, heals, and participates in the transmission of tradition. She may intervene in serious illness or injury, births and deaths, decisions concerning travel, internal disputes, religious ceremonies, omens, and choices relating to war or alliances.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p>A major part of her magic concerns repair of physical injury: bleeding, fractures, concussion, and muscular damage. The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices a highly specialized mental discipline; the <strong>Kekhavra</strong> integrates healing into a broader whole that includes herbs, environment, divination, and spiritual responsibility.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Remedies</h2>\n<p><strong>Herb Law</strong> allows the Kekhavra to enhance or exploit the properties of herbs. In the semi-arid lands of <strong>Khand</strong>, useful resources require deep experience to identify: plants against pain, stimulants, poisonous plants, remedies for riders, and preparations for travel.</p>\n<h2>Divination and Counsel</h2>\n<p><strong>Communal Ways</strong> complements her role with divination and information-gathering abilities. A Kekhavra may try to determine whether a journey exposes the clan to danger, interpret a proposal, recognize a significant sign, or illuminate a collective decision.</p>\n<h2>The Variags and the Shadow</h2>\n<p>The Variags live under strong influence from Mordor, and their religion gives a dominant place to a Dark Lord. This makes the Kekhavra particularly interesting: she may be faithful to the beliefs of her people, a pragmatic protector of the clan, a woman gradually awakening to the true nature of the Shadow, or a dissident seeking to preserve healing traditions while rejecting the darkest aspects of the cult.</p>\n<h2>Nomadic Life and Combat</h2>\n<p>The Variags are remarkable riders. A Kekhavra must travel on horseback, heal under rough conditions, recognize the resources of the land, prepare remedies quickly, and intervene after battle. She may know how to use a light blade, spear, bow, or mounted weapon.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Four injury-related Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Herbs.</strong> Herb Law, environmental skills, and remedies.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Adviser.</strong> Communal Ways, religion, and Influence.</p>\n<p><strong>Traditional Priestess.</strong> Divination, ritual, Influence, and knowledge of the cult.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident.</strong> Healing and traditional lore while distancing herself from Shadow-linked cults.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion’s Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has no equivalent standard Profession in RMU. We therefore build the adaptation upon the <strong>hybrid spell-user chassis</strong> while preserving the historical identity in full: Mentalism + Channeling, <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, restriction to Variag women, and the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For the RMU implementation, healing Lists use the corresponding modern mechanics where possible and are then reassigned as Kekhavra Base Lists. The adaptation chiefly develops the cultural dimension: healer, herbalist, priestess, adviser, and keeper of clan tradition.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"kekhavra","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Kekhavra","system":{"description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Kekhavra</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong> propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle réunit les fonctions de guérisseuse, herboriste, devineresse et guide spirituelle du clan.</p>\n<p>La tradition s’inscrit dans l’organisation matrilinéaire des Variags. Chaque clan possède normalement une Kekhavra, assistée de femmes plus jeunes qui apprennent auprès d’elle. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient guérison, pratique rituelle et autorité.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, environnement, herbes, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, Recherche et compétences sociales.</p>\n<h2>Autorité spirituelle du clan</h2>\n<p>Elle conseille, interprète, soigne et participe à la transmission de la tradition. Son autorité touche maladies, blessures, naissances, morts, décisions de déplacement, conflits internes, cérémonies et présages. Plusieurs apprenties peuvent exister, mais une seule femme porte pleinement le rôle dans le clan.</p>\n<h2>Guérison et herbes</h2>\n<p>Quatre Listes traitent sang, os, traumatismes et muscles. <strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law)</strong> renforce le savoir des plantes dans les terres semi-arides de Khand. Elle reste une guérisseuse capable d’agir sans magie grâce à ses connaissances pratiques.</p>\n<h2>Divination et conseil</h2>\n<p><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways)</strong> permet d’obtenir des informations par divination et communion. La Kekhavra peut chercher un danger, interpréter un signe ou conseiller le clan avant une décision importante.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite hémorragies et blessures liées au sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies des Traumatismes (Concussion’s Ways).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et pertes de points de vie.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Répare les lésions musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Herbes (Herb Law).</strong> Renforce et exploite les propriétés des herbes.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion communautaire.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Les Variags et l’Ombre</h2>\n<p>La culture variag vit sous une forte influence de Mordor. Une Kekhavra peut être fidèle à ses traditions, protectrice pragmatique du clan, consciente de la nature de l’Ombre ou dissidente. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement de la Sorcellerie ; l’apprentissage de pratiques maléfiques ou de pouvoirs liés à Sauron déclenche les règles de Corruption appropriées.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Kekhavra n’a pas d’équivalent standard complet dans RMU. MERP-RMU la construit comme <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong>, restriction aux femmes variags et six Listes historiques.</p>\n<p>Les mécanismes modernes de guérison servent de socle lorsque possible, tandis que Loi des Herbes et Voies Communales conservent leur identité culturelle propre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Kekhavra","system":{"description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Kekhavra</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She combines the functions of healer, herbalist, diviner, and above all <strong>spiritual guide of the clan</strong>. Her magic acts upon wounded bodies, medicinal plants, and the gathering of information, while her authority extends far beyond healing.</p>\n<p>The tradition of the Kekhavrathí belongs to the matrilineal organization of Variag society. Each clan normally has <strong>one Kekhavra</strong>, assisted by several younger women who learn from her and may one day succeed her. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Presence that of Mentalism; Self Discipline supports the mastery required for healing and ritual practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, skills involving animals and the environment, magical and religious lore, Herbs, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Research, and social skills useful to a position of authority.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Authority of the Clan</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra advises, interprets, heals, and participates in the transmission of tradition. She may intervene in serious illness or injury, births and deaths, decisions concerning travel, internal disputes, religious ceremonies, omens, and choices relating to war or alliances.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p>A major part of her magic concerns repair of physical injury: bleeding, fractures, concussion, and muscular damage. The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices a highly specialized mental discipline; the <strong>Kekhavra</strong> integrates healing into a broader whole that includes herbs, environment, divination, and spiritual responsibility.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Remedies</h2>\n<p><strong>Herb Law</strong> allows the Kekhavra to enhance or exploit the properties of herbs. In the semi-arid lands of <strong>Khand</strong>, useful resources require deep experience to identify: plants against pain, stimulants, poisonous plants, remedies for riders, and preparations for travel.</p>\n<h2>Divination and Counsel</h2>\n<p><strong>Communal Ways</strong> complements her role with divination and information-gathering abilities. A Kekhavra may try to determine whether a journey exposes the clan to danger, interpret a proposal, recognize a significant sign, or illuminate a collective decision.</p>\n<h2>The Variags and the Shadow</h2>\n<p>The Variags live under strong influence from Mordor, and their religion gives a dominant place to a Dark Lord. This makes the Kekhavra particularly interesting: she may be faithful to the beliefs of her people, a pragmatic protector of the clan, a woman gradually awakening to the true nature of the Shadow, or a dissident seeking to preserve healing traditions while rejecting the darkest aspects of the cult.</p>\n<h2>Nomadic Life and Combat</h2>\n<p>The Variags are remarkable riders. A Kekhavra must travel on horseback, heal under rough conditions, recognize the resources of the land, prepare remedies quickly, and intervene after battle. She may know how to use a light blade, spear, bow, or mounted weapon.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Four injury-related Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Herbs.</strong> Herb Law, environmental skills, and remedies.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Adviser.</strong> Communal Ways, religion, and Influence.</p>\n<p><strong>Traditional Priestess.</strong> Divination, ritual, Influence, and knowledge of the cult.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident.</strong> Healing and traditional lore while distancing herself from Shadow-linked cults.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion’s Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has no equivalent standard Profession in RMU. We therefore build the adaptation upon the <strong>hybrid spell-user chassis</strong> while preserving the historical identity in full: Mentalism + Channeling, <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, restriction to Variag women, and the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For the RMU implementation, healing Lists use the corresponding modern mechanics where possible and are then reassigned as Kekhavra Base Lists. The adaptation chiefly develops the cultural dimension: healer, herbalist, priestess, adviser, and keeper of clan tradition.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Hybrides","Théurgie + Mentalisme"],"en":["Hybrid Spell-user Professions","Channeling + Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-hybrides","professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Kekhavra","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Kekhavra</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Kekhavra</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She combines the functions of healer, herbalist, diviner, and above all <strong>spiritual guide of the clan</strong>. Her magic acts upon wounded bodies, medicinal plants, and the gathering of information, while her authority extends far beyond healing.</p>\n<p>The tradition of the Kekhavrathí belongs to the matrilineal organization of Variag society. Each clan normally has <strong>one Kekhavra</strong>, assisted by several younger women who learn from her and may one day succeed her. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Presence that of Mentalism; Self Discipline supports the mastery required for healing and ritual practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, skills involving animals and the environment, magical and religious lore, Herbs, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, Research, and social skills useful to a position of authority.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Authority of the Clan</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra advises, interprets, heals, and participates in the transmission of tradition. She may intervene in serious illness or injury, births and deaths, decisions concerning travel, internal disputes, religious ceremonies, omens, and choices relating to war or alliances.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p>A major part of her magic concerns repair of physical injury: bleeding, fractures, concussion, and muscular damage. The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices a highly specialized mental discipline; the <strong>Kekhavra</strong> integrates healing into a broader whole that includes herbs, environment, divination, and spiritual responsibility.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Remedies</h2>\n<p><strong>Herb Law</strong> allows the Kekhavra to enhance or exploit the properties of herbs. In the semi-arid lands of <strong>Khand</strong>, useful resources require deep experience to identify: plants against pain, stimulants, poisonous plants, remedies for riders, and preparations for travel.</p>\n<h2>Divination and Counsel</h2>\n<p><strong>Communal Ways</strong> complements her role with divination and information-gathering abilities. A Kekhavra may try to determine whether a journey exposes the clan to danger, interpret a proposal, recognize a significant sign, or illuminate a collective decision.</p>\n<h2>The Variags and the Shadow</h2>\n<p>The Variags live under strong influence from Mordor, and their religion gives a dominant place to a Dark Lord. This makes the Kekhavra particularly interesting: she may be faithful to the beliefs of her people, a pragmatic protector of the clan, a woman gradually awakening to the true nature of the Shadow, or a dissident seeking to preserve healing traditions while rejecting the darkest aspects of the cult.</p>\n<h2>Nomadic Life and Combat</h2>\n<p>The Variags are remarkable riders. A Kekhavra must travel on horseback, heal under rough conditions, recognize the resources of the land, prepare remedies quickly, and intervene after battle. She may know how to use a light blade, spear, bow, or mounted weapon.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Clan Healer.</strong> Four injury-related Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Herbs.</strong> Herb Law, environmental skills, and remedies.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Adviser.</strong> Communal Ways, religion, and Influence.</p>\n<p><strong>Traditional Priestess.</strong> Divination, ritual, Influence, and knowledge of the cult.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident.</strong> Healing and traditional lore while distancing herself from Shadow-linked cults.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion’s Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herb Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Kekhavra has no equivalent standard Profession in RMU. We therefore build the adaptation upon the <strong>hybrid spell-user chassis</strong> while preserving the historical identity in full: Mentalism + Channeling, <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, restriction to Variag women, and the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For the RMU implementation, healing Lists use the corresponding modern mechanics where possible and are then reassigned as Kekhavra Base Lists. The adaptation chiefly develops the cultural dimension: healer, herbalist, priestess, adviser, and keeper of clan tradition.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"bHnBool4FvAfjKkA"}
{"name":"Lay Healer","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"lay-healer","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"RMU Core Law — Lay Healer; MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Lay Healer"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"lay-healer","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Lay Healer","document":{"name":"Soigneur","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"lay-healer","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"RMU Core Law — Lay Healer; MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Lay Healer"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Soigneur","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":5,"realms":"Mentalism","description":"<h1>Soigneur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Soigneur</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la guérison directe du corps. Sa magie agit sur les blessures, fractures, muscles, nerfs, organes et pertes de sang. Elle repose sur une compréhension intime du fonctionnement du corps vivant et sur la capacité à diriger la puissance mentale vers la réparation.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession correspond à un praticien rare. Un Soigneur du Gondor peut avoir reçu une formation dans une maison de soin ; un Dúnadan d’Arnor conserver des connaissances héritées de Númenor ; un praticien dorwinadan associer Mentalisme et pharmacopée locale.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. La Présence est aussi la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient concentration, précision et contrôle.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un <strong>pur utilisateur de Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, médecine, anatomie, Premiers soins, Perception, concentration, Manipulation de la Puissance et Développement des Points de Puissance. La compétence médicale réelle complète la guérison magique.</p>\n<h2>Guérison directe</h2>\n<p>Sa magie traite directement hémorragies, fractures, traumatismes, lésions musculaires, nerveuses et organiques. Elle permet de réduire des séquelles, accélérer des réparations et stabiliser des blessures graves.</p>\n<h2>Limites de la guérison</h2>\n<p>Maladies, poisons, malédictions et atteintes spirituelles relèvent d’autres domaines. Une blessure de Morgul, par exemple, touche progressivement corps et esprit et dépasse la simple réparation physique.</p>\n<h2>Place dans les communautés</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, un Soigneur peut servir dans une maison de soin ou auprès d’une garnison ; chez les Dúnedain du Nord, accompagner des patrouilles ; au Rohan, traiter les blessures de cavaliers et les traumatismes liés aux chutes de cheval.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise du Sang (Blood Mastery).</strong> Contrôle les hémorragies et restaure les pertes sanguines.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Os (Bone Mastery).</strong> Répare fractures et lésions du squelette.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Traumatismes (Concussion Mastery).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et commotions.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Muscles (Muscle Mastery).</strong> Répare lésions et déchirures musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Nerfs et des Organes (Nerve And Organ Mastery).</strong> Traite lésions nerveuses et atteintes des organes internes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prothèses et techniques avancées</h2>\n<p>RMU ajoute <strong>Prothèses (Prosthetics)</strong>. MERP-RMU la maintient hors des Listes de base normales afin de préserver le ton de la Terre du Milieu. Une telle technique peut exister comme savoir rarissime ou projet unique associant guérisseur et artisan exceptionnel.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Lay Healer RMU</strong>, converti sous le nom Soigneur, avec <strong>Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU retient cinq Listes de base historiques et laisse Prothèses hors progression standard. La rareté de la guérison magique et l’importance des connaissances médicales ordinaires restent centrales.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Soigneur","system":{"description":"<h1>Soigneur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Soigneur</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la guérison directe du corps. Sa magie agit sur les blessures, fractures, muscles, nerfs, organes et pertes de sang. Elle repose sur une compréhension intime du fonctionnement du corps vivant et sur la capacité à diriger la puissance mentale vers la réparation.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession correspond à un praticien rare. Un Soigneur du Gondor peut avoir reçu une formation dans une maison de soin ; un Dúnadan d’Arnor conserver des connaissances héritées de Númenor ; un praticien dorwinadan associer Mentalisme et pharmacopée locale.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. La Présence est aussi la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient concentration, précision et contrôle.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un <strong>pur utilisateur de Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, médecine, anatomie, Premiers soins, Perception, concentration, Manipulation de la Puissance et Développement des Points de Puissance. La compétence médicale réelle complète la guérison magique.</p>\n<h2>Guérison directe</h2>\n<p>Sa magie traite directement hémorragies, fractures, traumatismes, lésions musculaires, nerveuses et organiques. Elle permet de réduire des séquelles, accélérer des réparations et stabiliser des blessures graves.</p>\n<h2>Limites de la guérison</h2>\n<p>Maladies, poisons, malédictions et atteintes spirituelles relèvent d’autres domaines. Une blessure de Morgul, par exemple, touche progressivement corps et esprit et dépasse la simple réparation physique.</p>\n<h2>Place dans les communautés</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, un Soigneur peut servir dans une maison de soin ou auprès d’une garnison ; chez les Dúnedain du Nord, accompagner des patrouilles ; au Rohan, traiter les blessures de cavaliers et les traumatismes liés aux chutes de cheval.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise du Sang (Blood Mastery).</strong> Contrôle les hémorragies et restaure les pertes sanguines.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Os (Bone Mastery).</strong> Répare fractures et lésions du squelette.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Traumatismes (Concussion Mastery).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et commotions.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Muscles (Muscle Mastery).</strong> Répare lésions et déchirures musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Nerfs et des Organes (Nerve And Organ Mastery).</strong> Traite lésions nerveuses et atteintes des organes internes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prothèses et techniques avancées</h2>\n<p>RMU ajoute <strong>Prothèses (Prosthetics)</strong>. MERP-RMU la maintient hors des Listes de base normales afin de préserver le ton de la Terre du Milieu. Une telle technique peut exister comme savoir rarissime ou projet unique associant guérisseur et artisan exceptionnel.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Lay Healer RMU</strong>, converti sous le nom Soigneur, avec <strong>Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU retient cinq Listes de base historiques et laisse Prothèses hors progression standard. La rareté de la guérison magique et l’importance des connaissances médicales ordinaires restent centrales.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Lay Healer","system":{"description":"<h1>Lay Healer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in direct healing of the body. The Profession’s magic acts upon wounds, fractures, muscles, nerves, organs, and blood loss. It rests on intimate understanding of the living body and the ability to direct mental power toward repair.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Lay Healer already occupied this place as a supernatural medical specialist. The Profession works chiefly upon <strong>specific physical trauma</strong>: broken bones, torn muscles, haemorrhage, and damage to nerves or organs.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, this is a rare practitioner. A Lay Healer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may have trained in a house of healing; a <strong>Dúnadan of Arnor</strong> may preserve knowledge inherited from Númenor; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> practitioner may combine Mentalism with the local pharmacopoeia.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong> and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism; Self Discipline reflects the concentration and precision needed to work upon delicate living structures.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, medical skills, Anatomy, First Aid, Perception, concentration, Power Manipulation, and Power Point Development.</p>\n<p>A capable Lay Healer knows how to examine a wound, judge its severity, and use ordinary medical treatment when magic is unnecessary or unavailable.</p>\n<h2>Direct Healing</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s magic can stop bleeding, mend bone, restore damaged muscle, treat concussion and trauma, repair certain nerves, and restore injured organs.</p>\n<p>A badly wounded Fighter may survive because of rapid intervention. A fracture that would immobilize a traveller for weeks may be stabilized or repaired far more quickly.</p>\n<h2>Limits of Healing</h2>\n<p>Diseases, poisons, curses, and spiritual injuries belong to other fields. A Lay Healer can recognize symptoms through medical knowledge, but direct magical treatment depends on the Lists available. A wound caused by an influence of the Shadow, such as a Morgul blade, requires more than simple physical repair.</p>\n<h2>Medicine and Observation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer must <strong>diagnose before acting</strong>. Pain may arise from trauma, disease, or poison; weakness of a limb may result from muscular, nervous, or skeletal injury. A Lay Healer of <strong>Minas Tirith</strong> may have studied in a learned environment; a wandering healer of <strong>Eriador</strong> may have developed an empirical approach grounded in experience.</p>\n<h2>Mentalism and Discretion</h2>\n<p>The magic may appear very subtle: laying hands upon a wound, examining the patient, slowing the breath, and concentrating power upon damaged tissues. Such discretion is especially appropriate to Middle-earth.</p>\n<h2>Place in the Community</h2>\n<p>In Gondor, a Lay Healer may serve in a house of healing, a garrison, or a great household. Among the Dúnedain of the North, the healer may accompany patrols. In a trading city such as Pelargir, the Profession may care for wounded sailors, soldiers, and travellers. Among the Rohirrim, a Lay Healer may be particularly valuable in treating injuries from falls from horseback.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Mystic Surgeon.</strong> Anatomy, First Aid, and Lists devoted to precise repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Battlefield Healer.</strong> Rapid intervention during or immediately after combat.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Physician.</strong> Magical healing, medicine, regional lore, and travel skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Court Healer.</strong> Social and medical abilities in the service of an important authority.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, the Lay Healer has <strong>five Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve And Organ Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Modern RMU also adds <strong>Prosthetics</strong>. We keep this outside the normal MERP-RMU Lay Healer progression in order to preserve the tone of Middle-earth. Such a technique may exist as exceedingly rare lore or as an exceptional project involving a master craftsman as well.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Ordinary Care</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer benefits greatly from bandages, splints, medicinal plants, sutures, rest, and immobilization. A Dorwinadan healer may use medicinal oils and wines; a Dúnadan remedies inherited from Númenórean traditions; a Lay Healer working with an Animist may gain access to herbs not covered by the Profession’s own training.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>RMU Lay Healer chassis</strong>, a pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in repair of the body. We explicitly restore <strong>Presence and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The principal adaptation concerns the Lists: modern RMU provides six with Prosthetics, while MERP-RMU retains the five historical healing traditions as Base Spell Lists. The adaptation also emphasizes the rarity of magical healing, the importance of ordinary medical knowledge, and cooperation with herbalists and Animists.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"lay-healer","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Soigneur","system":{"description":"<h1>Soigneur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Soigneur</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la guérison directe du corps. Sa magie agit sur les blessures, fractures, muscles, nerfs, organes et pertes de sang. Elle repose sur une compréhension intime du fonctionnement du corps vivant et sur la capacité à diriger la puissance mentale vers la réparation.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession correspond à un praticien rare. Un Soigneur du Gondor peut avoir reçu une formation dans une maison de soin ; un Dúnadan d’Arnor conserver des connaissances héritées de Númenor ; un praticien dorwinadan associer Mentalisme et pharmacopée locale.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. La Présence est aussi la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme ; l’Autodiscipline soutient concentration, précision et contrôle.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un <strong>pur utilisateur de Mentalisme</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, médecine, anatomie, Premiers soins, Perception, concentration, Manipulation de la Puissance et Développement des Points de Puissance. La compétence médicale réelle complète la guérison magique.</p>\n<h2>Guérison directe</h2>\n<p>Sa magie traite directement hémorragies, fractures, traumatismes, lésions musculaires, nerveuses et organiques. Elle permet de réduire des séquelles, accélérer des réparations et stabiliser des blessures graves.</p>\n<h2>Limites de la guérison</h2>\n<p>Maladies, poisons, malédictions et atteintes spirituelles relèvent d’autres domaines. Une blessure de Morgul, par exemple, touche progressivement corps et esprit et dépasse la simple réparation physique.</p>\n<h2>Place dans les communautés</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, un Soigneur peut servir dans une maison de soin ou auprès d’une garnison ; chez les Dúnedain du Nord, accompagner des patrouilles ; au Rohan, traiter les blessures de cavaliers et les traumatismes liés aux chutes de cheval.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise du Sang (Blood Mastery).</strong> Contrôle les hémorragies et restaure les pertes sanguines.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Os (Bone Mastery).</strong> Répare fractures et lésions du squelette.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Traumatismes (Concussion Mastery).</strong> Traite traumatismes généraux et commotions.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Muscles (Muscle Mastery).</strong> Répare lésions et déchirures musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Nerfs et des Organes (Nerve And Organ Mastery).</strong> Traite lésions nerveuses et atteintes des organes internes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prothèses et techniques avancées</h2>\n<p>RMU ajoute <strong>Prothèses (Prosthetics)</strong>. MERP-RMU la maintient hors des Listes de base normales afin de préserver le ton de la Terre du Milieu. Une telle technique peut exister comme savoir rarissime ou projet unique associant guérisseur et artisan exceptionnel.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Lay Healer RMU</strong>, converti sous le nom Soigneur, avec <strong>Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU retient cinq Listes de base historiques et laisse Prothèses hors progression standard. La rareté de la guérison magique et l’importance des connaissances médicales ordinaires restent centrales.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Lay Healer","system":{"description":"<h1>Lay Healer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in direct healing of the body. The Profession’s magic acts upon wounds, fractures, muscles, nerves, organs, and blood loss. It rests on intimate understanding of the living body and the ability to direct mental power toward repair.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Lay Healer already occupied this place as a supernatural medical specialist. The Profession works chiefly upon <strong>specific physical trauma</strong>: broken bones, torn muscles, haemorrhage, and damage to nerves or organs.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, this is a rare practitioner. A Lay Healer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may have trained in a house of healing; a <strong>Dúnadan of Arnor</strong> may preserve knowledge inherited from Númenor; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> practitioner may combine Mentalism with the local pharmacopoeia.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong> and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism; Self Discipline reflects the concentration and precision needed to work upon delicate living structures.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, medical skills, Anatomy, First Aid, Perception, concentration, Power Manipulation, and Power Point Development.</p>\n<p>A capable Lay Healer knows how to examine a wound, judge its severity, and use ordinary medical treatment when magic is unnecessary or unavailable.</p>\n<h2>Direct Healing</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s magic can stop bleeding, mend bone, restore damaged muscle, treat concussion and trauma, repair certain nerves, and restore injured organs.</p>\n<p>A badly wounded Fighter may survive because of rapid intervention. A fracture that would immobilize a traveller for weeks may be stabilized or repaired far more quickly.</p>\n<h2>Limits of Healing</h2>\n<p>Diseases, poisons, curses, and spiritual injuries belong to other fields. A Lay Healer can recognize symptoms through medical knowledge, but direct magical treatment depends on the Lists available. A wound caused by an influence of the Shadow, such as a Morgul blade, requires more than simple physical repair.</p>\n<h2>Medicine and Observation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer must <strong>diagnose before acting</strong>. Pain may arise from trauma, disease, or poison; weakness of a limb may result from muscular, nervous, or skeletal injury. A Lay Healer of <strong>Minas Tirith</strong> may have studied in a learned environment; a wandering healer of <strong>Eriador</strong> may have developed an empirical approach grounded in experience.</p>\n<h2>Mentalism and Discretion</h2>\n<p>The magic may appear very subtle: laying hands upon a wound, examining the patient, slowing the breath, and concentrating power upon damaged tissues. Such discretion is especially appropriate to Middle-earth.</p>\n<h2>Place in the Community</h2>\n<p>In Gondor, a Lay Healer may serve in a house of healing, a garrison, or a great household. Among the Dúnedain of the North, the healer may accompany patrols. In a trading city such as Pelargir, the Profession may care for wounded sailors, soldiers, and travellers. Among the Rohirrim, a Lay Healer may be particularly valuable in treating injuries from falls from horseback.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Mystic Surgeon.</strong> Anatomy, First Aid, and Lists devoted to precise repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Battlefield Healer.</strong> Rapid intervention during or immediately after combat.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Physician.</strong> Magical healing, medicine, regional lore, and travel skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Court Healer.</strong> Social and medical abilities in the service of an important authority.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, the Lay Healer has <strong>five Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve And Organ Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Modern RMU also adds <strong>Prosthetics</strong>. We keep this outside the normal MERP-RMU Lay Healer progression in order to preserve the tone of Middle-earth. Such a technique may exist as exceedingly rare lore or as an exceptional project involving a master craftsman as well.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Ordinary Care</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer benefits greatly from bandages, splints, medicinal plants, sutures, rest, and immobilization. A Dorwinadan healer may use medicinal oils and wines; a Dúnadan remedies inherited from Númenórean traditions; a Lay Healer working with an Animist may gain access to herbs not covered by the Profession’s own training.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>RMU Lay Healer chassis</strong>, a pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in repair of the body. We explicitly restore <strong>Presence and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The principal adaptation concerns the Lists: modern RMU provides six with Prosthetics, while MERP-RMU retains the five historical healing traditions as Base Spell Lists. The adaptation also emphasizes the rarity of magical healing, the importance of ordinary medical knowledge, and cooperation with herbalists and Animists.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Mentalisme"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Soigneur","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":5,"realms":"Mentalism","description":"<h1>Lay Healer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in direct healing of the body. The Profession’s magic acts upon wounds, fractures, muscles, nerves, organs, and blood loss. It rests on intimate understanding of the living body and the ability to direct mental power toward repair.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Lay Healer already occupied this place as a supernatural medical specialist. The Profession works chiefly upon <strong>specific physical trauma</strong>: broken bones, torn muscles, haemorrhage, and damage to nerves or organs.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, this is a rare practitioner. A Lay Healer of <strong>Gondor</strong> may have trained in a house of healing; a <strong>Dúnadan of Arnor</strong> may preserve knowledge inherited from Númenor; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> practitioner may combine Mentalism with the local pharmacopoeia.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong> and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism; Self Discipline reflects the concentration and precision needed to work upon delicate living structures.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, medical skills, Anatomy, First Aid, Perception, concentration, Power Manipulation, and Power Point Development.</p>\n<p>A capable Lay Healer knows how to examine a wound, judge its severity, and use ordinary medical treatment when magic is unnecessary or unavailable.</p>\n<h2>Direct Healing</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer’s magic can stop bleeding, mend bone, restore damaged muscle, treat concussion and trauma, repair certain nerves, and restore injured organs.</p>\n<p>A badly wounded Fighter may survive because of rapid intervention. A fracture that would immobilize a traveller for weeks may be stabilized or repaired far more quickly.</p>\n<h2>Limits of Healing</h2>\n<p>Diseases, poisons, curses, and spiritual injuries belong to other fields. A Lay Healer can recognize symptoms through medical knowledge, but direct magical treatment depends on the Lists available. A wound caused by an influence of the Shadow, such as a Morgul blade, requires more than simple physical repair.</p>\n<h2>Medicine and Observation</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer must <strong>diagnose before acting</strong>. Pain may arise from trauma, disease, or poison; weakness of a limb may result from muscular, nervous, or skeletal injury. A Lay Healer of <strong>Minas Tirith</strong> may have studied in a learned environment; a wandering healer of <strong>Eriador</strong> may have developed an empirical approach grounded in experience.</p>\n<h2>Mentalism and Discretion</h2>\n<p>The magic may appear very subtle: laying hands upon a wound, examining the patient, slowing the breath, and concentrating power upon damaged tissues. Such discretion is especially appropriate to Middle-earth.</p>\n<h2>Place in the Community</h2>\n<p>In Gondor, a Lay Healer may serve in a house of healing, a garrison, or a great household. Among the Dúnedain of the North, the healer may accompany patrols. In a trading city such as Pelargir, the Profession may care for wounded sailors, soldiers, and travellers. Among the Rohirrim, a Lay Healer may be particularly valuable in treating injuries from falls from horseback.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Mystic Surgeon.</strong> Anatomy, First Aid, and Lists devoted to precise repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Battlefield Healer.</strong> Rapid intervention during or immediately after combat.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Physician.</strong> Magical healing, medicine, regional lore, and travel skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Court Healer.</strong> Social and medical abilities in the service of an important authority.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, the Lay Healer has <strong>five Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Concussion Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Mastery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve And Organ Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Modern RMU also adds <strong>Prosthetics</strong>. We keep this outside the normal MERP-RMU Lay Healer progression in order to preserve the tone of Middle-earth. Such a technique may exist as exceedingly rare lore or as an exceptional project involving a master craftsman as well.</p>\n<h2>Herbs and Ordinary Care</h2>\n<p>The Lay Healer benefits greatly from bandages, splints, medicinal plants, sutures, rest, and immobilization. A Dorwinadan healer may use medicinal oils and wines; a Dúnadan remedies inherited from Númenórean traditions; a Lay Healer working with an Animist may gain access to herbs not covered by the Profession’s own training.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>RMU Lay Healer chassis</strong>, a pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in repair of the body. We explicitly restore <strong>Presence and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>The principal adaptation concerns the Lists: modern RMU provides six with Prosthetics, while MERP-RMU retains the five historical healing traditions as Base Spell Lists. The adaptation also emphasizes the rarity of magical healing, the importance of ordinary medical knowledge, and cooperation with herbalists and Animists.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"XnaBdc8p2ajlt9Ms"}
{"name":"Layman","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"layman","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Layman; RMU-compatible adaptation"},"compendiumSourceKey":"layman","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-non-magiques","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Sans Profession","system":{"description":"<h1>Sans Profession</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Sans Profession</strong> représente un personnage qui n’a pas suivi de formation professionnelle structurée comparable à celle d’un Guerrier, d’un Voleur, d’un Magicien ou d’un Rôdeur. Son apprentissage vient surtout de son milieu, de sa famille, de son métier et des circonstances de son existence.</p>\n<p>Cela n’implique ni inexpérience ni incapacité. Un fermier peut parfaitement connaître les animaux et les cultures ; un artisan maîtriser son métier ; un marchand organiser des voyages et négocier ; un serviteur connaître les usages de cour. La différence vient de l’absence d’une spécialisation professionnelle d’aventurier.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Le Sans Profession ne possède <strong>aucune caractéristique primordiale</strong>. Aucun astérisque ne doit apparaître lors de la génération. Le joueur choisit librement les caractéristiques correspondant à l’histoire du personnage.</p>\n<p>Un forgeron khazâd pourra privilégier Force et Constitution ; un marchand dorwinadan Présence, Mémoire ou Raisonnement ; un érudit de Minas Tirith Mémoire et Raisonnement ; un berger rohir Constitution, Intuition et Agilité.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Sans Profession est un <strong>non-utilisateur de sorts</strong>. Ses coûts sont plus généralistes : artisanat, compétences sociales, connaissances, métiers, aptitudes physiques, armes, Perception, animaux ou environnement peuvent tous être développés sans que la Profession définisse une vocation dominante.</p>\n<h2>Culture et profession</h2>\n<p>La Culture joue ici un rôle particulièrement fort. Un jeune Rohir peut conserver une forte identité de cavalier ; un Hobbit Pied-velu rester défini par ses compétences rurales et sa discrétion ; un habitant de Pelargir élevé parmi les bateliers développer navigation, commerce et connaissance des quais.</p>\n<h2>Métiers et vie quotidienne</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artisan.</strong> Forgeron, charpentier, tailleur, potier, tanneur ou bâtisseur.</li>\n<li><strong>Marchand.</strong> Influence, Commerce, Évaluation, langues et connaissances régionales.</li>\n<li><strong>Paysan ou éleveur.</strong> Animaux, climat, endurance et métiers agricoles.</li>\n<li><strong>Marin ou batelier.</strong> Navigation, météo, entretien des embarcations et commerce.</li>\n<li><strong>Serviteur ou intendant.</strong> Organisation, compétences sociales et connaissance des usages.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>Le Sans Profession peut savoir se battre par nécessité ou tradition culturelle. Un paysan d’Eriador peut utiliser lance ou arc ; un marin de Pelargir une lame courte ; un berger rohir une lance à cheval. Il développe toutefois moins facilement plusieurs styles qu’un combattant professionnel.</p>\n<h2>Un personnage qui évolue</h2>\n<p>Cette Profession convient particulièrement à quelqu’un dont l’histoire commence avant la vocation d’aventurier : fermier devenu combattant, marchand apprenant la survie, serviteur devenant espion, érudit découvrant la magie. Elle permet de raconter une évolution progressive et très naturelle dans la Terre du Milieu.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p><strong>Aucune.</strong> Aucun royaume magique n’est privilégié par la Profession.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons de RMU une Profession généraliste sans spécialisation marquée. MERP-RMU maintient surtout l’absence complète de caractéristiques primordiales et insiste sur la Culture, le métier et l’histoire individuelle.</p>\n<p>Le Sans Profession n’est pas un personnage vide : marchand dorwinadan, berger rohir, artisan khazâd, marin de Pelargir, fermier de Bree ou érudit de Minas Tirith peuvent tous posséder une identité très forte avant même que l’aventure commence.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Layman","system":{"description":"<h1>Layman</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Layman</strong> represents a character who has not followed a structured professional training comparable to that of a Fighter, Thief, Magician, or Ranger. The Profession has no predefined field of excellence and no strong orientation toward any particular group of skills. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, this profile describes someone whose learning was shaped by family, trade, surroundings, and the circumstances of ordinary life rather than by the formal training of an adventuring profession.</p>\n<p>A farmer may know animals and crops intimately; a craftsman may master a trade; a merchant may negotiate, appraise goods, and organize long journeys; a servant attached to a noble household may know courtly customs, great families, and the secrets of a master. The Layman is therefore not an unskilled character, but one whose expertise arises from <strong>life experience rather than a professional adventuring discipline</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Layman has <strong>no Prime Stats</strong>.</p>\n<p>This absence is fundamental to the Profession. No characteristic is structurally privileged by training: the character’s strengths come from Culture, personal choices, trade, and history.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Layman is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong> and has no professional Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<p>Development is deliberately broad. The character can invest in social, technical, outdoor, scholarly, craft, and practical skills according to background. Combat and magic are possible, but neither receives the favourable professional orientation of a dedicated Fighter or spell-user.</p>\n<h2>Culture and Profession</h2>\n<p>For the Layman, <strong>Culture matters more than for almost any other Profession</strong>. Cultural training supplies the foundation on which the character’s practical identity is built. A Bree-land farmer, a Rohir shepherd, a Khazâd artisan, a Pelargir sailor, and a Minas Tirith scribe may all be Laymen while possessing radically different capabilities.</p>\n<h2>Trades and Everyday Life</h2>\n<p><strong>Farmer or Herder.</strong> Animal Handling, Survival, weather sense, local lore, and a practical weapon such as spear or bow.</p>\n<p><strong>Craftsman.</strong> A primary Craft skill, appraisal, trade contacts, and knowledge of materials and tools.</p>\n<p><strong>Merchant.</strong> Influence, appraisal, languages, route knowledge, organization, and perhaps riding or sailing.</p>\n<p><strong>Sailor.</strong> A sailor of Pelargir, Umbar, or Dorwinion may know navigation, weather, vessel maintenance, and trade.</p>\n<p><strong>Servant or Steward.</strong> Social skills, Organization, Influence, and knowledge of customs; in a noble house of Gondor, an experienced steward may possess considerable political knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>A Layman may learn to fight through necessity or cultural tradition. A farmer of Eriador may know spear or bow; a sailor of Pelargir a short blade; a Rohir shepherd a spear from horseback. Developing several combat styles or reaching very high martial expertise requires more investment than it would for a specialized Profession.</p>\n<h2>A Character Who Changes</h2>\n<p>The Layman is especially useful for representing the beginning of a journey: a farmer who becomes a fighter after the destruction of a village, a merchant who learns survival and weapons, a servant who becomes a spy, or a scholar who gradually discovers magic. The Profession allows play to tell the story of a person transformed into an adventurer by events.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Layman has no professional Base Spell Lists and no magical realm favoured by the Profession.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a <strong>generalist Profession</strong> with no strong specialization and no professional Base Spell Lists. The essential MERP element is the complete absence of <strong>Prime Stats</strong>.</p>\n<p>Our adaptation places greater emphasis on <strong>Culture</strong> and <strong>trade</strong>. A Layman is not an empty character: a Dorwinadan merchant, Rohir shepherd, Khazâd craftsman, Pelargir sailor, Bree farmer, or Minas Tirith scholar may possess a very strong identity. The difference is that these abilities were acquired through ordinary life rather than through a formal adventuring profession.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions non magiques"],"en":["Non-magical Professions"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-non-magiques"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP / RMSS — adaptation RMU","profession":"Layman","spellCastingGroup":"Non","numberOfBaseLists":0,"realms":"","description":"<h1>Layman</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Layman</strong> represents a character who has not followed a structured professional training comparable to that of a Fighter, Thief, Magician, or Ranger. The Profession has no predefined field of excellence and no strong orientation toward any particular group of skills. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, this profile describes someone whose learning was shaped by family, trade, surroundings, and the circumstances of ordinary life rather than by the formal training of an adventuring profession.</p>\n<p>A farmer may know animals and crops intimately; a craftsman may master a trade; a merchant may negotiate, appraise goods, and organize long journeys; a servant attached to a noble household may know courtly customs, great families, and the secrets of a master. The Layman is therefore not an unskilled character, but one whose expertise arises from <strong>life experience rather than a professional adventuring discipline</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Layman has <strong>no Prime Stats</strong>.</p>\n<p>This absence is fundamental to the Profession. No characteristic is structurally privileged by training: the character’s strengths come from Culture, personal choices, trade, and history.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Layman is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong> and has no professional Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<p>Development is deliberately broad. The character can invest in social, technical, outdoor, scholarly, craft, and practical skills according to background. Combat and magic are possible, but neither receives the favourable professional orientation of a dedicated Fighter or spell-user.</p>\n<h2>Culture and Profession</h2>\n<p>For the Layman, <strong>Culture matters more than for almost any other Profession</strong>. Cultural training supplies the foundation on which the character’s practical identity is built. A Bree-land farmer, a Rohir shepherd, a Khazâd artisan, a Pelargir sailor, and a Minas Tirith scribe may all be Laymen while possessing radically different capabilities.</p>\n<h2>Trades and Everyday Life</h2>\n<p><strong>Farmer or Herder.</strong> Animal Handling, Survival, weather sense, local lore, and a practical weapon such as spear or bow.</p>\n<p><strong>Craftsman.</strong> A primary Craft skill, appraisal, trade contacts, and knowledge of materials and tools.</p>\n<p><strong>Merchant.</strong> Influence, appraisal, languages, route knowledge, organization, and perhaps riding or sailing.</p>\n<p><strong>Sailor.</strong> A sailor of Pelargir, Umbar, or Dorwinion may know navigation, weather, vessel maintenance, and trade.</p>\n<p><strong>Servant or Steward.</strong> Social skills, Organization, Influence, and knowledge of customs; in a noble house of Gondor, an experienced steward may possess considerable political knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>A Layman may learn to fight through necessity or cultural tradition. A farmer of Eriador may know spear or bow; a sailor of Pelargir a short blade; a Rohir shepherd a spear from horseback. Developing several combat styles or reaching very high martial expertise requires more investment than it would for a specialized Profession.</p>\n<h2>A Character Who Changes</h2>\n<p>The Layman is especially useful for representing the beginning of a journey: a farmer who becomes a fighter after the destruction of a village, a merchant who learns survival and weapons, a servant who becomes a spy, or a scholar who gradually discovers magic. The Profession allows play to tell the story of a person transformed into an adventurer by events.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Layman has no professional Base Spell Lists and no magical realm favoured by the Profession.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a <strong>generalist Profession</strong> with no strong specialization and no professional Base Spell Lists. The essential MERP element is the complete absence of <strong>Prime Stats</strong>.</p>\n<p>Our adaptation places greater emphasis on <strong>Culture</strong> and <strong>trade</strong>. A Layman is not an empty character: a Dorwinadan merchant, Rohir shepherd, Khazâd craftsman, Pelargir sailor, Bree farmer, or Minas Tirith scholar may possess a very strong identity. The difference is that these abilities were acquired through ordinary life rather than through a formal adventuring profession.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"1/2"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"3/5"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"3/5"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"4/6"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"4/6"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Metalcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Region Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","skillName":"First Aid"},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Administration"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Trade"}]},"_id":"MwjXWOQniT04mq7j"}
{"name":"Magician","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"magician","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"fvtt-Item-magician-BiE9Oupa2nYTEee5.json"},"compendiumSourceKey":"magician","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-essence","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Magicien","system":{"description":"<h1>Magicien</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Magicien</strong> est un utilisateur pur de l’<strong>Essence</strong>, spécialisé dans la manipulation des forces élémentaires du monde. Sa magie agit sur la terre, le feu, la glace, la lumière, l’eau et le vent. Il transforme son environnement, crée des obstacles, contrôle des phénomènes physiques et peut produire des attaques élémentaires extrêmement dangereuses.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, le Magicien reste une figure rare. Un humain du Gondor peut avoir découvert des traditions anciennes dans des archives ; un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar peut employer l’Essence comme instrument de puissance ; un Noldo peut aborder certaines manipulations élémentaires comme l’expression d’un savoir très ancien sur la matière et les forces d’Arda.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales du Magicien sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong> et <strong>Raisonnement (Re)</strong>. L’Empathie est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de l’Essence ; le Raisonnement représente la compréhension intellectuelle nécessaire aux principes complexes de la magie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Magicien est un <strong>utilisateur pur d’Essence</strong>. Il privilégie Listes de base, Listes Ouvertes d’Essence, connaissances magiques, Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement de la Puissance, Perception de la Puissance et Sorts dirigés. Le combat physique et les armures lourdes restent secondaires.</p>\n<h2>Essence et manipulation du monde</h2>\n<p>L’Essence agit sur les structures du monde matériel. Le Magicien peut chauffer ou refroidir, créer des flammes, modifier terre ou pierre, déplacer air ou eau, manipuler lumière et électricité. Il est particulièrement efficace lorsqu’il comprend son environnement et choisit le bon élément pour répondre à la situation.</p>\n<h2>Magie offensive</h2>\n<p>Ses Listes comportent Éclairs élémentaires, Boules élémentaires, murs et zones dangereuses. Cette puissance exige de véritables compétences de Sorts dirigés et une bonne gestion des Points de Puissance.</p>\n<h2>Un savoir dangereux</h2>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, une magie trop visible peut attirer l’attention, provoquer la peur ou révéler le lanceur aux forces de l’Ombre. Un Magicien du Troisième Âge qui déchaîne feu ou lumière dans une région surveillée par les serviteurs de Sauron prend donc un risque réel.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi de la Terre (Earth Law).</strong> Manipule terre, pierre et matières inorganiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi du Feu (Fire Law).</strong> Contrôle feu et chaleur et fournit de puissantes attaques élémentaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi de la Glace (Ice Law).</strong> Crée et manipule glace et froid.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi de la Lumière (Light Law).</strong> Manipule lumière et électricité.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi de l’Eau (Water Law).</strong> Contrôle eau et autres liquides.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi du Vent (Wind Law).</strong> Manipule air, vents et mouvements atmosphériques.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>La Profession doit rester rare. Dans le Gondor, certains savoirs peuvent dériver d’archives númenóréennes ; à Umbar, une tradition apparentée peut avoir évolué vers des usages plus sombres ; chez les Noldor, les frontières entre artisanat supérieur, connaissance du monde et magie sont souvent moins nettes aux yeux des Hommes.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons directement le <strong>châssis du Magician RMU</strong> et ses six Listes élémentaires. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Empathie et Raisonnement</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>L’adaptation concerne surtout la rareté et le contexte : disponibilité des Listes selon l’Âge, nécessité d’un maître ou d’une tradition, usage discret de la magie et <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>. La puissance mécanique reste RMU ; son expression respecte les limites de la Terre du Milieu.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Magician","system":{"description":"<h1>Magician</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Magician</strong> is a pure <strong>Essence</strong> spell-user specializing in the manipulation of the world’s elemental forces. Where other spell-users turn toward divination, healing, or domination of the mind, the Magician acts directly upon matter and energy: earth, fire, ice, light, water, and wind. A Magician can transform the environment, alter temperature, move or reshape matter, and unleash extremely dangerous magical attacks.</p>\n<p>This mastery does not reduce the Magician to a mere thrower of elemental projectiles. The Profession’s Lists can move or transform matter, alter temperatures, control air and liquids, create elemental walls, and exploit light and electricity. RMU fully preserves this identity by defining the Magician as a pure spell-user able to manipulate the six major elements of its magical system.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, however, the Magician is a <strong>rare and remarkable</strong> figure. Essence certainly exists, but open and spectacular magic is unusual, especially in the later Ages. A human Magician of <strong>Gondor</strong> may be a scholar who uncovered ancient traditions in the archives of Minas Tirith; a <strong>Black Númenórean</strong> of Umbar may wield Essence within a more authoritarian or destructive tradition; a <strong>Noldo</strong> may approach elemental manipulation as an expression of very ancient knowledge concerning matter and the forces of Arda. Depending on Age and Culture, the same Profession may therefore represent very different magical traditions.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Magician’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Empathy</strong> is also the realm stat of Essence. Here it represents the Magician’s ability to perceive and manipulate the magical structures permeating the world.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning</strong> reflects the intellectual understanding required to learn complex principles, analyse magical phenomena, and master highly technical Lists.</p>\n<p>Empathy may therefore be marked both as a <strong>Prime Stat</strong> and as a <strong>realm stat</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Magician is a <strong>pure Essence spell-user</strong>.</p>\n<p>Development strongly favours:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Base Spell Lists</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>Open</strong> Essence Lists;</li>\n<li>magical lore;</li>\n<li><strong>Power Manipulation</strong>;</li>\n<li>perception of magical phenomena;</li>\n<li><strong>Directed Spells</strong> and other elemental attacks;</li>\n<li>Power Development;</li>\n<li>skills related to study, research, and the sciences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RMU gives the Magician excellent development costs in Lore, Magical Expertise, Power Manipulation, and Spell Casting. Base and Open Lists are among the Profession’s most accessible developments. Physical combat, heavy armour, and specialized martial disciplines remain secondary.</p>\n<h2>Essence and the Manipulation of the World</h2>\n<p>Essence acts upon the fundamental structures of the material world. For the Magician, this is expressed chiefly through elemental magic. Spells can heat or cool matter, create flame, alter earth or stone, move air or water, and manipulate light.</p>\n<p>This magic can be very concrete. A Magician using <strong>Earth Law</strong> can affect earth and stone. <strong>Water Law</strong> controls liquids. <strong>Wind Law</strong> acts upon air and atmospheric movement. <strong>Light Law</strong> also includes the manipulation of electricity.</p>\n<p>The Magician is therefore especially effective when the character understands the environment and chooses the right element for the situation.</p>\n<h2>Offensive Magic</h2>\n<p>The Magician has one of the highest offensive potentials among spell-users. The Profession’s Lists contain many spells that produce <strong>Elemental Bolts</strong>, <strong>Elemental Balls</strong>, dangerous areas, walls of fire or ice, and persistent elemental manifestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire Law</strong> includes practical effects such as heating liquids or solids as well as offensive spells such as fire bolts and fire balls. <strong>Ice Law</strong> can produce cold attacks and transform terrain. <strong>Light Law</strong> provides manifestations of light and electricity.</p>\n<p>This power requires real investment in the appropriate skills. A Magician who wants to specialize in magical combat must develop <strong>Directed Spells</strong> and the skills required for elemental attacks.</p>\n<h2>Dangerous Lore</h2>\n<p>The Magician’s power comes from understanding and control. In Middle-earth, conspicuous magic can attract attention, inspire fear, or reveal the caster’s presence to the forces of the Shadow. A <strong>Third Age</strong> Magician openly casting manifestations of fire or light in a region watched by Sauron’s servants takes a very different risk from a scholar quietly using a perception spell.</p>\n<p>Some traditions may lead toward much more dangerous uses of Essence. A <strong>Black Númenórean</strong> mage of Umbar may seek to use magic as an instrument of domination, while a scholar of <strong>Gondor</strong> may regard such practices with deep suspicion.</p>\n<p>Elemental power is therefore always tied to the moral and historical context in which it is used.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Magician is not naturally a physical combatant. Weapon training is possible, but costly compared with that of a Fighter or Rogue. Heavy armour strongly interferes with the development and effective use of Essence.</p>\n<p>A Magician of <strong>Gondor</strong> may carry a dagger or staff as a secondary weapon. A travelling <strong>Dúnadan</strong> mage may learn the sword from necessity. A <strong>Noldo</strong> may possess excellent martial training through Culture while remaining professionally a Magician.</p>\n<p>Such abilities therefore arise more from <strong>Race, Culture, or personal history</strong> than from the Profession itself.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Offensive Elementalist.</strong> Favours Fire Law, Ice Law, or Light Law together with Directed Spells and excels in magical combat.</p>\n<p><strong>Master of Matter.</strong> Develops Earth Law and Water Law, using magic to alter the environment, move obstacles, or transform terrain.</p>\n<p><strong>Storm Mage.</strong> Combines Wind Law, Water Law, and Light Law to manipulate air, precipitation, and electricity.</p>\n<p><strong>Scholar of Essence.</strong> Invests more heavily in lore, Power Manipulation, Open and Closed Lists, and magical research.</p>\n<p><strong>Battle Mage.</strong> Combines elemental attacks, magical defenses, and secondary martial training. This profile may suit a mage employed by the armies of Gondor or Umbar.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Mage.</strong> Favours versatile Lists and exploration skills, solving problems through intelligent use of several elements.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Magician has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>, all retained in RMU.</p>\n<p><strong>Earth Law</strong><br />Manipulates earth, stone, and other inorganic materials.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire Law</strong><br />Controls fire and heat and includes several extremely dangerous elemental attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Ice Law</strong><br />Creates, manipulates, and exploits ice and cold.</p>\n<p><strong>Light Law</strong><br />Manipulates light and electricity and also provides elemental attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Water Law</strong><br />Controls water and other liquids.</p>\n<p><strong>Wind Law</strong><br />Manipulates air, winds, and their movement.</p>\n<p>These six Lists form the core of the Magician’s identity. They represent six different ways of acting directly upon the physical world.</p>\n<h2>Other Essence Lists</h2>\n<p>The Magician may also develop <strong>Open</strong> and <strong>Closed</strong> Essence Lists. Open Lists provide relatively universal techniques: detection, elemental protection, perception of Essence, runes, and manipulation of barriers. Closed Lists contain more complex practices such as dispelling, rapid movement, invisibility, transformation, spell control, and deeper manipulations of matter and mind.</p>\n<p>In our Middle-earth adaptation, however, availability depends on Age, Culture, and the existence of a master or living tradition capable of teaching them.</p>\n<h2>The Magician in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Profession should remain relatively rare. Great manifestations of elemental magic do exist in Middle-earth, but they are not an everyday technology. A village is no more likely to have a resident Magician than it is to possess an ancient lore-master.</p>\n<p>A human Magician may have learned from an isolated master, an esoteric tradition, or the study of ancient texts.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Gondor</strong>, some knowledge may derive from Númenórean archives or scholarly traditions preserved in the great cities. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, a related tradition may have developed toward much darker uses. Among some <strong>Elves</strong>, especially the Noldor, the boundaries between supreme craftsmanship, knowledge of the world, and magic may appear far less distinct to mortal observers.</p>\n<p>Choosing the Profession should therefore always include consideration of <strong>where the character’s magical training came from</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Magician is one of the Professions for which continuity between older editions and RMU is especially strong.</p>\n<p>We use the <strong>RMU Magician mechanical chassis</strong> directly: a pure Essence spell-user with six Base Lists and a strong specialization in magical disciplines. RMU still uses the six Lists <strong>Earth Law, Fire Law, Ice Law, Light Law, Water Law</strong>, and <strong>Wind Law</strong>.</p>\n<p>We explicitly restore <strong>Empathy and Reasoning</strong> as Prime Stats so that character creation follows the MERP tradition rather than a generic spell-user model.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation mainly affects the <strong>context in which magic is used</strong>. RMU mechanics allow powerful spell-users; in Middle-earth, their existence must be placed within a world where magic is rarer, more discreet, and far more dependent upon history.</p>\n<p>We therefore keep RMU’s modern mechanics for elemental attacks, Lists, and spell casting while adding the rules specific to this adaptation: List availability by Age, learning tied to genuine traditions, discreet use of magic, and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong> when magical manifestations become sufficiently significant.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU Magician thus remains fully capable of mastering the elements, while the Profession’s power serves as a reminder that in Middle-earth, <strong>openly calling upon magic is never an entirely trivial act</strong>.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Essence"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Essence"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-essence"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"RMU Core Law","profession":"Magician","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Essence","description":"<h1>Magician</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Magician</strong> is a pure <strong>Essence</strong> spell-user specializing in the manipulation of the world’s elemental forces. Where other spell-users turn toward divination, healing, or domination of the mind, the Magician acts directly upon matter and energy: earth, fire, ice, light, water, and wind. A Magician can transform the environment, alter temperature, move or reshape matter, and unleash extremely dangerous magical attacks.</p>\n<p>This mastery does not reduce the Magician to a mere thrower of elemental projectiles. The Profession’s Lists can move or transform matter, alter temperatures, control air and liquids, create elemental walls, and exploit light and electricity. RMU fully preserves this identity by defining the Magician as a pure spell-user able to manipulate the six major elements of its magical system.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, however, the Magician is a <strong>rare and remarkable</strong> figure. Essence certainly exists, but open and spectacular magic is unusual, especially in the later Ages. A human Magician of <strong>Gondor</strong> may be a scholar who uncovered ancient traditions in the archives of Minas Tirith; a <strong>Black Númenórean</strong> of Umbar may wield Essence within a more authoritarian or destructive tradition; a <strong>Noldo</strong> may approach elemental manipulation as an expression of very ancient knowledge concerning matter and the forces of Arda. Depending on Age and Culture, the same Profession may therefore represent very different magical traditions.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Magician’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Empathy</strong> is also the realm stat of Essence. Here it represents the Magician’s ability to perceive and manipulate the magical structures permeating the world.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning</strong> reflects the intellectual understanding required to learn complex principles, analyse magical phenomena, and master highly technical Lists.</p>\n<p>Empathy may therefore be marked both as a <strong>Prime Stat</strong> and as a <strong>realm stat</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Magician is a <strong>pure Essence spell-user</strong>.</p>\n<p>Development strongly favours:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Base Spell Lists</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>Open</strong> Essence Lists;</li>\n<li>magical lore;</li>\n<li><strong>Power Manipulation</strong>;</li>\n<li>perception of magical phenomena;</li>\n<li><strong>Directed Spells</strong> and other elemental attacks;</li>\n<li>Power Development;</li>\n<li>skills related to study, research, and the sciences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RMU gives the Magician excellent development costs in Lore, Magical Expertise, Power Manipulation, and Spell Casting. Base and Open Lists are among the Profession’s most accessible developments. Physical combat, heavy armour, and specialized martial disciplines remain secondary.</p>\n<h2>Essence and the Manipulation of the World</h2>\n<p>Essence acts upon the fundamental structures of the material world. For the Magician, this is expressed chiefly through elemental magic. Spells can heat or cool matter, create flame, alter earth or stone, move air or water, and manipulate light.</p>\n<p>This magic can be very concrete. A Magician using <strong>Earth Law</strong> can affect earth and stone. <strong>Water Law</strong> controls liquids. <strong>Wind Law</strong> acts upon air and atmospheric movement. <strong>Light Law</strong> also includes the manipulation of electricity.</p>\n<p>The Magician is therefore especially effective when the character understands the environment and chooses the right element for the situation.</p>\n<h2>Offensive Magic</h2>\n<p>The Magician has one of the highest offensive potentials among spell-users. The Profession’s Lists contain many spells that produce <strong>Elemental Bolts</strong>, <strong>Elemental Balls</strong>, dangerous areas, walls of fire or ice, and persistent elemental manifestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire Law</strong> includes practical effects such as heating liquids or solids as well as offensive spells such as fire bolts and fire balls. <strong>Ice Law</strong> can produce cold attacks and transform terrain. <strong>Light Law</strong> provides manifestations of light and electricity.</p>\n<p>This power requires real investment in the appropriate skills. A Magician who wants to specialize in magical combat must develop <strong>Directed Spells</strong> and the skills required for elemental attacks.</p>\n<h2>Dangerous Lore</h2>\n<p>The Magician’s power comes from understanding and control. In Middle-earth, conspicuous magic can attract attention, inspire fear, or reveal the caster’s presence to the forces of the Shadow. A <strong>Third Age</strong> Magician openly casting manifestations of fire or light in a region watched by Sauron’s servants takes a very different risk from a scholar quietly using a perception spell.</p>\n<p>Some traditions may lead toward much more dangerous uses of Essence. A <strong>Black Númenórean</strong> mage of Umbar may seek to use magic as an instrument of domination, while a scholar of <strong>Gondor</strong> may regard such practices with deep suspicion.</p>\n<p>Elemental power is therefore always tied to the moral and historical context in which it is used.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Magician is not naturally a physical combatant. Weapon training is possible, but costly compared with that of a Fighter or Rogue. Heavy armour strongly interferes with the development and effective use of Essence.</p>\n<p>A Magician of <strong>Gondor</strong> may carry a dagger or staff as a secondary weapon. A travelling <strong>Dúnadan</strong> mage may learn the sword from necessity. A <strong>Noldo</strong> may possess excellent martial training through Culture while remaining professionally a Magician.</p>\n<p>Such abilities therefore arise more from <strong>Race, Culture, or personal history</strong> than from the Profession itself.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Offensive Elementalist.</strong> Favours Fire Law, Ice Law, or Light Law together with Directed Spells and excels in magical combat.</p>\n<p><strong>Master of Matter.</strong> Develops Earth Law and Water Law, using magic to alter the environment, move obstacles, or transform terrain.</p>\n<p><strong>Storm Mage.</strong> Combines Wind Law, Water Law, and Light Law to manipulate air, precipitation, and electricity.</p>\n<p><strong>Scholar of Essence.</strong> Invests more heavily in lore, Power Manipulation, Open and Closed Lists, and magical research.</p>\n<p><strong>Battle Mage.</strong> Combines elemental attacks, magical defenses, and secondary martial training. This profile may suit a mage employed by the armies of Gondor or Umbar.</p>\n<p><strong>Wandering Mage.</strong> Favours versatile Lists and exploration skills, solving problems through intelligent use of several elements.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Magician has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>, all retained in RMU.</p>\n<p><strong>Earth Law</strong><br />Manipulates earth, stone, and other inorganic materials.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire Law</strong><br />Controls fire and heat and includes several extremely dangerous elemental attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Ice Law</strong><br />Creates, manipulates, and exploits ice and cold.</p>\n<p><strong>Light Law</strong><br />Manipulates light and electricity and also provides elemental attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Water Law</strong><br />Controls water and other liquids.</p>\n<p><strong>Wind Law</strong><br />Manipulates air, winds, and their movement.</p>\n<p>These six Lists form the core of the Magician’s identity. They represent six different ways of acting directly upon the physical world.</p>\n<h2>Other Essence Lists</h2>\n<p>The Magician may also develop <strong>Open</strong> and <strong>Closed</strong> Essence Lists. Open Lists provide relatively universal techniques: detection, elemental protection, perception of Essence, runes, and manipulation of barriers. Closed Lists contain more complex practices such as dispelling, rapid movement, invisibility, transformation, spell control, and deeper manipulations of matter and mind.</p>\n<p>In our Middle-earth adaptation, however, availability depends on Age, Culture, and the existence of a master or living tradition capable of teaching them.</p>\n<h2>The Magician in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Profession should remain relatively rare. Great manifestations of elemental magic do exist in Middle-earth, but they are not an everyday technology. A village is no more likely to have a resident Magician than it is to possess an ancient lore-master.</p>\n<p>A human Magician may have learned from an isolated master, an esoteric tradition, or the study of ancient texts.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Gondor</strong>, some knowledge may derive from Númenórean archives or scholarly traditions preserved in the great cities. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, a related tradition may have developed toward much darker uses. Among some <strong>Elves</strong>, especially the Noldor, the boundaries between supreme craftsmanship, knowledge of the world, and magic may appear far less distinct to mortal observers.</p>\n<p>Choosing the Profession should therefore always include consideration of <strong>where the character’s magical training came from</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Magician is one of the Professions for which continuity between older editions and RMU is especially strong.</p>\n<p>We use the <strong>RMU Magician mechanical chassis</strong> directly: a pure Essence spell-user with six Base Lists and a strong specialization in magical disciplines. RMU still uses the six Lists <strong>Earth Law, Fire Law, Ice Law, Light Law, Water Law</strong>, and <strong>Wind Law</strong>.</p>\n<p>We explicitly restore <strong>Empathy and Reasoning</strong> as Prime Stats so that character creation follows the MERP tradition rather than a generic spell-user model.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU adaptation mainly affects the <strong>context in which magic is used</strong>. RMU mechanics allow powerful spell-users; in Middle-earth, their existence must be placed within a world where magic is rarer, more discreet, and far more dependent upon history.</p>\n<p>We therefore keep RMU’s modern mechanics for elemental attacks, Lists, and spell casting while adding the rules specific to this adaptation: List availability by Age, learning tied to genuine traditions, discreet use of magic, and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong> when magical manifestations become sufficiently significant.</p>\n<p>The MERP-RMU Magician thus remains fully capable of mastering the elements, while the Profession’s power serves as a reminder that in Middle-earth, <strong>openly calling upon magic is never an entirely trivial act</strong>.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/3"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"3/4"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Attunement"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Runes"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Spell Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","skillName":"Grace"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Directed Spell"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Projection"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Architecture"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Astronomy"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Engineering"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Mathematics"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual"}]},"_id":"KhsTmYdc2rYJSwaZ"}
{"name":"Ranger","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"ranger","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"Compendium.rmu.core.Item.kBHmHUfOlJSfzqZV; MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Ranger"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"ranger","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":"kBHmHUfOlJSfzqZV","sourceName":null,"document":{"name":"Rôdeur","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"ranger","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"Compendium.rmu.core.Item.kBHmHUfOlJSfzqZV; MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Ranger"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Rôdeur","description":"<h1>Rôdeur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Rôdeur</strong> est un combattant des grands espaces, un voyageur endurant et un gardien des terres sauvages. Il associe formation martiale, connaissance de la nature et magie discrète de <strong>Théurgie</strong>. Ses sorts renforcent survie, déplacement, camouflage, sens et relation au monde naturel.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, la Profession convient aux chasseurs, pisteurs, gardiens de routes oubliées, patrouilleurs et protecteurs de frontières. Un Rôdeur d’Arnor, un gardien du Rhovanion ou un homme des collines connaissant chaque sentier de son territoire peuvent partager le même châssis avec des méthodes très différentes.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> et <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. La Constitution soutient endurance et résistance ; l’Intuition gouverne perception du terrain et sert également de caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Rôdeur est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Armes, activités de plein air, Perception, Pistage, Discrétion, déplacement, Survie, animaux, environnement et Listes professionnelles. Sa magie amplifie des talents qu’il possède déjà.</p>\n<h2>Combat et équipement</h2>\n<p>Il peut se spécialiser en armes de mêlée ou à distance. Un Rohir peut privilégier lance et équitation ; un Dúnadan du Nord arc et épée ; un Homme des Bois arc, lance et discrétion forestière. Les protections légères ou moyennes s’accordent généralement mieux avec sa mobilité.</p>\n<h2>Vie dans les terres sauvages</h2>\n<p>Le terrain est son domaine. Pistage, choix d’itinéraire, eau, campement, signes de danger, animaux, climat et plantes font partie de son quotidien. Il peut guider un groupe entier dans des régions éloignées des routes.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur.</strong> Perception, Pistage, Discrétion et armes à distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Chasseur.</strong> Pistage, animaux, Survie et tir.</li>\n<li><strong>Gardien des frontières.</strong> Armes, endurance, observation et connaissance du territoire.</li>\n<li><strong>Guide.</strong> Navigation terrestre, connaissances régionales et Survie.</li>\n<li><strong>Rôdeur mystique.</strong> Investissement plus important dans les Listes et la Théurgie.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Murs Intérieurs (Inner Walls).</strong> Renforce résistance et capacités pratiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies du Mouvement (Moving Ways).</strong> Améliore les déplacements et l’adaptation au terrain.</li>\n<li><strong>Masques de la Nature (Nature’s Guises).</strong> Camouflage et dissimulation dans l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Appels de la Nature (Nature’s Summons).</strong> Fait appel aux animaux, à la végétation et à certains phénomènes naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Nature (Nature’s Way).</strong> Survie, eau, abri et adaptation au milieu.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Sentiers (Path Mastery).</strong> Informations sur chemins, pistes et itinéraires.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Ranger RMU</strong> : semi-magique de Théurgie, capable de combattre et d’évoluer dans les terres sauvages. MERP-RMU restaure <strong>Constitution et Intuition</strong> et présente les six Listes selon leur identité traditionnelle.</p>\n<p>Lorsque RMU possède une mécanique moderne correspondante, elle sert de base technique ; les divergences sont adaptées afin de préserver le Rôdeur comme combattant, pisteur et homme des terres sauvages dont la magie soutient l’expérience plutôt qu’elle ne la remplace.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Rôdeur","system":{"description":"<h1>Rôdeur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Rôdeur</strong> est un combattant des grands espaces, un voyageur endurant et un gardien des terres sauvages. Il associe formation martiale, connaissance de la nature et magie discrète de <strong>Théurgie</strong>. Ses sorts renforcent survie, déplacement, camouflage, sens et relation au monde naturel.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, la Profession convient aux chasseurs, pisteurs, gardiens de routes oubliées, patrouilleurs et protecteurs de frontières. Un Rôdeur d’Arnor, un gardien du Rhovanion ou un homme des collines connaissant chaque sentier de son territoire peuvent partager le même châssis avec des méthodes très différentes.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> et <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. La Constitution soutient endurance et résistance ; l’Intuition gouverne perception du terrain et sert également de caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Rôdeur est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Armes, activités de plein air, Perception, Pistage, Discrétion, déplacement, Survie, animaux, environnement et Listes professionnelles. Sa magie amplifie des talents qu’il possède déjà.</p>\n<h2>Combat et équipement</h2>\n<p>Il peut se spécialiser en armes de mêlée ou à distance. Un Rohir peut privilégier lance et équitation ; un Dúnadan du Nord arc et épée ; un Homme des Bois arc, lance et discrétion forestière. Les protections légères ou moyennes s’accordent généralement mieux avec sa mobilité.</p>\n<h2>Vie dans les terres sauvages</h2>\n<p>Le terrain est son domaine. Pistage, choix d’itinéraire, eau, campement, signes de danger, animaux, climat et plantes font partie de son quotidien. Il peut guider un groupe entier dans des régions éloignées des routes.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur.</strong> Perception, Pistage, Discrétion et armes à distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Chasseur.</strong> Pistage, animaux, Survie et tir.</li>\n<li><strong>Gardien des frontières.</strong> Armes, endurance, observation et connaissance du territoire.</li>\n<li><strong>Guide.</strong> Navigation terrestre, connaissances régionales et Survie.</li>\n<li><strong>Rôdeur mystique.</strong> Investissement plus important dans les Listes et la Théurgie.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Murs Intérieurs (Inner Walls).</strong> Renforce résistance et capacités pratiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies du Mouvement (Moving Ways).</strong> Améliore les déplacements et l’adaptation au terrain.</li>\n<li><strong>Masques de la Nature (Nature’s Guises).</strong> Camouflage et dissimulation dans l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Appels de la Nature (Nature’s Summons).</strong> Fait appel aux animaux, à la végétation et à certains phénomènes naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Nature (Nature’s Way).</strong> Survie, eau, abri et adaptation au milieu.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Sentiers (Path Mastery).</strong> Informations sur chemins, pistes et itinéraires.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Ranger RMU</strong> : semi-magique de Théurgie, capable de combattre et d’évoluer dans les terres sauvages. MERP-RMU restaure <strong>Constitution et Intuition</strong> et présente les six Listes selon leur identité traditionnelle.</p>\n<p>Lorsque RMU possède une mécanique moderne correspondante, elle sert de base technique ; les divergences sont adaptées afin de préserver le Rôdeur comme combattant, pisteur et homme des terres sauvages dont la magie soutient l’expérience plutôt qu’elle ne la remplace.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Ranger","system":{"description":"<h1>Ranger</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Ranger</strong> is a warrior of the open lands, an enduring traveller, and a guardian of the wild. The Profession combines solid martial training with intimate knowledge of nature and subtle <strong>Channeling</strong> magic. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Ranger was already a semi-spell-user combining Weapons and Channeling, with magic devoted mainly to survival, movement, camouflage, animals, and natural conditions.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Profession is particularly suitable for characters who live on the borders of settled realms: hunters, trackers, guardians of forgotten roads, patrolmen, foresters, and protectors of isolated communities. A Ranger of Arnor, a hill-country warrior who knows every path of the homeland, or a keeper of the forest margins of Rhovanion may share the same Profession while possessing very different cultures and methods.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Ranger’s Prime Stats are <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> and <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. Constitution represents physical endurance; Intuition reflects the Ranger’s instinctive relationship with the environment and ability to use Channeling. Intuition is also the realm stat.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. The Profession remains much closer to a warrior and explorer than to a pure spell-user.</p>\n<p>Development favours <strong>Weapons</strong>, outdoor activities, <strong>Perception</strong> and <strong>Tracking</strong>, Stealth, movement and endurance, animals, the environment, Survival, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is naturally comfortable with weapons and may specialize in melee or ranged combat. The bow suits the traditional role especially well but is not mandatory. A Ranger may wield spear, sword, axe, or any weapon appropriate to Culture.</p>\n<p>Light or medium protection generally fits better with the mobility required by the Profession’s activities.</p>\n<h2>Life in the Wild</h2>\n<p>The land itself is the Ranger’s true domain. The Profession reads tracks, chooses routes, recognizes signs of danger, and draws upon the resources of the natural environment. <strong>Tracking</strong> naturally plays a major role, together with knowledge of animals, climate, plants, and regions travelled.</p>\n<p>A good Ranger can also guide an entire group: find water, choose a campsite, avoid a dangerous pass, or lead companions to their destination without attracting unnecessary attention.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Tracking, Stealth, and ranged weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Hunter.</strong> Tracking, animal lore, missile weapons, and Survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Border Warden.</strong> Weapons, endurance, observation, and knowledge of the territory.</p>\n<p><strong>Guide.</strong> Land navigation, regional lore, Survival, and avoidance of natural obstacles.</p>\n<p><strong>Mystic Ranger.</strong> Greater investment in Spell Lists and the relationship with animals, plants, and natural forces.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Ranger has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong> in the MERP tradition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inner Walls</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Moving Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Guises</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Summons</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Way</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Path Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Complementary Magic</h2>\n<p>The Ranger can learn some Open Channeling Lists more easily than forms of magic foreign to the Profession. Magic remains secondary to martial and exploration skills. Actual access to a new List depends on Age, master, and available tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>general RMU Ranger chassis</strong>: semi-spell-user status, Channeling realm, martial ability, and an orientation toward field skills. We explicitly restore <strong>Constitution and Intuition</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>RMU has six modern Ranger Lists. For MERP-RMU we preserve the identity more directly connected to Middle-earth in earlier editions: <strong>Inner Walls, Moving Ways, Nature’s Guises, Nature’s Summons, Nature’s Way</strong>, and <strong>Path Mastery</strong>. Where RMU provides mechanics matching these concepts, they serve as the technical base; where MERP identity diverges, the List is adapted.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"ranger","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-semi-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Rôdeur","system":{"description":"<h1>Rôdeur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Rôdeur</strong> est un combattant des grands espaces, un voyageur endurant et un gardien des terres sauvages. Il associe formation martiale, connaissance de la nature et magie discrète de <strong>Théurgie</strong>. Ses sorts renforcent survie, déplacement, camouflage, sens et relation au monde naturel.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, la Profession convient aux chasseurs, pisteurs, gardiens de routes oubliées, patrouilleurs et protecteurs de frontières. Un Rôdeur d’Arnor, un gardien du Rhovanion ou un homme des collines connaissant chaque sentier de son territoire peuvent partager le même châssis avec des méthodes très différentes.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> et <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. La Constitution soutient endurance et résistance ; l’Intuition gouverne perception du terrain et sert également de caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Rôdeur est un <strong>semi-utilisateur de Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Armes, activités de plein air, Perception, Pistage, Discrétion, déplacement, Survie, animaux, environnement et Listes professionnelles. Sa magie amplifie des talents qu’il possède déjà.</p>\n<h2>Combat et équipement</h2>\n<p>Il peut se spécialiser en armes de mêlée ou à distance. Un Rohir peut privilégier lance et équitation ; un Dúnadan du Nord arc et épée ; un Homme des Bois arc, lance et discrétion forestière. Les protections légères ou moyennes s’accordent généralement mieux avec sa mobilité.</p>\n<h2>Vie dans les terres sauvages</h2>\n<p>Le terrain est son domaine. Pistage, choix d’itinéraire, eau, campement, signes de danger, animaux, climat et plantes font partie de son quotidien. Il peut guider un groupe entier dans des régions éloignées des routes.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur.</strong> Perception, Pistage, Discrétion et armes à distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Chasseur.</strong> Pistage, animaux, Survie et tir.</li>\n<li><strong>Gardien des frontières.</strong> Armes, endurance, observation et connaissance du territoire.</li>\n<li><strong>Guide.</strong> Navigation terrestre, connaissances régionales et Survie.</li>\n<li><strong>Rôdeur mystique.</strong> Investissement plus important dans les Listes et la Théurgie.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Murs Intérieurs (Inner Walls).</strong> Renforce résistance et capacités pratiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies du Mouvement (Moving Ways).</strong> Améliore les déplacements et l’adaptation au terrain.</li>\n<li><strong>Masques de la Nature (Nature’s Guises).</strong> Camouflage et dissimulation dans l’environnement.</li>\n<li><strong>Appels de la Nature (Nature’s Summons).</strong> Fait appel aux animaux, à la végétation et à certains phénomènes naturels.</li>\n<li><strong>Voie de la Nature (Nature’s Way).</strong> Survie, eau, abri et adaptation au milieu.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Sentiers (Path Mastery).</strong> Informations sur chemins, pistes et itinéraires.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis du Ranger RMU</strong> : semi-magique de Théurgie, capable de combattre et d’évoluer dans les terres sauvages. MERP-RMU restaure <strong>Constitution et Intuition</strong> et présente les six Listes selon leur identité traditionnelle.</p>\n<p>Lorsque RMU possède une mécanique moderne correspondante, elle sert de base technique ; les divergences sont adaptées afin de préserver le Rôdeur comme combattant, pisteur et homme des terres sauvages dont la magie soutient l’expérience plutôt qu’elle ne la remplace.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Ranger","system":{"description":"<h1>Ranger</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Ranger</strong> is a warrior of the open lands, an enduring traveller, and a guardian of the wild. The Profession combines solid martial training with intimate knowledge of nature and subtle <strong>Channeling</strong> magic. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Ranger was already a semi-spell-user combining Weapons and Channeling, with magic devoted mainly to survival, movement, camouflage, animals, and natural conditions.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Profession is particularly suitable for characters who live on the borders of settled realms: hunters, trackers, guardians of forgotten roads, patrolmen, foresters, and protectors of isolated communities. A Ranger of Arnor, a hill-country warrior who knows every path of the homeland, or a keeper of the forest margins of Rhovanion may share the same Profession while possessing very different cultures and methods.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Ranger’s Prime Stats are <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> and <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. Constitution represents physical endurance; Intuition reflects the Ranger’s instinctive relationship with the environment and ability to use Channeling. Intuition is also the realm stat.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. The Profession remains much closer to a warrior and explorer than to a pure spell-user.</p>\n<p>Development favours <strong>Weapons</strong>, outdoor activities, <strong>Perception</strong> and <strong>Tracking</strong>, Stealth, movement and endurance, animals, the environment, Survival, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is naturally comfortable with weapons and may specialize in melee or ranged combat. The bow suits the traditional role especially well but is not mandatory. A Ranger may wield spear, sword, axe, or any weapon appropriate to Culture.</p>\n<p>Light or medium protection generally fits better with the mobility required by the Profession’s activities.</p>\n<h2>Life in the Wild</h2>\n<p>The land itself is the Ranger’s true domain. The Profession reads tracks, chooses routes, recognizes signs of danger, and draws upon the resources of the natural environment. <strong>Tracking</strong> naturally plays a major role, together with knowledge of animals, climate, plants, and regions travelled.</p>\n<p>A good Ranger can also guide an entire group: find water, choose a campsite, avoid a dangerous pass, or lead companions to their destination without attracting unnecessary attention.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Tracking, Stealth, and ranged weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Hunter.</strong> Tracking, animal lore, missile weapons, and Survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Border Warden.</strong> Weapons, endurance, observation, and knowledge of the territory.</p>\n<p><strong>Guide.</strong> Land navigation, regional lore, Survival, and avoidance of natural obstacles.</p>\n<p><strong>Mystic Ranger.</strong> Greater investment in Spell Lists and the relationship with animals, plants, and natural forces.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Ranger has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong> in the MERP tradition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inner Walls</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Moving Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Guises</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Summons</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Way</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Path Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Complementary Magic</h2>\n<p>The Ranger can learn some Open Channeling Lists more easily than forms of magic foreign to the Profession. Magic remains secondary to martial and exploration skills. Actual access to a new List depends on Age, master, and available tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>general RMU Ranger chassis</strong>: semi-spell-user status, Channeling realm, martial ability, and an orientation toward field skills. We explicitly restore <strong>Constitution and Intuition</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>RMU has six modern Ranger Lists. For MERP-RMU we preserve the identity more directly connected to Middle-earth in earlier editions: <strong>Inner Walls, Moving Ways, Nature’s Guises, Nature’s Summons, Nature’s Way</strong>, and <strong>Path Mastery</strong>. Where RMU provides mechanics matching these concepts, they serve as the technical base; where MERP identity diverges, the List is adapted.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Semi-Magiques","Théurgie"],"en":["Semi Spell-user Professions","Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-semi-magiques","professions-semi-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Core Law","profession":"Rôdeur","description":"<h1>Ranger</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Ranger</strong> is a warrior of the open lands, an enduring traveller, and a guardian of the wild. The Profession combines solid martial training with intimate knowledge of nature and subtle <strong>Channeling</strong> magic. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Ranger was already a semi-spell-user combining Weapons and Channeling, with magic devoted mainly to survival, movement, camouflage, animals, and natural conditions.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Profession is particularly suitable for characters who live on the borders of settled realms: hunters, trackers, guardians of forgotten roads, patrolmen, foresters, and protectors of isolated communities. A Ranger of Arnor, a hill-country warrior who knows every path of the homeland, or a keeper of the forest margins of Rhovanion may share the same Profession while possessing very different cultures and methods.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Ranger’s Prime Stats are <strong>Constitution (Co)</strong> and <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>. Constitution represents physical endurance; Intuition reflects the Ranger’s instinctive relationship with the environment and ability to use Channeling. Intuition is also the realm stat.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is a <strong>semi-spell-user of Channeling</strong>. The Profession remains much closer to a warrior and explorer than to a pure spell-user.</p>\n<p>Development favours <strong>Weapons</strong>, outdoor activities, <strong>Perception</strong> and <strong>Tracking</strong>, Stealth, movement and endurance, animals, the environment, Survival, and Base Spell Lists.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Equipment</h2>\n<p>The Ranger is naturally comfortable with weapons and may specialize in melee or ranged combat. The bow suits the traditional role especially well but is not mandatory. A Ranger may wield spear, sword, axe, or any weapon appropriate to Culture.</p>\n<p>Light or medium protection generally fits better with the mobility required by the Profession’s activities.</p>\n<h2>Life in the Wild</h2>\n<p>The land itself is the Ranger’s true domain. The Profession reads tracks, chooses routes, recognizes signs of danger, and draws upon the resources of the natural environment. <strong>Tracking</strong> naturally plays a major role, together with knowledge of animals, climate, plants, and regions travelled.</p>\n<p>A good Ranger can also guide an entire group: find water, choose a campsite, avoid a dangerous pass, or lead companions to their destination without attracting unnecessary attention.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Tracking, Stealth, and ranged weapons.</p>\n<p><strong>Hunter.</strong> Tracking, animal lore, missile weapons, and Survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Border Warden.</strong> Weapons, endurance, observation, and knowledge of the territory.</p>\n<p><strong>Guide.</strong> Land navigation, regional lore, Survival, and avoidance of natural obstacles.</p>\n<p><strong>Mystic Ranger.</strong> Greater investment in Spell Lists and the relationship with animals, plants, and natural forces.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Ranger has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong> in the MERP tradition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inner Walls</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Moving Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Guises</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Summons</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nature’s Way</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Path Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Complementary Magic</h2>\n<p>The Ranger can learn some Open Channeling Lists more easily than forms of magic foreign to the Profession. Magic remains secondary to martial and exploration skills. Actual access to a new List depends on Age, master, and available tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the <strong>general RMU Ranger chassis</strong>: semi-spell-user status, Channeling realm, martial ability, and an orientation toward field skills. We explicitly restore <strong>Constitution and Intuition</strong> as Prime Stats.</p>\n<p>RMU has six modern Ranger Lists. For MERP-RMU we preserve the identity more directly connected to Middle-earth in earlier editions: <strong>Inner Walls, Moving Ways, Nature’s Guises, Nature’s Summons, Nature’s Way</strong>, and <strong>Path Mastery</strong>. Where RMU provides mechanics matching these concepts, they serve as the technical base; where MERP identity diverges, the List is adapted.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"udsOP8QW8uXDECcw"}
{"name":"Razak-Zinul","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"razak-zinul","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 pp.146, 233–240; Alchemy Companion Dwarven Alchemist imbed lists"},"compendiumSourceKey":"razak-zinul","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Razak-Zinul","system":{"description":"<h1>Razak-Zinul</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Razak-Zinul</strong>, « Barbe-de-Forge » en khuzdul, est un utilisateur pur de <strong>Théurgie</strong> exclusivement issu des <strong>Khazâd</strong>. Sa magie est tournée vers l’artisanat, la création d’armes et d’armures, l’enchantement d’objets, l’analyse des artefacts et la protection par des inscriptions ou des runes.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession représente une expression naturelle de la magie khazâd. Un artisan de la Maison de Durin peut travailler sur les traditions de Khazad-dûm ; un Barbe-de-Feu privilégier la forge et les métaux ; un membre d’une Maison orientale transmettre d’autres traditions de pierre, gemmes ou inscriptions.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Raisonnement (Re)</strong>. L’Intuition est aussi la caractéristique de royaume de la Théurgie ; le Raisonnement soutient conception, analyse et procédés artisanaux complexes.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Razak-Zinul favorise Artisanat, Listes de base, connaissances magiques et anciennes, Développement des Points de Puissance, Perception de la Puissance, compétences techniques, Influence et Armes.</p>\n<h2>Aulë et la tradition khazâd</h2>\n<p>La Théurgie du Razak-Zinul s’interprète à travers la relation des Nains à <strong>Aulë/Mahal</strong>. Le travail de la matière possède une dimension sacrée et la Profession donne une expression magique à cet héritage.</p>\n<h2>Runes et inscriptions</h2>\n<p>Les sorts intégrés dans un objet sont associés à des inscriptions ou runes. Une rune peut être gravée sur une lame, cachée dans une armure, inscrite sur une pierre, un mécanisme ou un bijou. Le langage et la tradition de gravure peuvent servir de clé d’activation et de protection du savoir.</p>\n<h2>Neuf Listes, six choix</h2>\n<p>Le Razak-Zinul possède neuf traditions professionnelles et en choisit <strong>six comme Listes de base</strong>. Les trois restantes restent plus difficiles et ne deviennent pas automatiquement de nouvelles Listes de base. Ce choix définit très tôt la spécialité artisanale du personnage.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forge d’Armures (Armourer).</strong> Crée des armures magiques et améliore leurs défenses.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise de l’Artisan (Craftsman).</strong> Travaille matières organiques et dispositifs mécaniques.</li>\n<li><strong>Techniques Inorganiques Naines (Dwarven Inorganic Skills).</strong> Travaille les matières inorganiques selon les traditions khazâd.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise du Savoir (Loremaster).</strong> Crée des objets généraux et analyse les objets de pouvoir.</li>\n<li><strong>Imprégnation d’un Autre Royaume (Other Realm Imbedding).</strong> Fixe des sorts d’un autre royaume dans un objet.</li>\n<li><strong>Imprégnation de son Propre Royaume (Own Realm Imbedding).</strong> Fixe des sorts de Théurgie dans un objet.</li>\n<li><strong>Symboles (Symbols).</strong> Crée des inscriptions contenant des sorts.</li>\n<li><strong>Gardes (Wards).</strong> Crée des protections magiques pour personnes et lieux.</li>\n<li><strong>Forge d’Armes (Weaponsmith).</strong> Crée des armes magiques et améliore leurs capacités de combat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Dans Erebor, il peut servir une grande forge ; dans les Montagnes Bleues, conserver des traditions anciennes ; dans les anciennes demeures de Durin, rechercher des techniques perdues. Voyages, minerais rares, ateliers abandonnés, objets volés et runes oubliées constituent des motivations naturelles d’aventure.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Le Razak-Zinul n’a pas d’équivalent complet dans RMU. MERP-RMU conserve une Profession <strong>pure de Théurgie khazâd</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition et Raisonnement</strong> et le mécanisme historique de <strong>six Listes choisies parmi neuf</strong>.</p>\n<p>Les mécanismes modernes d’Artisanat, d’enchantement, d’analyse et de runes servent de socle lorsque possible, tandis que les traditions spécifiquement naines et les règles d’inscription restent propres à MERP-RMU.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Razak-Zinul","system":{"description":"<h1>Razak-Zinul</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Razak-Zinul</strong>, literally <strong>“Forge-beard”</strong> in Khuzdul, is a pure <strong>Channeling</strong> spell-user found exclusively among the <strong>Khazâd</strong>. The Profession’s magic is directed toward craft, the making of weapons and armour, the enchantment of objects, analysis of artefacts, and protection through inscriptions and runes. The Razak-Zinul joins the spiritual tradition of the Dwarves to their exceptional mastery of matter.</p>\n<p>A Razak-Zinul of the <strong>House of Durin</strong> may work upon weapons, armour, and inscriptions inherited from Khazad-dûm; a craftsman of the <strong>Firebeards</strong> may place greater emphasis on forge-work and metals; a member of an eastern House may preserve a different tradition of engraving, stonework, or object-making.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Reasoning represents the technical understanding needed to design an object, analyse its structure, and organize the stages of complex manufacture.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul is a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development particularly favours <strong>Craft</strong>, Base Spell Lists, magical and ancient lore, Power Point Development, Power Perception, selected technical skills, Influence, and Weapons.</p>\n<h2>Craft and Magic</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul regards magic as one component of <strong>work properly done</strong>. Power is integrated into metal, stone, wood, a weapon, armour, a mechanism, an inscription, or a protective symbol. Creation demands as much craftsmanship as magical ability.</p>\n<h2>Mahal and the Khazâd Tradition</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul’s Channeling is naturally understood through the Dwarves’ relationship with <strong>Aulë, Mahal</strong>. The Khazâd were created by him, and their exceptional affinity with stone, metal, and craft belongs directly to that heritage. The Razak-Zinul gives magical expression to this relationship.</p>\n<h2>Runes and Inscriptions</h2>\n<p>Spells embedded in an object are linked to an inscription or rune written in a specific language. The inscription must be read aloud to activate the power. A rune may be carved into a blade, hidden inside armour, cut into stone, worked into a jewel, or placed upon a door.</p>\n<h2>Weapons, Armour, and Matter</h2>\n<p><strong>Weaponsmith</strong> creates enchanted weapons. <strong>Armourer</strong> creates magical protections. <strong>Craftsman</strong> works with organic materials and mechanisms. <strong>Dwarven Inorganic Skills</strong> represents Khazâd lore concerning stone, metal, gems, and minerals.</p>\n<h2>Loremaster and Imbedding</h2>\n<p><strong>Loremaster</strong> allows the creation of general magical items and the analysis of objects of power. <strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong> fixes Channeling spells into objects. <strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong> permits work with a Magician, Lay Healer, or another spell-user in order to incorporate magic from a different realm.</p>\n<h2>Symbols and Wards</h2>\n<p><strong>Symbols</strong> creates inscriptions capable of holding spells. <strong>Wards</strong> protects people and places: doors, halls, workshops, treasuries, tombs, or important objects. In a Khazâd dwelling these protections may be combined with physical mechanisms and massive doors.</p>\n<h2>Nine Lists, Six Choices</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul has <strong>nine professional Lists</strong>, but the character chooses only <strong>six as Base Spell Lists</strong>. The remaining three become more difficult to develop. This rule forces an early choice about what kind of craftsman the character will become.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Master Smith.</strong> Weaponsmith, Armourer, and Dwarven Inorganic Skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Rune-master.</strong> Symbols, Wards, and Own Realm Imbedding.</p>\n<p><strong>Maker of Objects.</strong> Craftsman, Loremaster, and Imbedding techniques.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of the Gates.</strong> Wards, Symbols, Craft, and defensive mechanisms.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul chooses <strong>six Base Spell Lists from nine</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Armourer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Craftsman</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dwarven Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loremaster</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symbols</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Wards</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weaponsmith</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Secrecy and Transmission</h2>\n<p>The Dwarves guard their lore jealously. Enchanting techniques, runes, forging methods, and knowledge of certain materials may be restricted to a family, House, guild, or workshop. The choice of a master is therefore an important part of the character’s history.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul has no complete direct professional equivalent among standard RMU Professions. We preserve the historical identity of a <strong>pure Khazâd Channeling spell-user specializing in magical craft</strong>, the Prime Stats <strong>Intuition and Reasoning</strong>, the restriction to the Khazâd, and the defining mechanism of <strong>nine available Lists of which only six become Base Spell Lists</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adaptation emphasizes the <strong>rare, secret, and enduring</strong> character of this magic. A Razak-Zinul creates works intended to outlive their maker, sometimes by centuries: faith in Mahal, mastery of matter, and knowledge of runes united within a single tradition.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Théurgie"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMSS","profession":"Razak-zinul","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling","description":"<h1>Razak-Zinul</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Razak-Zinul</strong>, literally <strong>“Forge-beard”</strong> in Khuzdul, is a pure <strong>Channeling</strong> spell-user found exclusively among the <strong>Khazâd</strong>. The Profession’s magic is directed toward craft, the making of weapons and armour, the enchantment of objects, analysis of artefacts, and protection through inscriptions and runes. The Razak-Zinul joins the spiritual tradition of the Dwarves to their exceptional mastery of matter.</p>\n<p>A Razak-Zinul of the <strong>House of Durin</strong> may work upon weapons, armour, and inscriptions inherited from Khazad-dûm; a craftsman of the <strong>Firebeards</strong> may place greater emphasis on forge-work and metals; a member of an eastern House may preserve a different tradition of engraving, stonework, or object-making.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> and <strong>Reasoning (Re)</strong>. Intuition is the realm stat of Channeling; Reasoning represents the technical understanding needed to design an object, analyse its structure, and organize the stages of complex manufacture.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul is a <strong>pure Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development particularly favours <strong>Craft</strong>, Base Spell Lists, magical and ancient lore, Power Point Development, Power Perception, selected technical skills, Influence, and Weapons.</p>\n<h2>Craft and Magic</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul regards magic as one component of <strong>work properly done</strong>. Power is integrated into metal, stone, wood, a weapon, armour, a mechanism, an inscription, or a protective symbol. Creation demands as much craftsmanship as magical ability.</p>\n<h2>Mahal and the Khazâd Tradition</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul’s Channeling is naturally understood through the Dwarves’ relationship with <strong>Aulë, Mahal</strong>. The Khazâd were created by him, and their exceptional affinity with stone, metal, and craft belongs directly to that heritage. The Razak-Zinul gives magical expression to this relationship.</p>\n<h2>Runes and Inscriptions</h2>\n<p>Spells embedded in an object are linked to an inscription or rune written in a specific language. The inscription must be read aloud to activate the power. A rune may be carved into a blade, hidden inside armour, cut into stone, worked into a jewel, or placed upon a door.</p>\n<h2>Weapons, Armour, and Matter</h2>\n<p><strong>Weaponsmith</strong> creates enchanted weapons. <strong>Armourer</strong> creates magical protections. <strong>Craftsman</strong> works with organic materials and mechanisms. <strong>Dwarven Inorganic Skills</strong> represents Khazâd lore concerning stone, metal, gems, and minerals.</p>\n<h2>Loremaster and Imbedding</h2>\n<p><strong>Loremaster</strong> allows the creation of general magical items and the analysis of objects of power. <strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong> fixes Channeling spells into objects. <strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong> permits work with a Magician, Lay Healer, or another spell-user in order to incorporate magic from a different realm.</p>\n<h2>Symbols and Wards</h2>\n<p><strong>Symbols</strong> creates inscriptions capable of holding spells. <strong>Wards</strong> protects people and places: doors, halls, workshops, treasuries, tombs, or important objects. In a Khazâd dwelling these protections may be combined with physical mechanisms and massive doors.</p>\n<h2>Nine Lists, Six Choices</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul has <strong>nine professional Lists</strong>, but the character chooses only <strong>six as Base Spell Lists</strong>. The remaining three become more difficult to develop. This rule forces an early choice about what kind of craftsman the character will become.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Master Smith.</strong> Weaponsmith, Armourer, and Dwarven Inorganic Skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Rune-master.</strong> Symbols, Wards, and Own Realm Imbedding.</p>\n<p><strong>Maker of Objects.</strong> Craftsman, Loremaster, and Imbedding techniques.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of the Gates.</strong> Wards, Symbols, Craft, and defensive mechanisms.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul chooses <strong>six Base Spell Lists from nine</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Armourer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Craftsman</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dwarven Inorganic Skills</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loremaster</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Other Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Own Realm Imbedding</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symbols</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Wards</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weaponsmith</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Secrecy and Transmission</h2>\n<p>The Dwarves guard their lore jealously. Enchanting techniques, runes, forging methods, and knowledge of certain materials may be restricted to a family, House, guild, or workshop. The choice of a master is therefore an important part of the character’s history.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Razak-Zinul has no complete direct professional equivalent among standard RMU Professions. We preserve the historical identity of a <strong>pure Khazâd Channeling spell-user specializing in magical craft</strong>, the Prime Stats <strong>Intuition and Reasoning</strong>, the restriction to the Khazâd, and the defining mechanism of <strong>nine available Lists of which only six become Base Spell Lists</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adaptation emphasizes the <strong>rare, secret, and enduring</strong> character of this magic. A Razak-Zinul creates works intended to outlive their maker, sometimes by centuries: faith in Mahal, mastery of matter, and knowledge of runes united within a single tradition.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"8/12"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"6/8"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"2/3"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"5/7"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"6/8"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"9/12"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"15/20"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Attunement"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","skillName":"Runes"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Spell Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","skillName":"Materials Lore"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","skillName":"Grace"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","skillName":"Power Development"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Metalcraft"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","skillName":"Stonecraft"},{"skillCategory":"Science","skillName":"Engineering"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Locks"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Crafting"}]},"_id":"dEHzL3WVX5GRAhIs"}
{"name":"Rogue","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"rogue","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Rogue; RMU-compatible adaptation"},"compendiumSourceKey":"rogue","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-non-magiques","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Roublard","system":{"description":"<h1>Roublard</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Roublard</strong> est un combattant polyvalent qui associe maîtrise des armes, discrétion, intrusion, tromperie et débrouillardise. Il se situe entre le Guerrier, spécialiste du combat, et le Voleur, spécialiste des activités clandestines.</p>\n<p>La Profession correspond à quelqu’un qui a appris à se battre et à résoudre les problèmes hors des cadres conventionnels. Un Corsaire d’Umbar peut mêler combat naval, intimidation et contrebande ; un Dunlending peut servir de guide ou de passeur ; un Dúnadan d’Arnor peut agir comme éclaireur ; un marchand Dorwinadan peut associer négociation, estimation et maniement des armes.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales du Roublard sont <strong>Agilité (Ag)</strong> et <strong>Force (St)</strong>. L’Agilité soutient précision, mobilité et discrétion ; la Force rappelle qu’il reste un véritable combattant.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Roublard est un <strong>non-utilisateur de sorts</strong>. Il partage son développement entre Armes, Discrétion, Subterfuge, Perception, aptitudes gymniques, Développement corporel, compétences urbaines et connaissances pratiques.</p>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>Le Roublard peut choisir une arme principale et atteindre un niveau très élevé avec elle. Un Corsaire d’Umbar peut privilégier une lame adaptée au pont d’un navire ; un Dunlending une hache ou une épée ; un Dorwinadan l’arc et une arme légère ; un agent du Gondor une lame facile à dissimuler.</p>\n<h2>Subterfuge et discrétion</h2>\n<p>Filature, dissimulation, infiltration, crochetage, manipulation de mécanismes, mensonge, escroquerie et contrebande peuvent entrer dans son domaine. Un espion d’Umbar peut infiltrer une cité ennemie ; un passeur dunlending évitera les patrouilles ; un agent du Gondor développera plutôt filature, observation et réseaux urbains.</p>\n<h2>Milieu urbain et réseaux</h2>\n<p>Le Roublard apprend à repérer les autorités réelles, les marchés clandestins, les intermédiaires et les lieux où disparaître. À Pelargir ou Umbar, il peut connaître les quais et les équipages ; en Eriador, son réseau peut reposer sur des aubergistes, des familles locales et des relais discrets.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur.</strong> Perception, Discrétion, déplacement et arme à distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Espion.</strong> Influence, Tromperie, Filature, Discrétion et arme discrète.</li>\n<li><strong>Contrebandier.</strong> Négociation, routes, dissimulation, équitation ou navigation.</li>\n<li><strong>Chasseur de primes.</strong> Recherche d’informations, combat, observation et intimidation.</li>\n<li><strong>Homme de confiance.</strong> Garde, collecteur, négociateur et résolution de problèmes.</li>\n<li><strong>Aventurier polyvalent.</strong> Équilibre entre combat, perception, discrétion et compétences sociales.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p><strong>Aucune.</strong></p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons de RMU une Profession martiale polyvalente située entre combattant et spécialiste du Subterfuge. MERP-RMU fixe explicitement <strong>Agilité et Force</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales et développe son rôle social et culturel.</p>\n<p>Le Roublard peut être espion d’Umbar, passeur dunlending, éclaireur dúnadan, marchand-aventurier dorwinadan ou homme de confiance. Son domaine est la polyvalence dans les situations où armes, discrétion et débrouillardise doivent être utilisées ensemble.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Rogue","system":{"description":"<h1>Rogue</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Rogue</strong> is a versatile combatant who combines solid weapon training with stealth, intrusion, deception, and resourcefulness. The Profession lies between the <strong>Fighter</strong>, a specialist in combat, and the <strong>Thief</strong>, a specialist in clandestine activities. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, this flexibility is the Rogue’s defining strength: a Rogue can approach a Fighter’s effectiveness with one chosen weapon while developing enough subterfuge to serve as a scout, spy, smuggler, or urban adventurer.</p>\n<p>A Rogue is not necessarily a criminal. The Profession represents someone who has learned to <strong>fight and survive outside conventional structures</strong>. A <strong>Corsair of Umbar</strong> may combine shipboard combat, intimidation, and smuggling; a <strong>Dunlending</strong> guide or ferryman may mix terrain knowledge, stealth, and weapons; a <strong>Dúnadan of Arnor</strong> serving as a scout may emphasize observation and infiltration; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> merchant travelling dangerous roads may develop negotiation, appraisal, and weapon skills.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Rogue’s Prime Stats are <strong>Agility (Ag)</strong> and <strong>Strength (St)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Agility</strong> reflects precision, stealthy movement, climbing, mechanical manipulation, and the finesse required by certain weapons. <strong>Strength</strong> matters far more to the Rogue than to the Thief: the Rogue is a genuine combatant.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Rogue is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. Development balances combat, physical abilities, and subterfuge.</p>\n<p>Favoured areas include <strong>Weapons</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, <strong>Subterfuge</strong>, <strong>Perception</strong> and searching, gymnastic skills, <strong>Body Development</strong>, urban skills, and selected technical or professional skills.</p>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>A Rogue can select a primary weapon and become extremely effective with it. In that one field the Rogue can come close to a Fighter, although the Profession cannot develop many different fighting styles with the same ease.</p>\n<p>A Dunlending may favour axe or sword; a Corsair of Umbar a blade suited to fighting on a ship’s deck; a Dorwinadan travelling with caravans a bow and a light melee weapon; an agent of Gondor a short sword or dagger that is easier to conceal.</p>\n<h2>Subterfuge and Stealth</h2>\n<p>The Rogue has excellent access to Subterfuge skills: shadowing, concealment, infiltration, lock-picking, mechanical manipulation, lying, fraud, smuggling, and knowledge of clandestine networks.</p>\n<p>A spy from <strong>Umbar</strong> may use these abilities to infiltrate an enemy city; a <strong>Dunlending</strong> smuggler to avoid patrols and conceal goods; in a great city of <strong>Gondor</strong>, an investigator or underworld figure may instead develop contacts, surveillance, and observation.</p>\n<h2>Urban Environments and Networks</h2>\n<p>The Rogue is particularly comfortable where information and relationships matter as much as force. In <strong>Pelargir</strong> or <strong>Umbar</strong>, this may mean knowing docks, crews, and traffickers. In a city of <strong>Gondor</strong>, it may involve contacts among artisans, guards, and guilds. In a small community of <strong>Eriador</strong>, the same abilities may take the form of a network of innkeepers, ferrymen, and local families.</p>\n<h2>Rogue and Thief</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Thief</strong> devotes a much larger share of development to infiltration, locks, traps, and other clandestine techniques. The <strong>Rogue</strong> spreads development more evenly between those areas and combat.</p>\n<p>Against a complex lock or a particularly difficult infiltration, a specialized Thief will usually retain the advantage. When the situation turns into open combat, the Rogue normally has superior martial training.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Stealth, movement, and a ranged weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Spy.</strong> Influence, Duping, Shadowing, Stealth, and an easily concealed weapon. Suitable for agents of Umbar, Angmar, Gondor, or the former realms of Arnor.</p>\n<p><strong>Smuggler.</strong> Negotiation, route knowledge, concealment, riding or sailing, and enough martial skill to defend a cargo.</p>\n<p><strong>Bounty Hunter.</strong> Tracking, information gathering, combat, observation, and intimidation.</p>\n<p><strong>Trusted Hand.</strong> Guard, collector, negotiator, and problem-solver when words alone are not enough.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Rogue has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magic remains an exceptional and costly direction of development.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a <strong>versatile martial Profession</strong> positioned between the dedicated fighter and the Subterfuge specialist. The Prime Stats are explicitly <strong>Agility and Strength</strong>. We also preserve the idea that a Rogue can become nearly as capable as a Fighter with <strong>one favoured weapon</strong> while retaining excellent access to Stealth and urban skills.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation strongly develops the Profession’s social and cultural dimension. A Rogue may be a spy of Umbar, a Dunlending smuggler, a Dúnadan scout, a Dorwinadan merchant-adventurer, a street guard, or the trusted agent of a noble house. The Rogue’s true domain is versatility in situations where weapons, stealth, and resourcefulness must be used together.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions non magiques"],"en":["Non-magical Professions"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-non-magiques"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP / RMSS — adaptation RMU","profession":"Rogue","spellCastingGroup":"Non","numberOfBaseLists":0,"realms":"","description":"<h1>Rogue</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Rogue</strong> is a versatile combatant who combines solid weapon training with stealth, intrusion, deception, and resourcefulness. The Profession lies between the <strong>Fighter</strong>, a specialist in combat, and the <strong>Thief</strong>, a specialist in clandestine activities. In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, this flexibility is the Rogue’s defining strength: a Rogue can approach a Fighter’s effectiveness with one chosen weapon while developing enough subterfuge to serve as a scout, spy, smuggler, or urban adventurer.</p>\n<p>A Rogue is not necessarily a criminal. The Profession represents someone who has learned to <strong>fight and survive outside conventional structures</strong>. A <strong>Corsair of Umbar</strong> may combine shipboard combat, intimidation, and smuggling; a <strong>Dunlending</strong> guide or ferryman may mix terrain knowledge, stealth, and weapons; a <strong>Dúnadan of Arnor</strong> serving as a scout may emphasize observation and infiltration; a <strong>Dorwinadan</strong> merchant travelling dangerous roads may develop negotiation, appraisal, and weapon skills.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Rogue’s Prime Stats are <strong>Agility (Ag)</strong> and <strong>Strength (St)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Agility</strong> reflects precision, stealthy movement, climbing, mechanical manipulation, and the finesse required by certain weapons. <strong>Strength</strong> matters far more to the Rogue than to the Thief: the Rogue is a genuine combatant.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Rogue is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. Development balances combat, physical abilities, and subterfuge.</p>\n<p>Favoured areas include <strong>Weapons</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, <strong>Subterfuge</strong>, <strong>Perception</strong> and searching, gymnastic skills, <strong>Body Development</strong>, urban skills, and selected technical or professional skills.</p>\n<h2>Combat</h2>\n<p>A Rogue can select a primary weapon and become extremely effective with it. In that one field the Rogue can come close to a Fighter, although the Profession cannot develop many different fighting styles with the same ease.</p>\n<p>A Dunlending may favour axe or sword; a Corsair of Umbar a blade suited to fighting on a ship’s deck; a Dorwinadan travelling with caravans a bow and a light melee weapon; an agent of Gondor a short sword or dagger that is easier to conceal.</p>\n<h2>Subterfuge and Stealth</h2>\n<p>The Rogue has excellent access to Subterfuge skills: shadowing, concealment, infiltration, lock-picking, mechanical manipulation, lying, fraud, smuggling, and knowledge of clandestine networks.</p>\n<p>A spy from <strong>Umbar</strong> may use these abilities to infiltrate an enemy city; a <strong>Dunlending</strong> smuggler to avoid patrols and conceal goods; in a great city of <strong>Gondor</strong>, an investigator or underworld figure may instead develop contacts, surveillance, and observation.</p>\n<h2>Urban Environments and Networks</h2>\n<p>The Rogue is particularly comfortable where information and relationships matter as much as force. In <strong>Pelargir</strong> or <strong>Umbar</strong>, this may mean knowing docks, crews, and traffickers. In a city of <strong>Gondor</strong>, it may involve contacts among artisans, guards, and guilds. In a small community of <strong>Eriador</strong>, the same abilities may take the form of a network of innkeepers, ferrymen, and local families.</p>\n<h2>Rogue and Thief</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Thief</strong> devotes a much larger share of development to infiltration, locks, traps, and other clandestine techniques. The <strong>Rogue</strong> spreads development more evenly between those areas and combat.</p>\n<p>Against a complex lock or a particularly difficult infiltration, a specialized Thief will usually retain the advantage. When the situation turns into open combat, the Rogue normally has superior martial training.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Stealth, movement, and a ranged weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Spy.</strong> Influence, Duping, Shadowing, Stealth, and an easily concealed weapon. Suitable for agents of Umbar, Angmar, Gondor, or the former realms of Arnor.</p>\n<p><strong>Smuggler.</strong> Negotiation, route knowledge, concealment, riding or sailing, and enough martial skill to defend a cargo.</p>\n<p><strong>Bounty Hunter.</strong> Tracking, information gathering, combat, observation, and intimidation.</p>\n<p><strong>Trusted Hand.</strong> Guard, collector, negotiator, and problem-solver when words alone are not enough.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Rogue has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magic remains an exceptional and costly direction of development.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s concept of a <strong>versatile martial Profession</strong> positioned between the dedicated fighter and the Subterfuge specialist. The Prime Stats are explicitly <strong>Agility and Strength</strong>. We also preserve the idea that a Rogue can become nearly as capable as a Fighter with <strong>one favoured weapon</strong> while retaining excellent access to Stealth and urban skills.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation strongly develops the Profession’s social and cultural dimension. A Rogue may be a spy of Umbar, a Dunlending smuggler, a Dúnadan scout, a Dorwinadan merchant-adventurer, a street guard, or the trusted agent of a noble house. The Rogue’s true domain is versatility in situations where weapons, stealth, and resourcefulness must be used together.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"1/3"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"2/3"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"2/3"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"3/5"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"3/5"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Observation"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Climbing"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running"},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Deception"},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Trading"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Hiding"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Locks"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Traps"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Trade"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Melee Weapons"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Ranged Weapons"}]},"_id":"UQ5gHIhlG4RydY0W"}
{"name":"Seer","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"seer","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Mentalist chassis; MERP/RMSS Seer p.149; Mentalism Companion pp.121-126"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"seer","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Mentalist","document":{"name":"Devin","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"seer","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Mentalist chassis; MERP/RMSS Seer p.149; Mentalism Companion pp.121-126"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / Mentalism Companion","profession":"Seer","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Mentalism","description":"<h1>Devin</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Devin</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la recherche d’informations par les moyens de l’esprit. Sa magie explore le passé, observe le présent, anticipe certaines possibilités futures et étend les capacités sensorielles.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, le Devin représente rarement un prophète connaissant l’avenir avec certitude. Ses visions prennent plutôt la forme d’images fragmentaires, de perceptions lointaines, de souvenirs réveillés et de pressentiments. Un Dúnadan peut conserver une tradition issue de Númenor ; un conseiller du Gondor l’utiliser pour l’enquête et l’analyse ; un praticien d’Umbar pour l’espionnage.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Autodiscipline soutient concentration et contrôle des visions ; la Présence est la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme et gouverne projection de conscience et puissance psychique.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Devin favorise Listes de base, Perception, Recherche, connaissances, Perception de la Puissance, Manipulation de la Puissance, Autocontrôle et compétences sociales utiles à l’interprétation des comportements.</p>\n<h2>Passé, présent et avenir</h2>\n<p>Les visions du passé peuvent révéler des fragments liés à une personne, un lieu ou un objet. Les visions du présent étendent les sens ou sondent certaines informations. Les visions futures apportent tendances, dangers, symboles et possibilités plutôt qu’une certitude absolue.</p>\n<h2>Interpréter les visions</h2>\n<p>La magie fournit l’information brute ; le savoir du personnage lui donne son sens. Un Dúnadan connaissant l’histoire d’Arnor identifiera plus facilement une scène des royaumes du Nord ; un savant du Gondor reconnaîtra un symbole númenóréen ; un Dorwinadan comprendra mieux les routes et peuples de l’Est.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Visions Lointaines (Far Visions).</strong> Étend et modifie les capacités sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Futur (Future Visions).</strong> Précognition et pressentiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions de l’Esprit (Mind Visions).</strong> Détection mentale, perception du mensonge et sondage psychique.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Passé (Past Visions).</strong> Évoque le passé des personnes, lieux et objets.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Empruntée (Vision Borrowing).</strong> Partage ou utilise les sens d’autres êtres.</li>\n<li><strong>Garde des Visions (Vision Guard).</strong> Protège contre scrutation, évocation et divination.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Dangers de la connaissance</h2>\n<p>Observer le passé d’un lieu marqué par Sauron, sonder l’esprit d’un serviteur de l’Ombre ou chercher une forteresse ennemie peut exposer le Devin à des informations traumatisantes et au <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le Devin comme <strong>pur Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline et Présence</strong> et les six Listes traditionnelles.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU insiste sur la nature fragmentaire et interprétable des visions, la disponibilité réelle des enseignements et le risque lié à la recherche d’informations concernant les grandes puissances de l’Ombre.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Devin","system":{"description":"<h1>Devin</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Devin</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la recherche d’informations par les moyens de l’esprit. Sa magie explore le passé, observe le présent, anticipe certaines possibilités futures et étend les capacités sensorielles.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, le Devin représente rarement un prophète connaissant l’avenir avec certitude. Ses visions prennent plutôt la forme d’images fragmentaires, de perceptions lointaines, de souvenirs réveillés et de pressentiments. Un Dúnadan peut conserver une tradition issue de Númenor ; un conseiller du Gondor l’utiliser pour l’enquête et l’analyse ; un praticien d’Umbar pour l’espionnage.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Autodiscipline soutient concentration et contrôle des visions ; la Présence est la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme et gouverne projection de conscience et puissance psychique.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Devin favorise Listes de base, Perception, Recherche, connaissances, Perception de la Puissance, Manipulation de la Puissance, Autocontrôle et compétences sociales utiles à l’interprétation des comportements.</p>\n<h2>Passé, présent et avenir</h2>\n<p>Les visions du passé peuvent révéler des fragments liés à une personne, un lieu ou un objet. Les visions du présent étendent les sens ou sondent certaines informations. Les visions futures apportent tendances, dangers, symboles et possibilités plutôt qu’une certitude absolue.</p>\n<h2>Interpréter les visions</h2>\n<p>La magie fournit l’information brute ; le savoir du personnage lui donne son sens. Un Dúnadan connaissant l’histoire d’Arnor identifiera plus facilement une scène des royaumes du Nord ; un savant du Gondor reconnaîtra un symbole númenóréen ; un Dorwinadan comprendra mieux les routes et peuples de l’Est.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Visions Lointaines (Far Visions).</strong> Étend et modifie les capacités sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Futur (Future Visions).</strong> Précognition et pressentiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions de l’Esprit (Mind Visions).</strong> Détection mentale, perception du mensonge et sondage psychique.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Passé (Past Visions).</strong> Évoque le passé des personnes, lieux et objets.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Empruntée (Vision Borrowing).</strong> Partage ou utilise les sens d’autres êtres.</li>\n<li><strong>Garde des Visions (Vision Guard).</strong> Protège contre scrutation, évocation et divination.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Dangers de la connaissance</h2>\n<p>Observer le passé d’un lieu marqué par Sauron, sonder l’esprit d’un serviteur de l’Ombre ou chercher une forteresse ennemie peut exposer le Devin à des informations traumatisantes et au <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le Devin comme <strong>pur Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline et Présence</strong> et les six Listes traditionnelles.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU insiste sur la nature fragmentaire et interprétable des visions, la disponibilité réelle des enseignements et le risque lié à la recherche d’informations concernant les grandes puissances de l’Ombre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Seer","system":{"description":"<h1>Seer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in the gathering of information through the powers of the mind. The Profession’s magic explores the past, observes the present, anticipates certain possibilities of the future, and extends the character’s sensory abilities.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Seer is rarely a prophet who knows the future with certainty. Visions are more often fragmented images, distant perceptions, awakened memories, premonitions, and information obtained through concentration. A <strong>Dúnadan</strong> Seer may inherit a tradition preserved since Númenor; an adviser of <strong>Gondor</strong> may use these talents to verify information; a practitioner in <strong>Umbar</strong> may employ the same powers for espionage.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Seer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Self Discipline represents the concentration required to sustain a vision; Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism and governs projection of the mind.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Seer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Perception, Research, Lore, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Self Control, and social skills useful for interpreting behaviour.</p>\n<h2>Seeing Beyond Ordinary Senses</h2>\n<p>The Seer can enhance personal senses, perceive through the senses of another creature, or examine a scene outside immediate sight. A Seer travelling with the Dúnedain of the North may observe a dangerous passage; in Minas Tirith, discreetly watch a location; in Umbar, serve as an instrument of espionage between rival houses.</p>\n<h2>The Past</h2>\n<p><strong>Past Visions</strong> recovers fragments of earlier events. A Seer examining a blade found in the ruins of Arnor may perceive important moments in its history; on an old battlefield of Gondor, the Seer may try to understand how a unit vanished. Such visions are best kept partial and contextual.</p>\n<h2>The Present</h2>\n<p><strong>Mind Visions</strong> provides mental detection, perception of lies, and certain forms of psychic probing. These abilities are valuable in investigation and diplomacy. An adviser of Gondor may try to determine whether a messenger is concealing something; a Seer of Umbar may employ the same techniques more intrusively during interrogation.</p>\n<h2>The Future</h2>\n<p><strong>Future Visions</strong> provides premonitions and fragments connected with events yet to come. In Middle-earth, this form of divination retains uncertainty: a road strewn with bodies, a tower wrapped in flame, the face of a companion in danger, or a symbol whose meaning remains obscure.</p>\n<h2>Borrowed Vision and Defences</h2>\n<p><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong> allows the Seer to share or use the senses of other beings. <strong>Vision Guard</strong> protects against evocation, scrying, and divination. The Seer therefore becomes both observer and magical counter-intelligence specialist.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting Visions</h2>\n<p>Receiving a vision is only the first step. Lore, Research, and Perception allow the character to understand what was seen. A Dúnadan Seer familiar with the history of Arnor will more readily interpret a scene connected with the old northern kingdoms; a scholar of Gondor may recognize Númenórean symbols; a Dorwinadan Seer may possess useful knowledge of eastern peoples.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Visionary.</strong> Future Visions and Self Control.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychometrist.</strong> Past Visions together with the history of objects and places.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Spy.</strong> Mind Visions, Vision Borrowing, and social skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Adviser.</strong> Divination, lore, Influence, and interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigator.</strong> Research, Perception, Past Visions, and mental detection.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Seer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Future Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Past Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Guard</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Knowledge</h2>\n<p>Some information carries its own danger. Observing the past of a place marked by Morgoth or Sauron, probing the mind of a servant of the Shadow, or attempting to scry an enemy fortress may expose the Seer to traumatic images or attract the attention of whatever is being observed. Such uses therefore interact with the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the Seer as a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in information gathering</strong>, with <strong>Self Discipline and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. The six traditional Lists structure the Profession around past, present, future, perception, and counter-divination.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly affects the nature of the information obtained: fragmented visions, the past shown through meaningful scenes, and scrying that remains limited and dangerous. The Seer’s true talent lies as much in understanding what is seen as in obtaining the vision itself.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"seer","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-pures-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Devin","system":{"description":"<h1>Devin</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Devin</strong> est un utilisateur pur du <strong>Mentalisme</strong>, spécialisé dans la recherche d’informations par les moyens de l’esprit. Sa magie explore le passé, observe le présent, anticipe certaines possibilités futures et étend les capacités sensorielles.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, le Devin représente rarement un prophète connaissant l’avenir avec certitude. Ses visions prennent plutôt la forme d’images fragmentaires, de perceptions lointaines, de souvenirs réveillés et de pressentiments. Un Dúnadan peut conserver une tradition issue de Númenor ; un conseiller du Gondor l’utiliser pour l’enquête et l’analyse ; un praticien d’Umbar pour l’espionnage.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong> et <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong>. L’Autodiscipline soutient concentration et contrôle des visions ; la Présence est la caractéristique de royaume du Mentalisme et gouverne projection de conscience et puissance psychique.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Devin favorise Listes de base, Perception, Recherche, connaissances, Perception de la Puissance, Manipulation de la Puissance, Autocontrôle et compétences sociales utiles à l’interprétation des comportements.</p>\n<h2>Passé, présent et avenir</h2>\n<p>Les visions du passé peuvent révéler des fragments liés à une personne, un lieu ou un objet. Les visions du présent étendent les sens ou sondent certaines informations. Les visions futures apportent tendances, dangers, symboles et possibilités plutôt qu’une certitude absolue.</p>\n<h2>Interpréter les visions</h2>\n<p>La magie fournit l’information brute ; le savoir du personnage lui donne son sens. Un Dúnadan connaissant l’histoire d’Arnor identifiera plus facilement une scène des royaumes du Nord ; un savant du Gondor reconnaîtra un symbole númenóréen ; un Dorwinadan comprendra mieux les routes et peuples de l’Est.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Visions Lointaines (Far Visions).</strong> Étend et modifie les capacités sensorielles.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Futur (Future Visions).</strong> Précognition et pressentiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions de l’Esprit (Mind Visions).</strong> Détection mentale, perception du mensonge et sondage psychique.</li>\n<li><strong>Visions du Passé (Past Visions).</strong> Évoque le passé des personnes, lieux et objets.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision Empruntée (Vision Borrowing).</strong> Partage ou utilise les sens d’autres êtres.</li>\n<li><strong>Garde des Visions (Vision Guard).</strong> Protège contre scrutation, évocation et divination.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Dangers de la connaissance</h2>\n<p>Observer le passé d’un lieu marqué par Sauron, sonder l’esprit d’un serviteur de l’Ombre ou chercher une forteresse ennemie peut exposer le Devin à des informations traumatisantes et au <strong>Risque d’attirer l’Ombre</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le Devin comme <strong>pur Mentalisme</strong>, avec <strong>Autodiscipline et Présence</strong> et les six Listes traditionnelles.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU insiste sur la nature fragmentaire et interprétable des visions, la disponibilité réelle des enseignements et le risque lié à la recherche d’informations concernant les grandes puissances de l’Ombre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Seer","system":{"description":"<h1>Seer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in the gathering of information through the powers of the mind. The Profession’s magic explores the past, observes the present, anticipates certain possibilities of the future, and extends the character’s sensory abilities.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Seer is rarely a prophet who knows the future with certainty. Visions are more often fragmented images, distant perceptions, awakened memories, premonitions, and information obtained through concentration. A <strong>Dúnadan</strong> Seer may inherit a tradition preserved since Númenor; an adviser of <strong>Gondor</strong> may use these talents to verify information; a practitioner in <strong>Umbar</strong> may employ the same powers for espionage.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Seer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Self Discipline represents the concentration required to sustain a vision; Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism and governs projection of the mind.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Seer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Perception, Research, Lore, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Self Control, and social skills useful for interpreting behaviour.</p>\n<h2>Seeing Beyond Ordinary Senses</h2>\n<p>The Seer can enhance personal senses, perceive through the senses of another creature, or examine a scene outside immediate sight. A Seer travelling with the Dúnedain of the North may observe a dangerous passage; in Minas Tirith, discreetly watch a location; in Umbar, serve as an instrument of espionage between rival houses.</p>\n<h2>The Past</h2>\n<p><strong>Past Visions</strong> recovers fragments of earlier events. A Seer examining a blade found in the ruins of Arnor may perceive important moments in its history; on an old battlefield of Gondor, the Seer may try to understand how a unit vanished. Such visions are best kept partial and contextual.</p>\n<h2>The Present</h2>\n<p><strong>Mind Visions</strong> provides mental detection, perception of lies, and certain forms of psychic probing. These abilities are valuable in investigation and diplomacy. An adviser of Gondor may try to determine whether a messenger is concealing something; a Seer of Umbar may employ the same techniques more intrusively during interrogation.</p>\n<h2>The Future</h2>\n<p><strong>Future Visions</strong> provides premonitions and fragments connected with events yet to come. In Middle-earth, this form of divination retains uncertainty: a road strewn with bodies, a tower wrapped in flame, the face of a companion in danger, or a symbol whose meaning remains obscure.</p>\n<h2>Borrowed Vision and Defences</h2>\n<p><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong> allows the Seer to share or use the senses of other beings. <strong>Vision Guard</strong> protects against evocation, scrying, and divination. The Seer therefore becomes both observer and magical counter-intelligence specialist.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting Visions</h2>\n<p>Receiving a vision is only the first step. Lore, Research, and Perception allow the character to understand what was seen. A Dúnadan Seer familiar with the history of Arnor will more readily interpret a scene connected with the old northern kingdoms; a scholar of Gondor may recognize Númenórean symbols; a Dorwinadan Seer may possess useful knowledge of eastern peoples.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Visionary.</strong> Future Visions and Self Control.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychometrist.</strong> Past Visions together with the history of objects and places.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Spy.</strong> Mind Visions, Vision Borrowing, and social skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Adviser.</strong> Divination, lore, Influence, and interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigator.</strong> Research, Perception, Past Visions, and mental detection.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Seer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Future Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Past Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Guard</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Knowledge</h2>\n<p>Some information carries its own danger. Observing the past of a place marked by Morgoth or Sauron, probing the mind of a servant of the Shadow, or attempting to scry an enemy fortress may expose the Seer to traumatic images or attract the attention of whatever is being observed. Such uses therefore interact with the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the Seer as a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in information gathering</strong>, with <strong>Self Discipline and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. The six traditional Lists structure the Profession around past, present, future, perception, and counter-divination.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly affects the nature of the information obtained: fragmented visions, the past shown through meaningful scenes, and scrying that remains limited and dangerous. The Seer’s true talent lies as much in understanding what is seen as in obtaining the vision itself.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Magiques Pures","Mentalisme"],"en":["Pure Spell-user Professions","Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-magiques-pures","professions-pures-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / Mentalism Companion","profession":"Seer","spellCastingGroup":"Pure","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Mentalism","description":"<h1>Seer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Seer</strong> is a pure <strong>Mentalism</strong> spell-user specializing in the gathering of information through the powers of the mind. The Profession’s magic explores the past, observes the present, anticipates certain possibilities of the future, and extends the character’s sensory abilities.</p>\n<p>In Middle-earth, the Seer is rarely a prophet who knows the future with certainty. Visions are more often fragmented images, distant perceptions, awakened memories, premonitions, and information obtained through concentration. A <strong>Dúnadan</strong> Seer may inherit a tradition preserved since Númenor; an adviser of <strong>Gondor</strong> may use these talents to verify information; a practitioner in <strong>Umbar</strong> may employ the same powers for espionage.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Seer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong> and <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>. Self Discipline represents the concentration required to sustain a vision; Presence is the realm stat of Mentalism and governs projection of the mind.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Seer is a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Perception, Research, Lore, Power Perception, Power Manipulation, Self Control, and social skills useful for interpreting behaviour.</p>\n<h2>Seeing Beyond Ordinary Senses</h2>\n<p>The Seer can enhance personal senses, perceive through the senses of another creature, or examine a scene outside immediate sight. A Seer travelling with the Dúnedain of the North may observe a dangerous passage; in Minas Tirith, discreetly watch a location; in Umbar, serve as an instrument of espionage between rival houses.</p>\n<h2>The Past</h2>\n<p><strong>Past Visions</strong> recovers fragments of earlier events. A Seer examining a blade found in the ruins of Arnor may perceive important moments in its history; on an old battlefield of Gondor, the Seer may try to understand how a unit vanished. Such visions are best kept partial and contextual.</p>\n<h2>The Present</h2>\n<p><strong>Mind Visions</strong> provides mental detection, perception of lies, and certain forms of psychic probing. These abilities are valuable in investigation and diplomacy. An adviser of Gondor may try to determine whether a messenger is concealing something; a Seer of Umbar may employ the same techniques more intrusively during interrogation.</p>\n<h2>The Future</h2>\n<p><strong>Future Visions</strong> provides premonitions and fragments connected with events yet to come. In Middle-earth, this form of divination retains uncertainty: a road strewn with bodies, a tower wrapped in flame, the face of a companion in danger, or a symbol whose meaning remains obscure.</p>\n<h2>Borrowed Vision and Defences</h2>\n<p><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong> allows the Seer to share or use the senses of other beings. <strong>Vision Guard</strong> protects against evocation, scrying, and divination. The Seer therefore becomes both observer and magical counter-intelligence specialist.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting Visions</h2>\n<p>Receiving a vision is only the first step. Lore, Research, and Perception allow the character to understand what was seen. A Dúnadan Seer familiar with the history of Arnor will more readily interpret a scene connected with the old northern kingdoms; a scholar of Gondor may recognize Númenórean symbols; a Dorwinadan Seer may possess useful knowledge of eastern peoples.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Visionary.</strong> Future Visions and Self Control.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychometrist.</strong> Past Visions together with the history of objects and places.</p>\n<p><strong>Mental Spy.</strong> Mind Visions, Vision Borrowing, and social skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Adviser.</strong> Divination, lore, Influence, and interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigator.</strong> Research, Perception, Past Visions, and mental detection.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Seer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Far Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Future Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Past Visions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Borrowing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vision Guard</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Risk and Knowledge</h2>\n<p>Some information carries its own danger. Observing the past of a place marked by Morgoth or Sauron, probing the mind of a servant of the Shadow, or attempting to scry an enemy fortress may expose the Seer to traumatic images or attract the attention of whatever is being observed. Such uses therefore interact with the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the Seer as a <strong>pure Mentalism spell-user specializing in information gathering</strong>, with <strong>Self Discipline and Presence</strong> as Prime Stats. The six traditional Lists structure the Profession around past, present, future, perception, and counter-divination.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly affects the nature of the information obtained: fragmented visions, the past shown through meaningful scenes, and scrying that remains limited and dangerous. The Seer’s true talent lies as much in understanding what is seen as in obtaining the vision itself.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"SAHjNg77NK4PNLPY"}
{"name":"Sorcerer","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"sorcerer","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"RMU Sorcerer chassis and base lists; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Sorcerer (MERP), p.155"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"sorcerer","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Sorcerer","document":{"name":"Sorcier","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"sorcerer","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-plus-merp-editorial","reference":"RMU Sorcerer chassis and base lists; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Sorcerer (MERP), p.155"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Sorcier","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Essence","description":"<h1>Sorcier</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Sorcier</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>l’Essence et de la Théurgie</strong>, spécialisé dans les formes les plus destructrices de la magie. Sa puissance vise directement la structure de la matière, du corps, de l’esprit et de l’âme, jusqu’à leur destruction.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession est liée à l’<strong>Élément de Morgoth</strong>. Ses six Listes de base sont des formes de <strong>Sorcellerie</strong>. Un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar peut hériter de savoirs corrompus ; un mage d’Angmar recevoir l’enseignement de serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier ; des cultes secrets du Harad ou de Khand transmettre des pratiques apparentées.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient le contrôle intérieur face à une magie violemment corruptrice.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier est un <strong>hybride Essence / Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques, Perception de la Puissance et Sorts dirigés.</p>\n<h2>Une magie de destruction</h2>\n<p>Ses Listes couvrent chair vivante, fluides, gaz, processus mentaux, matières solides et âme. Cette organisation donne une polyvalence destructrice exceptionnelle et permet d’attaquer directement la nature de la cible plutôt que de passer uniquement par des manifestations élémentaires.</p>\n<h2>Sorcellerie et Élément de Morgoth</h2>\n<p>Les six Listes sont classées comme <strong>Sorcellerie</strong> dans MERP-RMU. Elles cherchent à déformer ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda et sont directement concernées par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier commence traditionnellement avec une corruption liée aux Listes de Sorcellerie déjà apprises. Dans MERP-RMU, leur utilisation déclenche les mécanismes correspondants. Le doublement spécifique des points d’Ombre/Corruption associés à la Sorcellerie doit être automatisé dans le module.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Destruction de la Chair (Flesh Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou dégrade les tissus vivants.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Fluides (Fluid Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou manipule les liquides.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Gaz (Gas Destruction).</strong> Élimine, raréfie ou manipule les gaz.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Esprit (Mind Destruction).</strong> Attaque douleur, mémoire, pensée et facultés psychiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Solides (Solid Destruction).</strong> Fissure, dégrade ou détruit terre, bois, pierre, métal et structures.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Âme (Soul Destruction).</strong> Attaque directement l’esprit et l’essence spirituelle des êtres.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, ces pratiques seraient condamnées ou fortement surveillées ; à Umbar, certaines lignées de Númenóréens Noirs peuvent les conserver ; en Angmar, elles peuvent être enseignées directement par les serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier. Leur simple apprentissage doit toujours avoir une histoire et une source.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis Sorcerer RMU</strong>, hybride Essence/Théurgie, et ses six Listes de destruction. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>L’adaptation la plus importante est métaphysique : les six Listes sont explicitement reliées à l’Élément de Morgoth, classées comme Sorcellerie et intégrées aux mécanismes de Corruption et de Risque d’attirer l’Ombre.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Sorcier","system":{"description":"<h1>Sorcier</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Sorcier</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>l’Essence et de la Théurgie</strong>, spécialisé dans les formes les plus destructrices de la magie. Sa puissance vise directement la structure de la matière, du corps, de l’esprit et de l’âme, jusqu’à leur destruction.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession est liée à l’<strong>Élément de Morgoth</strong>. Ses six Listes de base sont des formes de <strong>Sorcellerie</strong>. Un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar peut hériter de savoirs corrompus ; un mage d’Angmar recevoir l’enseignement de serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier ; des cultes secrets du Harad ou de Khand transmettre des pratiques apparentées.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient le contrôle intérieur face à une magie violemment corruptrice.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier est un <strong>hybride Essence / Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques, Perception de la Puissance et Sorts dirigés.</p>\n<h2>Une magie de destruction</h2>\n<p>Ses Listes couvrent chair vivante, fluides, gaz, processus mentaux, matières solides et âme. Cette organisation donne une polyvalence destructrice exceptionnelle et permet d’attaquer directement la nature de la cible plutôt que de passer uniquement par des manifestations élémentaires.</p>\n<h2>Sorcellerie et Élément de Morgoth</h2>\n<p>Les six Listes sont classées comme <strong>Sorcellerie</strong> dans MERP-RMU. Elles cherchent à déformer ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda et sont directement concernées par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier commence traditionnellement avec une corruption liée aux Listes de Sorcellerie déjà apprises. Dans MERP-RMU, leur utilisation déclenche les mécanismes correspondants. Le doublement spécifique des points d’Ombre/Corruption associés à la Sorcellerie doit être automatisé dans le module.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Destruction de la Chair (Flesh Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou dégrade les tissus vivants.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Fluides (Fluid Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou manipule les liquides.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Gaz (Gas Destruction).</strong> Élimine, raréfie ou manipule les gaz.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Esprit (Mind Destruction).</strong> Attaque douleur, mémoire, pensée et facultés psychiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Solides (Solid Destruction).</strong> Fissure, dégrade ou détruit terre, bois, pierre, métal et structures.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Âme (Soul Destruction).</strong> Attaque directement l’esprit et l’essence spirituelle des êtres.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, ces pratiques seraient condamnées ou fortement surveillées ; à Umbar, certaines lignées de Númenóréens Noirs peuvent les conserver ; en Angmar, elles peuvent être enseignées directement par les serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier. Leur simple apprentissage doit toujours avoir une histoire et une source.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis Sorcerer RMU</strong>, hybride Essence/Théurgie, et ses six Listes de destruction. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>L’adaptation la plus importante est métaphysique : les six Listes sont explicitement reliées à l’Élément de Morgoth, classées comme Sorcellerie et intégrées aux mécanismes de Corruption et de Risque d’attirer l’Ombre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Sorcerer","system":{"description":"<h1>Sorcerer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Sorcerer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Essence / Channeling</strong> spell-user specializing in the most destructive forms of magic. The Profession’s Lists attack <strong>matter, body, mind, and soul</strong> directly. In Middle-earth, the Profession is explicitly connected with the <strong>Morgoth Element</strong>: all six Base Spell Lists are treated as forms of <strong>Sorcery</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may have inherited corrupted lore reaching back to Sauron; a human mage serving <strong>Angmar</strong> may have learned to destroy the bodies or minds of enemies; secret cults in <strong>Harad</strong> or <strong>Khand</strong> may preserve practices akin to Sorcery.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the inner control required by profoundly dangerous magic.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer is a <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Power Perception, and Directed Spells.</p>\n<h2>Magic of Destruction</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous magic rests on the idea that anything can be destroyed if its nature is understood deeply enough. The Profession’s Lists cover living flesh, liquids, gases, mental processes, solid matter, and the soul. This specialization makes the Sorcerer extraordinarily versatile in destruction: the caster can target the structure of body, matter, or mind itself.</p>\n<h2>Sorcery and the Morgoth Element</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, all six Sorcerer Base Lists are classified as <strong>Sorcery</strong>. They draw power from the Morgoth Element and interact directly with the rules for <strong>Corruption</strong>.</p>\n<p>The moral purpose of an individual act may vary, but the nature of the magic remains corrupting. Destroying the flesh of an Orc to save a companion employs the same force that could be used to torture a prisoner.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer traditionally begins with a degree of Corruption linked to Sorcery Lists already learned. In our adaptation, Sorcery spells systematically trigger the appropriate Corruption mechanics and worsen the consequences of failure.</p>\n<p>Earlier versions doubled Corruption Points received when a spell or List was classified as Sorcery. This specific mechanism is intended to be automated in MERP-UI.</p>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer should remain rare. In <strong>Gondor</strong>, open study of such practices would be condemned or closely watched. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, some Black Númenórean lineages may preserve much more permissive traditions. In <strong>Angmar</strong>, Sorcery may be taught directly by servants of the Witch-king. In parts of Harad or Khand, secret cults may also transmit such powers.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Destroyer of Flesh.</strong> Powers that attack living organisms directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Siege Sorcerer.</strong> Destruction of stone, wood, metal, and structures.</p>\n<p><strong>Mind-breaker.</strong> Mental destruction, pain, and disorientation.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Destroyer.</strong> Attacks against souls and supernatural entities.</p>\n<p><strong>Repentant Sorcerer.</strong> Forbidden knowledge used reluctantly; every use of power carries a moral risk.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flesh Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fluid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gas Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Solid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Soul Destruction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All six are treated as <strong>Sorcery Lists</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Learning and Transmission</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous knowledge is rarely taught openly. A character may discover a corrupted Númenórean grimoire in the ruins of Umbar, receive instruction from a servant of the Witch-king in Angmar, or uncover a secret tradition within a cult of Harad. The origin of the character’s Lists should be an integral part of the story.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling chassis</strong> and the six destruction Base Lists. We explicitly restore the three Prime Stats <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly changes the <strong>nature of the magic itself</strong>: all Lists are connected to the Morgoth Element, classified as Sorcery, and subject to Corruption and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>. The Sorcerer gains extraordinary power at the price of a permanent relationship with magic that intrinsically bears the mark of the Enemy.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"sorcerer","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-hybrides-essence-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Sorcier","system":{"description":"<h1>Sorcier</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Sorcier</strong> est un utilisateur hybride de <strong>l’Essence et de la Théurgie</strong>, spécialisé dans les formes les plus destructrices de la magie. Sa puissance vise directement la structure de la matière, du corps, de l’esprit et de l’âme, jusqu’à leur destruction.</p>\n<p>Dans la Terre du Milieu, cette Profession est liée à l’<strong>Élément de Morgoth</strong>. Ses six Listes de base sont des formes de <strong>Sorcellerie</strong>. Un Númenóréen Noir d’Umbar peut hériter de savoirs corrompus ; un mage d’Angmar recevoir l’enseignement de serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier ; des cultes secrets du Harad ou de Khand transmettre des pratiques apparentées.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient le contrôle intérieur face à une magie violemment corruptrice.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier est un <strong>hybride Essence / Théurgie</strong>. Il favorise Listes de base, Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques, Perception de la Puissance et Sorts dirigés.</p>\n<h2>Une magie de destruction</h2>\n<p>Ses Listes couvrent chair vivante, fluides, gaz, processus mentaux, matières solides et âme. Cette organisation donne une polyvalence destructrice exceptionnelle et permet d’attaquer directement la nature de la cible plutôt que de passer uniquement par des manifestations élémentaires.</p>\n<h2>Sorcellerie et Élément de Morgoth</h2>\n<p>Les six Listes sont classées comme <strong>Sorcellerie</strong> dans MERP-RMU. Elles cherchent à déformer ou détruire l’ordre naturel d’Arda et sont directement concernées par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>Le Sorcier commence traditionnellement avec une corruption liée aux Listes de Sorcellerie déjà apprises. Dans MERP-RMU, leur utilisation déclenche les mécanismes correspondants. Le doublement spécifique des points d’Ombre/Corruption associés à la Sorcellerie doit être automatisé dans le module.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Destruction de la Chair (Flesh Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou dégrade les tissus vivants.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Fluides (Fluid Destruction).</strong> Détruit ou manipule les liquides.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Gaz (Gas Destruction).</strong> Élimine, raréfie ou manipule les gaz.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Esprit (Mind Destruction).</strong> Attaque douleur, mémoire, pensée et facultés psychiques.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction des Solides (Solid Destruction).</strong> Fissure, dégrade ou détruit terre, bois, pierre, métal et structures.</li>\n<li><strong>Destruction de l’Âme (Soul Destruction).</strong> Attaque directement l’esprit et l’essence spirituelle des êtres.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>Dans le Gondor, ces pratiques seraient condamnées ou fortement surveillées ; à Umbar, certaines lignées de Númenóréens Noirs peuvent les conserver ; en Angmar, elles peuvent être enseignées directement par les serviteurs du Roi-Sorcier. Leur simple apprentissage doit toujours avoir une histoire et une source.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons le <strong>châssis Sorcerer RMU</strong>, hybride Essence/Théurgie, et ses six Listes de destruction. MERP-RMU rétablit <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales.</p>\n<p>L’adaptation la plus importante est métaphysique : les six Listes sont explicitement reliées à l’Élément de Morgoth, classées comme Sorcellerie et intégrées aux mécanismes de Corruption et de Risque d’attirer l’Ombre.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Sorcerer","system":{"description":"<h1>Sorcerer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Sorcerer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Essence / Channeling</strong> spell-user specializing in the most destructive forms of magic. The Profession’s Lists attack <strong>matter, body, mind, and soul</strong> directly. In Middle-earth, the Profession is explicitly connected with the <strong>Morgoth Element</strong>: all six Base Spell Lists are treated as forms of <strong>Sorcery</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may have inherited corrupted lore reaching back to Sauron; a human mage serving <strong>Angmar</strong> may have learned to destroy the bodies or minds of enemies; secret cults in <strong>Harad</strong> or <strong>Khand</strong> may preserve practices akin to Sorcery.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the inner control required by profoundly dangerous magic.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer is a <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Power Perception, and Directed Spells.</p>\n<h2>Magic of Destruction</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous magic rests on the idea that anything can be destroyed if its nature is understood deeply enough. The Profession’s Lists cover living flesh, liquids, gases, mental processes, solid matter, and the soul. This specialization makes the Sorcerer extraordinarily versatile in destruction: the caster can target the structure of body, matter, or mind itself.</p>\n<h2>Sorcery and the Morgoth Element</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, all six Sorcerer Base Lists are classified as <strong>Sorcery</strong>. They draw power from the Morgoth Element and interact directly with the rules for <strong>Corruption</strong>.</p>\n<p>The moral purpose of an individual act may vary, but the nature of the magic remains corrupting. Destroying the flesh of an Orc to save a companion employs the same force that could be used to torture a prisoner.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer traditionally begins with a degree of Corruption linked to Sorcery Lists already learned. In our adaptation, Sorcery spells systematically trigger the appropriate Corruption mechanics and worsen the consequences of failure.</p>\n<p>Earlier versions doubled Corruption Points received when a spell or List was classified as Sorcery. This specific mechanism is intended to be automated in MERP-UI.</p>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer should remain rare. In <strong>Gondor</strong>, open study of such practices would be condemned or closely watched. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, some Black Númenórean lineages may preserve much more permissive traditions. In <strong>Angmar</strong>, Sorcery may be taught directly by servants of the Witch-king. In parts of Harad or Khand, secret cults may also transmit such powers.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Destroyer of Flesh.</strong> Powers that attack living organisms directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Siege Sorcerer.</strong> Destruction of stone, wood, metal, and structures.</p>\n<p><strong>Mind-breaker.</strong> Mental destruction, pain, and disorientation.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Destroyer.</strong> Attacks against souls and supernatural entities.</p>\n<p><strong>Repentant Sorcerer.</strong> Forbidden knowledge used reluctantly; every use of power carries a moral risk.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flesh Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fluid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gas Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Solid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Soul Destruction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All six are treated as <strong>Sorcery Lists</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Learning and Transmission</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous knowledge is rarely taught openly. A character may discover a corrupted Númenórean grimoire in the ruins of Umbar, receive instruction from a servant of the Witch-king in Angmar, or uncover a secret tradition within a cult of Harad. The origin of the character’s Lists should be an integral part of the story.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling chassis</strong> and the six destruction Base Lists. We explicitly restore the three Prime Stats <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly changes the <strong>nature of the magic itself</strong>: all Lists are connected to the Morgoth Element, classified as Sorcery, and subject to Corruption and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>. The Sorcerer gains extraordinary power at the price of a permanent relationship with magic that intrinsically bears the mark of the Enemy.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Hybrides","Essence + Théurgie"],"en":["Hybrid Spell-user Professions","Essence + Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-hybrides","professions-hybrides-essence-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Sorcier","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Essence","description":"<h1>Sorcerer</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Sorcerer</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Essence / Channeling</strong> spell-user specializing in the most destructive forms of magic. The Profession’s Lists attack <strong>matter, body, mind, and soul</strong> directly. In Middle-earth, the Profession is explicitly connected with the <strong>Morgoth Element</strong>: all six Base Spell Lists are treated as forms of <strong>Sorcery</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Black Númenórean of Umbar</strong> may have inherited corrupted lore reaching back to Sauron; a human mage serving <strong>Angmar</strong> may have learned to destroy the bodies or minds of enemies; secret cults in <strong>Harad</strong> or <strong>Khand</strong> may preserve practices akin to Sorcery.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the inner control required by profoundly dangerous magic.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer is a <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Power Perception, and Directed Spells.</p>\n<h2>Magic of Destruction</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous magic rests on the idea that anything can be destroyed if its nature is understood deeply enough. The Profession’s Lists cover living flesh, liquids, gases, mental processes, solid matter, and the soul. This specialization makes the Sorcerer extraordinarily versatile in destruction: the caster can target the structure of body, matter, or mind itself.</p>\n<h2>Sorcery and the Morgoth Element</h2>\n<p>In MERP-RMU, all six Sorcerer Base Lists are classified as <strong>Sorcery</strong>. They draw power from the Morgoth Element and interact directly with the rules for <strong>Corruption</strong>.</p>\n<p>The moral purpose of an individual act may vary, but the nature of the magic remains corrupting. Destroying the flesh of an Orc to save a companion employs the same force that could be used to torture a prisoner.</p>\n<h2>Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer traditionally begins with a degree of Corruption linked to Sorcery Lists already learned. In our adaptation, Sorcery spells systematically trigger the appropriate Corruption mechanics and worsen the consequences of failure.</p>\n<p>Earlier versions doubled Corruption Points received when a spell or List was classified as Sorcery. This specific mechanism is intended to be automated in MERP-UI.</p>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer should remain rare. In <strong>Gondor</strong>, open study of such practices would be condemned or closely watched. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, some Black Númenórean lineages may preserve much more permissive traditions. In <strong>Angmar</strong>, Sorcery may be taught directly by servants of the Witch-king. In parts of Harad or Khand, secret cults may also transmit such powers.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Destroyer of Flesh.</strong> Powers that attack living organisms directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Siege Sorcerer.</strong> Destruction of stone, wood, metal, and structures.</p>\n<p><strong>Mind-breaker.</strong> Mental destruction, pain, and disorientation.</p>\n<p><strong>Spiritual Destroyer.</strong> Attacks against souls and supernatural entities.</p>\n<p><strong>Repentant Sorcerer.</strong> Forbidden knowledge used reluctantly; every use of power carries a moral risk.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Sorcerer has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flesh Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fluid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gas Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mind Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Solid Destruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Soul Destruction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All six are treated as <strong>Sorcery Lists</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Learning and Transmission</h2>\n<p>Sorcerous knowledge is rarely taught openly. A character may discover a corrupted Númenórean grimoire in the ruins of Umbar, receive instruction from a servant of the Witch-king in Angmar, or uncover a secret tradition within a cult of Harad. The origin of the character’s Lists should be an integral part of the story.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain RMU’s <strong>hybrid Essence / Channeling chassis</strong> and the six destruction Base Lists. We explicitly restore the three Prime Stats <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation chiefly changes the <strong>nature of the magic itself</strong>: all Lists are connected to the Morgoth Element, classified as Sorcery, and subject to Corruption and the <strong>Risk of Attracting the Shadow</strong>. The Sorcerer gains extraordinary power at the price of a permanent relationship with magic that intrinsically bears the mark of the Enemy.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"Skc4u2D4o07MhsMT"}
{"name":"Thief","type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"thief","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"merp-rmss-to-rmu-adaptation","reference":"MERP Character Creation Using RMSS/RMFRP v2.5 — Thief; RMU-compatible adaptation"},"compendiumSourceKey":"thief","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-non-magiques","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Voleur","system":{"description":"<h1>Voleur — ou Cambrioleur</h1>\n<p>Le <strong>Voleur</strong>, parfois appelé <strong>Cambrioleur</strong>, est un spécialiste de l’intrusion, de la discrétion et de la manipulation. Il pousse plus loin que le Roublard les compétences permettant d’entrer dans un lieu protégé, observer sans être vu, neutraliser les obstacles mécaniques et repartir sans laisser de trace.</p>\n<p>Le terme Cambrioleur convient particulièrement à la Terre du Milieu : la Profession peut représenter un véritable criminel, mais aussi un éclaireur, un récupérateur, un espion, un chercheur de trésors, un spécialiste des serrures ou un aventurier chargé de pénétrer dans des lieux dangereux.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales du Voleur sont <strong>Agilité (Ag)</strong> et <strong>Rapidité (Qu)</strong>. L’Agilité gouverne les gestes précis ; la Rapidité permet de profiter d’une ouverture, agir avant d’être repéré et disparaître lorsque la situation se dégrade.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>Le Voleur est un <strong>non-utilisateur de sorts</strong>. Il privilégie Subterfuge, Discrétion, Serrures, Pièges, Perception, Recherche, aptitudes gymniques, compétences urbaines et armes adaptées à son mode d’action.</p>\n<h2>Intrusion et mécanique</h2>\n<p>Serrures, pièges, coffres, portes secrètes et mécanismes dissimulés constituent son domaine emblématique. À Minas Tirith, il peut connaître les réserves et passages de service des grandes maisons ; à Umbar, les entrepôts et coffres marchands ; dans les ruines d’Arnor, il appliquera les mêmes compétences à des portes anciennes et mécanismes oubliés.</p>\n<h2>Discrétion</h2>\n<p>Le Voleur sait utiliser ombres et couvert, attendre le bon moment, suivre une cible et se dissimuler rapidement. À Pelargir, cela peut prendre la forme de filatures sur les quais ; chez un Hobbit, d’une discrétion naturelle et d’une connaissance précise des chemins secondaires ; chez un espion dúnadan, d’une infiltration en territoire surveillé.</p>\n<h2>Combat et armure</h2>\n<p>Le Voleur sait se battre mais privilégie les armes légères ou faciles à transporter : dague, épée courte, gourdin, arc court, fronde ou armes de jet. Un Corsaire d’Umbar peut toutefois adopter une lame plus lourde selon sa Culture. Les protections légères préservent mieux mobilité, escalade et discrétion.</p>\n<h2>Voleur et Roublard</h2>\n<p>Le Roublard recherche l’équilibre entre combat et subterfuge. Le Voleur se spécialise dans les serrures, pièges, infiltrations et opérations clandestines. Lors d’un affrontement prolongé, le Roublard possède généralement l’avantage ; face à une serrure complexe ou une mission de discrétion, le Voleur doit rester le meilleur spécialiste.</p>\n<h2>Profils possibles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cambrioleur.</strong> Serrures, Discrétion, Perception, Escalade et recherche de pièges.</li>\n<li><strong>Voleur à la tire.</strong> Observation, discrétion, manipulation et connaissance des foules.</li>\n<li><strong>Espion.</strong> Discrétion, Serrures, Perception, Influence et déguisement.</li>\n<li><strong>Pilleur de ruines.</strong> Pièges, serrures, recherche, escalade et connaissances techniques.</li>\n<li><strong>Receleur.</strong> Contacts, évaluation, négociation et compétences urbaines.</li>\n<li><strong>Éclaireur clandestin.</strong> Furtivité, perception, déplacement et arme à distance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<p><strong>Aucune.</strong></p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>Nous conservons de RMU le spécialiste du Subterfuge et des compétences techniques. MERP-RMU rétablit explicitement <strong>Agilité et Rapidité</strong> comme caractéristiques primordiales et maintient une distinction forte avec le Roublard.</p>\n<p>La Profession dépasse le simple criminel : Hobbit cambrioleur, espion dúnadan, spécialiste des ruines d’Arnor, voleur des quais d’Umbar ou aventurier chargé de franchir les obstacles que le groupe ne peut résoudre ouvertement.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Thief","system":{"description":"<h1>Thief — or Burglar</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Thief</strong>, sometimes called a <strong>Burglar</strong>, is a specialist in intrusion, stealth, and manipulation. Where the Rogue balances combat and subterfuge, the Thief develops much further the skills needed to enter places where one should not be, observe without being seen, overcome mechanical obstacles, and leave without a trace.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Thief is a non-spell-user particularly gifted with mechanical skills such as lock-picking and trap disarming, highly capable in stealth, and sufficiently trained with weapons to defend himself without becoming a front-line fighter.</p>\n<p>The word <strong>Burglar</strong> is especially appropriate in Middle-earth. It describes the heart of the Profession without automatically implying criminality. A Thief may indeed be a professional criminal, but may equally be a scout, retriever, spy, treasure-seeker, locksmith, or adventurer whose task is to penetrate dangerous places.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Thief’s Prime Stats are <strong>Agility (Ag)</strong> and <strong>Quickness (Qu)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Agility</strong> is essential for precise actions: working a lock, disarming a mechanism, climbing, moving silently, or acting in confined spaces. <strong>Quickness</strong> represents the ability to react immediately, exploit an opening, vanish before being noticed, or escape a situation that has become dangerous.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Thief is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. Development strongly favours <strong>Subterfuge</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, mechanical skills, <strong>Perception</strong> and searching, gymnastic skills, urban abilities, and light weapons suited to clandestine action.</p>\n<h2>Intrusion and Mechanics</h2>\n<p>The Thief’s most emblematic field is mechanical intrusion: locks, traps, locking devices, hidden mechanisms, strongboxes, secret doors, and technical systems.</p>\n<p>A Burglar working in <strong>Minas Tirith</strong> may know the complex locks and service passages of the great houses. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, the same specialist may be familiar with merchants’ chests, harbour warehouses, and shipboard locking systems. In the ruined cities of <strong>Arnor</strong>, an adventurer may use those skills to explore ancient sites and force long-sealed doors.</p>\n<h2>Stealth</h2>\n<p>The Thief knows how to move without attracting attention, use shadows and cover, wait for the right moment, observe a target’s habits, hide quickly, and follow someone without being noticed.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Pelargir</strong>, this may mean shadowing someone along the docks; for a <strong>Hobbit</strong> of Eriador, it may reflect natural quietness and intimate knowledge of side paths; for a <strong>Dúnadan</strong> spy, it may be used to cross watched territory or observe an enemy position.</p>\n<h2>The Thief and Society</h2>\n<p>A Thief may be a professional burglar, a member of an urban underworld, a spy, a ruin-explorer, or a retriever. In Umbar, the Profession may work with smugglers and Corsair captains; in the cities of Gondor, belong to a criminal network or serve as an intelligence agent; in Eriador, explore old Númenórean ruins.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Armour</h2>\n<p>The Thief can fight, but combat remains secondary. Daggers, short swords, clubs, short bows, slings, and thrown weapons are common choices. These are not absolute restrictions: a Corsair of Umbar may employ a heavier blade, while a Hobbit burglar will favour very different arms.</p>\n<p>Heavy armour naturally interferes with mobility and infiltration. Leather, reinforced clothing, and light protection therefore suit the Profession better.</p>\n<h2>Criminal Networks and Fences</h2>\n<p>Technical skill is not always enough. A professional must identify worthwhile targets, assess the value of an object, find a buyer, and understand whom it is safer not to rob. In Umbar, such a network may include fences and merchants tied to Corsair crews; in Pelargir, dockworkers, taverns, and harbour intermediaries; in Eriador, a handful of accommodating innkeepers and merchants.</p>\n<h2>Thief and Rogue</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Rogue</strong> seeks balance: fight, infiltrate, improvise. The <strong>Thief</strong> is a specialist: observe, enter, manipulate, disappear. Against a difficult lock, trap, or infiltration, the Thief should normally have the advantage; in a prolonged fight, the Rogue is better prepared.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Burglar.</strong> Locks, traps, Stealth, climbing, and appraisal.</p>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Stealth, movement, and a ranged weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Spy.</strong> Shadowing, Influence, Stealth, observation, and an easily concealed weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Ruin Delver.</strong> Searching, mechanics, traps, climbing, and lore useful for old sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Urban Thief.</strong> Contacts, Stealth, locks, appraisal, and knowledge of streets and markets.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Thief has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magical development remains exceptional and costly.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the traditional MERP/Rolemaster concept of the Thief as a <strong>specialist in clandestine and mechanical skills</strong>. The Prime Stats are explicitly <strong>Agility and Quickness</strong>. The Profession remains more focused than the Rogue: less capable in open combat, but superior in intrusion, traps, mechanisms, and stealth.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation emphasizes the term <strong>Burglar</strong> and the diversity of possible roles. The Profession may be criminal, exploratory, military, or practical depending on Culture and circumstances. Its identity lies not in crime itself, but in the ability to enter, observe, manipulate, recover, and escape.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions non magiques"],"en":["Non-magical Professions"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-non-magiques"],"compendiumResolverBacked":false}},"system":{"book":"MERP / RMSS — adaptation RMU","profession":"Thief","spellCastingGroup":"Non","numberOfBaseLists":0,"realms":"","description":"<h1>Thief — or Burglar</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Thief</strong>, sometimes called a <strong>Burglar</strong>, is a specialist in intrusion, stealth, and manipulation. Where the Rogue balances combat and subterfuge, the Thief develops much further the skills needed to enter places where one should not be, observe without being seen, overcome mechanical obstacles, and leave without a trace.</p>\n<p>In earlier Middle-earth and Rolemaster games, the Thief is a non-spell-user particularly gifted with mechanical skills such as lock-picking and trap disarming, highly capable in stealth, and sufficiently trained with weapons to defend himself without becoming a front-line fighter.</p>\n<p>The word <strong>Burglar</strong> is especially appropriate in Middle-earth. It describes the heart of the Profession without automatically implying criminality. A Thief may indeed be a professional criminal, but may equally be a scout, retriever, spy, treasure-seeker, locksmith, or adventurer whose task is to penetrate dangerous places.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Thief’s Prime Stats are <strong>Agility (Ag)</strong> and <strong>Quickness (Qu)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Agility</strong> is essential for precise actions: working a lock, disarming a mechanism, climbing, moving silently, or acting in confined spaces. <strong>Quickness</strong> represents the ability to react immediately, exploit an opening, vanish before being noticed, or escape a situation that has become dangerous.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Thief is a <strong>non-spell-user</strong>. Development strongly favours <strong>Subterfuge</strong>, <strong>Stealth</strong>, mechanical skills, <strong>Perception</strong> and searching, gymnastic skills, urban abilities, and light weapons suited to clandestine action.</p>\n<h2>Intrusion and Mechanics</h2>\n<p>The Thief’s most emblematic field is mechanical intrusion: locks, traps, locking devices, hidden mechanisms, strongboxes, secret doors, and technical systems.</p>\n<p>A Burglar working in <strong>Minas Tirith</strong> may know the complex locks and service passages of the great houses. In <strong>Umbar</strong>, the same specialist may be familiar with merchants’ chests, harbour warehouses, and shipboard locking systems. In the ruined cities of <strong>Arnor</strong>, an adventurer may use those skills to explore ancient sites and force long-sealed doors.</p>\n<h2>Stealth</h2>\n<p>The Thief knows how to move without attracting attention, use shadows and cover, wait for the right moment, observe a target’s habits, hide quickly, and follow someone without being noticed.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Pelargir</strong>, this may mean shadowing someone along the docks; for a <strong>Hobbit</strong> of Eriador, it may reflect natural quietness and intimate knowledge of side paths; for a <strong>Dúnadan</strong> spy, it may be used to cross watched territory or observe an enemy position.</p>\n<h2>The Thief and Society</h2>\n<p>A Thief may be a professional burglar, a member of an urban underworld, a spy, a ruin-explorer, or a retriever. In Umbar, the Profession may work with smugglers and Corsair captains; in the cities of Gondor, belong to a criminal network or serve as an intelligence agent; in Eriador, explore old Númenórean ruins.</p>\n<h2>Combat and Armour</h2>\n<p>The Thief can fight, but combat remains secondary. Daggers, short swords, clubs, short bows, slings, and thrown weapons are common choices. These are not absolute restrictions: a Corsair of Umbar may employ a heavier blade, while a Hobbit burglar will favour very different arms.</p>\n<p>Heavy armour naturally interferes with mobility and infiltration. Leather, reinforced clothing, and light protection therefore suit the Profession better.</p>\n<h2>Criminal Networks and Fences</h2>\n<p>Technical skill is not always enough. A professional must identify worthwhile targets, assess the value of an object, find a buyer, and understand whom it is safer not to rob. In Umbar, such a network may include fences and merchants tied to Corsair crews; in Pelargir, dockworkers, taverns, and harbour intermediaries; in Eriador, a handful of accommodating innkeepers and merchants.</p>\n<h2>Thief and Rogue</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Rogue</strong> seeks balance: fight, infiltrate, improvise. The <strong>Thief</strong> is a specialist: observe, enter, manipulate, disappear. Against a difficult lock, trap, or infiltration, the Thief should normally have the advantage; in a prolonged fight, the Rogue is better prepared.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Burglar.</strong> Locks, traps, Stealth, climbing, and appraisal.</p>\n<p><strong>Scout.</strong> Perception, Stealth, movement, and a ranged weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Spy.</strong> Shadowing, Influence, Stealth, observation, and an easily concealed weapon.</p>\n<p><strong>Ruin Delver.</strong> Searching, mechanics, traps, climbing, and lore useful for old sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Urban Thief.</strong> Contacts, Stealth, locks, appraisal, and knowledge of streets and markets.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p><strong>None.</strong></p>\n<p>The Thief has no professional Base Spell Lists. Magical development remains exceptional and costly.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>We retain the traditional MERP/Rolemaster concept of the Thief as a <strong>specialist in clandestine and mechanical skills</strong>. The Prime Stats are explicitly <strong>Agility and Quickness</strong>. The Profession remains more focused than the Rogue: less capable in open combat, but superior in intrusion, traps, mechanisms, and stealth.</p>\n<p>The Middle-earth adaptation emphasizes the term <strong>Burglar</strong> and the diversity of possible roles. The Profession may be criminal, exploratory, military, or practical depending on Culture and circumstances. Its identity lies not in crime itself, but in the ability to enter, observe, manipulate, recover, and escape.</p>","skillDevelopmentCosts":[{"skillCategory":"Animal","cost":"2/3"},{"skillCategory":"Awareness","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Battle Expertise","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Body Discipline","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Brawn","cost":"4/6"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Expertise","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Composition","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Crafting","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Delving","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Environmental","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Lore","cost":"3/5"},{"skillCategory":"Magical Expertise","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Medical","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Mental Discipline","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","cost":"1/3"},{"skillCategory":"Performance Art","cost":"3/4"},{"skillCategory":"Power Manipulation","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Science","cost":"5/7"},{"skillCategory":"Social","cost":"2/4"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Base","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Open","cost":"7/10"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Magical Ritual","cost":"9/12"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Arcane","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Closed","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Spellcasting","skillName":"Restricted","cost":"15/20"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","cost":"1/1"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","cost":"1/2"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","cost":"1/2"}],"combatTrainingCosts":[{"trainingGroup":"Melee Weapons","cost":"2/4"},{"trainingGroup":"Ranged Weapons","cost":"3/5"},{"trainingGroup":"Shield","cost":"4/6"},{"trainingGroup":"Unarmed","cost":"3/5"}],"professionalSkills":[{"skillCategory":"Awareness","skillName":"Observation"},{"skillCategory":"Gymnastic","skillName":"Climbing"},{"skillCategory":"Movement","skillName":"Running"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Stalking"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Hiding"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Locks"},{"skillCategory":"Subterfuge","skillName":"Pick Pockets"},{"skillCategory":"Technical","skillName":"Traps"},{"skillCategory":"Vocation","skillName":"Trade"},{"skillCategory":"Social","skillName":"Deception"},{"skillCategory":"Combat Training","skillName":"Melee Weapons"}]},"_id":"brN002vUinm0BW72"}
{"name":"Vracara","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"vracara","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Vracara, p.156"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"vracara","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Sorcerer","document":{"name":"Vracara","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"vracara","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Vracara, p.156"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Vracara","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Vracara</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong>, propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle appartient au sacerdoce féminin de Tûmrakhí, Seigneur des Ténèbres, et exerce une magie mêlant guérison, discipline mentale et canalisation d’une puissance spirituelle liée à son culte.</p>\n<p>Plusieurs sectes de Vracarathí représentent différents aspects de Tûmrakhí. MERP-RMU utilise comme référence la <strong>secte Odchí</strong>, dont l’enseignement associe cinq domaines de guérison à une Liste de canalisation sombre. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence sont les caractéristiques des deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient méditation, concentration et discipline sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Divination, Influence, activités de plein air et certaines compétences martiales.</p>\n<h2>La secte Odchí</h2>\n<p>Son enseignement couvre sang, os, muscles, nerfs, organes et canalisation de la puissance de Tûmrakhí. La guérison constitue donc le cœur de sa pratique, tandis que la sixième Liste révèle sa fonction sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Vracara et Soigneur</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un pur Mentalisme spécialisé dans la réparation du corps. La Vracara est une hybride dont toute la guérison s’inscrit dans une tradition sacerdotale. Deux effets mécaniquement proches peuvent donc avoir une signification culturelle et morale radicalement différente.</p>\n<h2>Canaux Sombres et Corruption</h2>\n<p><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels)</strong> exprime directement le lien avec Tûmrakhí. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement corruptrices ; les manifestations reçues d’une puissance liée à l’Ombre, l’usage de Sorcellerie ou l’apprentissage de Listes maléfiques entraînent les conséquences prévues par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite blessures et pertes de sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Traite les dommages musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Nerfs (Nerve Law).</strong> Traite les lésions nerveuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Organes (Organ Law).</strong> Traite les blessures des organes internes.</li>\n<li><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance provenant de Tûmrakhí.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Autres sectes</h2>\n<p>D’autres sectes peuvent conserver le même statut hybride, les mêmes caractéristiques primordiales, coûts et restrictions culturelles tout en remplaçant plusieurs Listes de base. Cette structure permet d’étendre ultérieurement les traditions variags sans créer une nouvelle Profession à chaque fois.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est reconstruite autour d’un <strong>châssis hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes variags.</p>\n<p>RMU moderne regroupe certaines techniques de guérison ; MERP-RMU préserve <strong>Loi des Nerfs</strong> et <strong>Loi des Organes</strong> comme deux spécialisations distinctes et conserve toute la dimension religieuse de Canaux Sombres.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Vracara","system":{"description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Vracara</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong>, propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle appartient au sacerdoce féminin de Tûmrakhí, Seigneur des Ténèbres, et exerce une magie mêlant guérison, discipline mentale et canalisation d’une puissance spirituelle liée à son culte.</p>\n<p>Plusieurs sectes de Vracarathí représentent différents aspects de Tûmrakhí. MERP-RMU utilise comme référence la <strong>secte Odchí</strong>, dont l’enseignement associe cinq domaines de guérison à une Liste de canalisation sombre. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence sont les caractéristiques des deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient méditation, concentration et discipline sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Divination, Influence, activités de plein air et certaines compétences martiales.</p>\n<h2>La secte Odchí</h2>\n<p>Son enseignement couvre sang, os, muscles, nerfs, organes et canalisation de la puissance de Tûmrakhí. La guérison constitue donc le cœur de sa pratique, tandis que la sixième Liste révèle sa fonction sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Vracara et Soigneur</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un pur Mentalisme spécialisé dans la réparation du corps. La Vracara est une hybride dont toute la guérison s’inscrit dans une tradition sacerdotale. Deux effets mécaniquement proches peuvent donc avoir une signification culturelle et morale radicalement différente.</p>\n<h2>Canaux Sombres et Corruption</h2>\n<p><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels)</strong> exprime directement le lien avec Tûmrakhí. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement corruptrices ; les manifestations reçues d’une puissance liée à l’Ombre, l’usage de Sorcellerie ou l’apprentissage de Listes maléfiques entraînent les conséquences prévues par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite blessures et pertes de sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Traite les dommages musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Nerfs (Nerve Law).</strong> Traite les lésions nerveuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Organes (Organ Law).</strong> Traite les blessures des organes internes.</li>\n<li><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance provenant de Tûmrakhí.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Autres sectes</h2>\n<p>D’autres sectes peuvent conserver le même statut hybride, les mêmes caractéristiques primordiales, coûts et restrictions culturelles tout en remplaçant plusieurs Listes de base. Cette structure permet d’étendre ultérieurement les traditions variags sans créer une nouvelle Profession à chaque fois.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est reconstruite autour d’un <strong>châssis hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes variags.</p>\n<p>RMU moderne regroupe certaines techniques de guérison ; MERP-RMU préserve <strong>Loi des Nerfs</strong> et <strong>Loi des Organes</strong> comme deux spécialisations distinctes et conserve toute la dimension religieuse de Canaux Sombres.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Vracara","system":{"description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Vracara</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She belongs to the female priesthood of <strong>Tûmrakhí, Lord of Darkness</strong>, and practices a magic combining healing, mental discipline, and the channeling of spiritual power directly linked to the cult she serves.</p>\n<p>Several Vracarathí sects represent different aspects of Tûmrakhí. They share a common general training, but their <strong>Base Spell Lists differ by sect</strong>. MERP-RMU uses the <strong>Odchí sect</strong> as its reference: five fields of healing and one List that channels the power of the deity. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Vracara’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition corresponds to Channeling, Presence to Mentalism, and Self Discipline to the control required by demanding religious practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Religion, Divination, Influence, outdoor activities, Meditation, and selected martial skills.</p>\n<h2>The Priesthood of Tûmrakhí</h2>\n<p>The Vracara knows the rites, taboos, prayers, meditative forms, symbols, and obligations of her sect. The <strong>Odchí sect</strong> gives great importance to the body: five of its six Lists concern the direct healing of injuries. Healer and priestess are two aspects of the same character.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood Law</strong> treats bleeding; <strong>Bone Law</strong> fractures; <strong>Muscle Law</strong> muscular injuries; <strong>Nerve Law</strong> nerve damage; <strong>Organ Law</strong> internal wounds. This anatomical division gives the Odchí tradition a very precise specialization.</p>\n<h2>Dark Channels</h2>\n<p>The sixth List is <strong>Dark Channels</strong>. It channels power originating with Tûmrakhí and directly expresses the relationship with the cult. Its use must always be understood in the context of the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Shadow and Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Vracara explicitly serves a Lord of Darkness. The healing Lists can be treated according to their own magical nature; <strong>Dark Channels</strong> requires different attention. Power deliberately received from an entity associated with the Shadow may have much greater spiritual consequences. Learning Evil Lists or Sorcery produces the Corruption effects established by the general rules.</p>\n<h2>Faith and Interpretation</h2>\n<p>A Vracara may be devoutly loyal to her sect, primarily a healer devoted to her people, pragmatic, dissident, or ambitious. The same mechanics can therefore support radically different moral paths.</p>\n<h2>Life among the Variags</h2>\n<p>The Profession is adapted to the Variag way of life. A Vracara must be able to follow a mobile community, treat injuries far from permanent settlements, and function under the conditions of Khand. Riding, Survival, regional lore, First Aid, and culturally appropriate weapons all fit naturally.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Odchí Healer.</strong> Five bodily healing Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Priestess of Tûmrakhí.</strong> Dark Channels, Religion, Influence, and Divination.</p>\n<p><strong>War Healer.</strong> Healing, Riding, weapons, and fieldcraft.</p>\n<p><strong>Clan Adviser.</strong> Influence, Divination, cultural lore, and supporting magic.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident Priestess.</strong> Preserves healing and traditional knowledge while questioning the cult’s darkest obligations.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>For the <strong>Odchí sect</strong>, the Vracara has six Base Spell Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Organ Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dark Channels</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Other Sects</h2>\n<p>Other Vracarathí sects may replace some or all of these Lists according to the aspect of Tûmrakhí they serve. Their existence allows a Game Master to create distinct priestly traditions without changing the fundamental Profession.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara has no direct standard equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>, preserve <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, retain the restriction to Variag women, and use the Odchí sect as the standard six-List package.</p>\n<p>The adaptation emphasizes the tension between genuine healing skill and service to a power linked to the Shadow. Dark Channels, Evil Lists, and Sorcery remain subject to the MERP-RMU rules for Corruption and the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"vracara","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Vracara","system":{"description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Vracara</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Mentalisme et de Théurgie</strong>, propre aux Variags de Khand. Elle appartient au sacerdoce féminin de Tûmrakhí, Seigneur des Ténèbres, et exerce une magie mêlant guérison, discipline mentale et canalisation d’une puissance spirituelle liée à son culte.</p>\n<p>Plusieurs sectes de Vracarathí représentent différents aspects de Tûmrakhí. MERP-RMU utilise comme référence la <strong>secte Odchí</strong>, dont l’enseignement associe cinq domaines de guérison à une Liste de canalisation sombre. La Profession est réservée aux <strong>femmes de Culture variag</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Présence (Pr)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition et Présence sont les caractéristiques des deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient méditation, concentration et discipline sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est une <strong>hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Perception et Manipulation de la Puissance, Développement des Points de Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, Divination, Influence, activités de plein air et certaines compétences martiales.</p>\n<h2>La secte Odchí</h2>\n<p>Son enseignement couvre sang, os, muscles, nerfs, organes et canalisation de la puissance de Tûmrakhí. La guérison constitue donc le cœur de sa pratique, tandis que la sixième Liste révèle sa fonction sacerdotale.</p>\n<h2>Vracara et Soigneur</h2>\n<p>Le Soigneur est un pur Mentalisme spécialisé dans la réparation du corps. La Vracara est une hybride dont toute la guérison s’inscrit dans une tradition sacerdotale. Deux effets mécaniquement proches peuvent donc avoir une signification culturelle et morale radicalement différente.</p>\n<h2>Canaux Sombres et Corruption</h2>\n<p><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels)</strong> exprime directement le lien avec Tûmrakhí. Les Listes de guérison ne sont pas automatiquement corruptrices ; les manifestations reçues d’une puissance liée à l’Ombre, l’usage de Sorcellerie ou l’apprentissage de Listes maléfiques entraînent les conséquences prévues par les règles de Corruption.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loi du Sang (Blood Law).</strong> Traite blessures et pertes de sang.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Os (Bone Law).</strong> Traite fractures et lésions osseuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Muscles (Muscle Law).</strong> Traite les dommages musculaires.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Nerfs (Nerve Law).</strong> Traite les lésions nerveuses.</li>\n<li><strong>Loi des Organes (Organ Law).</strong> Traite les blessures des organes internes.</li>\n<li><strong>Canaux Sombres (Dark Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance provenant de Tûmrakhí.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Autres sectes</h2>\n<p>D’autres sectes peuvent conserver le même statut hybride, les mêmes caractéristiques primordiales, coûts et restrictions culturelles tout en remplaçant plusieurs Listes de base. Cette structure permet d’étendre ultérieurement les traditions variags sans créer une nouvelle Profession à chaque fois.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Vracara est reconstruite autour d’un <strong>châssis hybride Mentalisme / Théurgie</strong>, avec <strong>Intuition, Présence et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes variags.</p>\n<p>RMU moderne regroupe certaines techniques de guérison ; MERP-RMU préserve <strong>Loi des Nerfs</strong> et <strong>Loi des Organes</strong> comme deux spécialisations distinctes et conserve toute la dimension religieuse de Canaux Sombres.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Vracara","system":{"description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Vracara</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She belongs to the female priesthood of <strong>Tûmrakhí, Lord of Darkness</strong>, and practices a magic combining healing, mental discipline, and the channeling of spiritual power directly linked to the cult she serves.</p>\n<p>Several Vracarathí sects represent different aspects of Tûmrakhí. They share a common general training, but their <strong>Base Spell Lists differ by sect</strong>. MERP-RMU uses the <strong>Odchí sect</strong> as its reference: five fields of healing and one List that channels the power of the deity. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Vracara’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition corresponds to Channeling, Presence to Mentalism, and Self Discipline to the control required by demanding religious practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Religion, Divination, Influence, outdoor activities, Meditation, and selected martial skills.</p>\n<h2>The Priesthood of Tûmrakhí</h2>\n<p>The Vracara knows the rites, taboos, prayers, meditative forms, symbols, and obligations of her sect. The <strong>Odchí sect</strong> gives great importance to the body: five of its six Lists concern the direct healing of injuries. Healer and priestess are two aspects of the same character.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood Law</strong> treats bleeding; <strong>Bone Law</strong> fractures; <strong>Muscle Law</strong> muscular injuries; <strong>Nerve Law</strong> nerve damage; <strong>Organ Law</strong> internal wounds. This anatomical division gives the Odchí tradition a very precise specialization.</p>\n<h2>Dark Channels</h2>\n<p>The sixth List is <strong>Dark Channels</strong>. It channels power originating with Tûmrakhí and directly expresses the relationship with the cult. Its use must always be understood in the context of the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Shadow and Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Vracara explicitly serves a Lord of Darkness. The healing Lists can be treated according to their own magical nature; <strong>Dark Channels</strong> requires different attention. Power deliberately received from an entity associated with the Shadow may have much greater spiritual consequences. Learning Evil Lists or Sorcery produces the Corruption effects established by the general rules.</p>\n<h2>Faith and Interpretation</h2>\n<p>A Vracara may be devoutly loyal to her sect, primarily a healer devoted to her people, pragmatic, dissident, or ambitious. The same mechanics can therefore support radically different moral paths.</p>\n<h2>Life among the Variags</h2>\n<p>The Profession is adapted to the Variag way of life. A Vracara must be able to follow a mobile community, treat injuries far from permanent settlements, and function under the conditions of Khand. Riding, Survival, regional lore, First Aid, and culturally appropriate weapons all fit naturally.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Odchí Healer.</strong> Five bodily healing Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Priestess of Tûmrakhí.</strong> Dark Channels, Religion, Influence, and Divination.</p>\n<p><strong>War Healer.</strong> Healing, Riding, weapons, and fieldcraft.</p>\n<p><strong>Clan Adviser.</strong> Influence, Divination, cultural lore, and supporting magic.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident Priestess.</strong> Preserves healing and traditional knowledge while questioning the cult’s darkest obligations.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>For the <strong>Odchí sect</strong>, the Vracara has six Base Spell Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Organ Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dark Channels</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Other Sects</h2>\n<p>Other Vracarathí sects may replace some or all of these Lists according to the aspect of Tûmrakhí they serve. Their existence allows a Game Master to create distinct priestly traditions without changing the fundamental Profession.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara has no direct standard equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>, preserve <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, retain the restriction to Variag women, and use the Odchí sect as the standard six-List package.</p>\n<p>The adaptation emphasizes the tension between genuine healing skill and service to a power linked to the Shadow. Dark Channels, Evil Lists, and Sorcery remain subject to the MERP-RMU rules for Corruption and the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Hybrides","Théurgie + Mentalisme"],"en":["Hybrid Spell-user Professions","Channeling + Mentalism"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-hybrides","professions-hybrides-canalisation-mentalisme"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Vracara","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Mentalism","description":"<h1>Vracara</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Vracara</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Mentalism / Channeling</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Variags of Khand</strong>. She belongs to the female priesthood of <strong>Tûmrakhí, Lord of Darkness</strong>, and practices a magic combining healing, mental discipline, and the channeling of spiritual power directly linked to the cult she serves.</p>\n<p>Several Vracarathí sects represent different aspects of Tûmrakhí. They share a common general training, but their <strong>Base Spell Lists differ by sect</strong>. MERP-RMU uses the <strong>Odchí sect</strong> as its reference: five fields of healing and one List that channels the power of the deity. The Profession is restricted to <strong>women of Variag Culture</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Vracara’s Prime Stats are <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, <strong>Presence (Pr)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Intuition corresponds to Channeling, Presence to Mentalism, and Self Discipline to the control required by demanding religious practice.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara is a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Perception and Power Manipulation, Power Point Development, magical lore, Religion, Divination, Influence, outdoor activities, Meditation, and selected martial skills.</p>\n<h2>The Priesthood of Tûmrakhí</h2>\n<p>The Vracara knows the rites, taboos, prayers, meditative forms, symbols, and obligations of her sect. The <strong>Odchí sect</strong> gives great importance to the body: five of its six Lists concern the direct healing of injuries. Healer and priestess are two aspects of the same character.</p>\n<h2>Healing</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood Law</strong> treats bleeding; <strong>Bone Law</strong> fractures; <strong>Muscle Law</strong> muscular injuries; <strong>Nerve Law</strong> nerve damage; <strong>Organ Law</strong> internal wounds. This anatomical division gives the Odchí tradition a very precise specialization.</p>\n<h2>Dark Channels</h2>\n<p>The sixth List is <strong>Dark Channels</strong>. It channels power originating with Tûmrakhí and directly expresses the relationship with the cult. Its use must always be understood in the context of the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Shadow and Corruption</h2>\n<p>The Vracara explicitly serves a Lord of Darkness. The healing Lists can be treated according to their own magical nature; <strong>Dark Channels</strong> requires different attention. Power deliberately received from an entity associated with the Shadow may have much greater spiritual consequences. Learning Evil Lists or Sorcery produces the Corruption effects established by the general rules.</p>\n<h2>Faith and Interpretation</h2>\n<p>A Vracara may be devoutly loyal to her sect, primarily a healer devoted to her people, pragmatic, dissident, or ambitious. The same mechanics can therefore support radically different moral paths.</p>\n<h2>Life among the Variags</h2>\n<p>The Profession is adapted to the Variag way of life. A Vracara must be able to follow a mobile community, treat injuries far from permanent settlements, and function under the conditions of Khand. Riding, Survival, regional lore, First Aid, and culturally appropriate weapons all fit naturally.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Odchí Healer.</strong> Five bodily healing Lists, First Aid, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Priestess of Tûmrakhí.</strong> Dark Channels, Religion, Influence, and Divination.</p>\n<p><strong>War Healer.</strong> Healing, Riding, weapons, and fieldcraft.</p>\n<p><strong>Clan Adviser.</strong> Influence, Divination, cultural lore, and supporting magic.</p>\n<p><strong>Dissident Priestess.</strong> Preserves healing and traditional knowledge while questioning the cult’s darkest obligations.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>For the <strong>Odchí sect</strong>, the Vracara has six Base Spell Lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bone Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Muscle Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nerve Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Organ Law</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dark Channels</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Other Sects</h2>\n<p>Other Vracarathí sects may replace some or all of these Lists according to the aspect of Tûmrakhí they serve. Their existence allows a Game Master to create distinct priestly traditions without changing the fundamental Profession.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Vracara has no direct standard equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Mentalism / Channeling spell-user</strong>, preserve <strong>Intuition, Presence, and Self Discipline</strong> as Prime Stats, retain the restriction to Variag women, and use the Odchí sect as the standard six-List package.</p>\n<p>The adaptation emphasizes the tension between genuine healing skill and service to a power linked to the Shadow. Dark Channels, Evil Lists, and Sorcery remain subject to the MERP-RMU rules for Corruption and the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"B24jAjjNwcpTllLK"}
{"name":"Wegech","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"wegech","collection":"merp-rmu-compendium","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Wegech, p.157"},"compendiumNativeProfession":true,"nativeProfessionDefinition":{"key":"wegech","sourcePack":"rmu.core","sourceId":null,"sourceName":"Sorcerer","document":{"name":"Wegech","flags":{"merp-ui":{"key":"wegech","collection":"merp-rmu","contentVersion":3,"source":{"type":"rmu-native-chassis-plus-rmss-profession","reference":"RMU Sorcerer hybrid chassis; MERP/RMSS Character Creation v2.5 — Wegech, p.157"}}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Wegech","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Essence","description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Wegech</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Théurgie et d’Essence</strong>, propre aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Elle occupe les fonctions de chamane, prêtresse, guérisseuse, exorciste et gardienne des traditions spirituelles du camp.</p>\n<p>Chaque camp possède normalement une Wegech, « connaisseuse des esprits ». Cette fonction est exclusivement féminine et constitue la seule charge héréditaire de leur société. Son autorité dépasse les questions religieuses et elle agit comme une véritable matriarche.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient les longues préparations rituelles et les pratiques d’exorcisme et de prophétie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est une <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Manipulation et Perception de la Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, activités de plein air, Sorts dirigés, rituels et objets symboliques.</p>\n<h2>Une magie rituelle</h2>\n<p>Ses incantations demandent souvent davantage de préparation que les pratiques occidentales. Autels de bois, bois gravés, pigments, peintures corporelles, peaux couvertes de signes, talismans, pierres et objets ancestraux structurent le rite.</p>\n<h2>Guérison spirituelle et ancêtres</h2>\n<p>La Wegech peut accomplir des guérisons puissantes lorsqu’elle dispose de temps et d’outils appropriés. Les croyances des Hommes des Collines lient certaines maladies à des influences spirituelles, tandis que les ancêtres et les Boghain jouent un rôle protecteur dans la vie du clan.</p>\n<h2>Exorcisme et symboles</h2>\n<p>La Profession excelle contre les présences surnaturelles hostiles. Les ruines, tertres et champs de bataille du Rhudaur donnent une importance particulière à l’exorcisme. Les symboles gravés sur pierre, cairns, grottes ou autels servent de supports à sa magie.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canaux (Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance spirituelle et produit notamment certains effets offensifs.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion.</li>\n<li><strong>Protections (Protections).</strong> Défenses contre différentes forces hostiles.</li>\n<li><strong>Répulsion des Esprits (Exorcisms).</strong> Repousse morts-vivants et certaines entités surnaturelles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Symboliques (Symbolic Ways).</strong> Place des effets magiques dans la pierre et les symboles.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Portes (Gate Mastery).</strong> Invoque des créatures ou présences appropriées.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>La Wegech appartient essentiellement aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Une Wegech loin de sa région peut suivre une quête imposée par les ancêtres, rechercher un totem perdu, fuir un conflit tribal ou poursuivre une influence d’Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est reconstruite comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>, avec <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes des Hommes des Collines.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU conserve les six Listes historiques et utilise les mécanismes RMU correspondants lorsque possible. La ritualisation, les outils votifs et les temps de préparation prolongés constituent des éléments propres à la Profession qui devront rester visibles dans son fonctionnement.</p>"}},"localizations":{"fr":{"name":"Wegech","system":{"description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Wegech</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Théurgie et d’Essence</strong>, propre aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Elle occupe les fonctions de chamane, prêtresse, guérisseuse, exorciste et gardienne des traditions spirituelles du camp.</p>\n<p>Chaque camp possède normalement une Wegech, « connaisseuse des esprits ». Cette fonction est exclusivement féminine et constitue la seule charge héréditaire de leur société. Son autorité dépasse les questions religieuses et elle agit comme une véritable matriarche.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient les longues préparations rituelles et les pratiques d’exorcisme et de prophétie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est une <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Manipulation et Perception de la Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, activités de plein air, Sorts dirigés, rituels et objets symboliques.</p>\n<h2>Une magie rituelle</h2>\n<p>Ses incantations demandent souvent davantage de préparation que les pratiques occidentales. Autels de bois, bois gravés, pigments, peintures corporelles, peaux couvertes de signes, talismans, pierres et objets ancestraux structurent le rite.</p>\n<h2>Guérison spirituelle et ancêtres</h2>\n<p>La Wegech peut accomplir des guérisons puissantes lorsqu’elle dispose de temps et d’outils appropriés. Les croyances des Hommes des Collines lient certaines maladies à des influences spirituelles, tandis que les ancêtres et les Boghain jouent un rôle protecteur dans la vie du clan.</p>\n<h2>Exorcisme et symboles</h2>\n<p>La Profession excelle contre les présences surnaturelles hostiles. Les ruines, tertres et champs de bataille du Rhudaur donnent une importance particulière à l’exorcisme. Les symboles gravés sur pierre, cairns, grottes ou autels servent de supports à sa magie.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canaux (Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance spirituelle et produit notamment certains effets offensifs.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion.</li>\n<li><strong>Protections (Protections).</strong> Défenses contre différentes forces hostiles.</li>\n<li><strong>Répulsion des Esprits (Exorcisms).</strong> Repousse morts-vivants et certaines entités surnaturelles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Symboliques (Symbolic Ways).</strong> Place des effets magiques dans la pierre et les symboles.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Portes (Gate Mastery).</strong> Invoque des créatures ou présences appropriées.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>La Wegech appartient essentiellement aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Une Wegech loin de sa région peut suivre une quête imposée par les ancêtres, rechercher un totem perdu, fuir un conflit tribal ou poursuivre une influence d’Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est reconstruite comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>, avec <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes des Hommes des Collines.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU conserve les six Listes historiques et utilise les mécanismes RMU correspondants lorsque possible. La ritualisation, les outils votifs et les temps de préparation prolongés constituent des éléments propres à la Profession qui devront rester visibles dans son fonctionnement.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Wegech","system":{"description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Wegech</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Essence</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. She simultaneously serves as shaman, priestess, healer, exorcist, and guardian of the camp’s spiritual traditions. Her magic rests on spirits of nature, the clan’s dead, ritual symbols, and votive objects, giving her spellcraft a form very different from western magical traditions.</p>\n<p>Among the Hillmen, each camp normally has a Wegech, literally a “knower of spirits.” The role is exclusively female and is the only hereditary office in their society. The Wegech acts as a matriarch whose authority extends far beyond religious questions.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Wegech’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the concentration required for prolonged ritual work.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Perception, Religion, ritual lore, Influence, natural knowledge, healing skills, Craft, and the social abilities required by a matriarchal role.</p>\n<h2>Ritual Magic</h2>\n<p>Wegech magic is strongly ritualized. Spells may require prepared stones, body paint, skins marked with signs, talismans, ancestral objects, and carved stones. Such practice makes her magic immediately recognizable. Losing ritual equipment can become a serious handicap, forcing the Wegech to improvise new tools from available resources.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Healing</h2>\n<p>When she has sufficient time and appropriate tools, the Wegech can perform especially powerful spiritual healing. She often acts after immediate danger has passed: preparing the patient, purifying the spirit, carrying out the necessary rites, and only then beginning the healing itself.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices precise and rapid magical medicine; the <strong>Wegech</strong> excels when treatment can be ritualized and the physical and spiritual dimensions of harm addressed together.</p>\n<h2>Ancestors and Spirits</h2>\n<p>The Hillmen interpret some illnesses and wounds through a spiritual worldview. The <strong>Boghain</strong>, ghosts of ancestors, are believed to protect certain places. The Wegech serves as intermediary with such presences and may interpret dreams, coincidences, or unusual phenomena as messages.</p>\n<h2>Personal Totems</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Ail-legan</strong>, “little jewels,” are pieces of copper chosen by the Wegech for the clan’s children. They become personal totems worn throughout life. Losing one is a major humiliation and may lead to a difficult quest to obtain a new totem.</p>\n<h2>Exorcism</h2>\n<p><strong>Exorcisms</strong> repels undead and certain entities. In Rhudaur and former Arnor, this may become essential when dealing with barrows, ruins, spectres, and manifestations inherited from Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Symbols and Summoning</h2>\n<p><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong> places spells into prepared stones and symbols. <strong>Gate Mastery</strong> can summon appropriate creatures or presences. Calling an entity associated with the Shadow immediately engages the rules for Corruption or the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Camp Healer.</strong> Protections, natural lore, and healing rites.</p>\n<p><strong>Exorcist.</strong> Exorcisms, spiritual lore, and Power Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of the Ancestors.</strong> Communal Ways, divination, and clan traditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Symbols.</strong> Symbolic Ways, Craft, and ritual lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Summoner.</strong> Gate Mastery and knowledge of spirits and creatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Matriarch.</strong> Influence, traditions, Leadership, and social skills.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Channels</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Protections</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exorcisms</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gate Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Wegech belongs essentially to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. A Wegech encountered far from her homeland should have a clear reason: a quest imposed by the ancestors, the search for a lost Ail-legan, exile, a mission against an influence of Angmar, or the need to obtain knowledge absent from her own tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>, with <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>, while preserving the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For RMU implementation, modern mechanics matching the function of these Lists are used where possible. The main specific adaptation concerns the <strong>ritualization of magic</strong>: extended preparation times, votive tools, and powerful spiritual healing are essential features of the Profession.</p>"}}}},"compendiumSourceKey":"wegech","compendiumPack":"merp-rmu-professions","compendiumSourceFile":"data/merp-rmu/khazad.json#rmuNativeProfessions","compendiumFolderKey":"professions-hybrides-essence-canalisation","compendiumBuildLanguage":"en","compendiumLocalizations":{"fr":{"name":"Wegech","system":{"description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>La <strong>Wegech</strong> est une utilisatrice hybride de <strong>Théurgie et d’Essence</strong>, propre aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Elle occupe les fonctions de chamane, prêtresse, guérisseuse, exorciste et gardienne des traditions spirituelles du camp.</p>\n<p>Chaque camp possède normalement une Wegech, « connaisseuse des esprits ». Cette fonction est exclusivement féminine et constitue la seule charge héréditaire de leur société. Son autorité dépasse les questions religieuses et elle agit comme une véritable matriarche.</p>\n<h2>Caractéristiques primordiales</h2>\n<p>Les caractéristiques primordiales sont <strong>Empathie (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong> et <strong>Autodiscipline (SD)</strong>. Empathie et Intuition correspondent aux deux royaumes ; Autodiscipline soutient les longues préparations rituelles et les pratiques d’exorcisme et de prophétie.</p>\n<h2>Orientation technique</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est une <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>. Elle favorise Listes de base, Manipulation et Perception de la Puissance, connaissances magiques et religieuses, activités de plein air, Sorts dirigés, rituels et objets symboliques.</p>\n<h2>Une magie rituelle</h2>\n<p>Ses incantations demandent souvent davantage de préparation que les pratiques occidentales. Autels de bois, bois gravés, pigments, peintures corporelles, peaux couvertes de signes, talismans, pierres et objets ancestraux structurent le rite.</p>\n<h2>Guérison spirituelle et ancêtres</h2>\n<p>La Wegech peut accomplir des guérisons puissantes lorsqu’elle dispose de temps et d’outils appropriés. Les croyances des Hommes des Collines lient certaines maladies à des influences spirituelles, tandis que les ancêtres et les Boghain jouent un rôle protecteur dans la vie du clan.</p>\n<h2>Exorcisme et symboles</h2>\n<p>La Profession excelle contre les présences surnaturelles hostiles. Les ruines, tertres et champs de bataille du Rhudaur donnent une importance particulière à l’exorcisme. Les symboles gravés sur pierre, cairns, grottes ou autels servent de supports à sa magie.</p>\n<h2>Listes de sorts de base</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canaux (Channels).</strong> Canalise une puissance spirituelle et produit notamment certains effets offensifs.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Communales (Communal Ways).</strong> Divination et communion.</li>\n<li><strong>Protections (Protections).</strong> Défenses contre différentes forces hostiles.</li>\n<li><strong>Répulsion des Esprits (Exorcisms).</strong> Repousse morts-vivants et certaines entités surnaturelles.</li>\n<li><strong>Voies Symboliques (Symbolic Ways).</strong> Place des effets magiques dans la pierre et les symboles.</li>\n<li><strong>Maîtrise des Portes (Gate Mastery).</strong> Invoque des créatures ou présences appropriées.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place dans la Terre du Milieu</h2>\n<p>La Wegech appartient essentiellement aux Hommes des Collines du Rhudaur. Une Wegech loin de sa région peut suivre une quête imposée par les ancêtres, rechercher un totem perdu, fuir un conflit tribal ou poursuivre une influence d’Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Adaptation RMU / MERP</h2>\n<p>La Wegech est reconstruite comme <strong>hybride Théurgie / Essence</strong>, avec <strong>Empathie, Intuition et Autodiscipline</strong> et restriction aux femmes des Hommes des Collines.</p>\n<p>MERP-RMU conserve les six Listes historiques et utilise les mécanismes RMU correspondants lorsque possible. La ritualisation, les outils votifs et les temps de préparation prolongés constituent des éléments propres à la Profession qui devront rester visibles dans son fonctionnement.</p>"}},"en":{"name":"Wegech","system":{"description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Wegech</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Essence</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. She simultaneously serves as shaman, priestess, healer, exorcist, and guardian of the camp’s spiritual traditions. Her magic rests on spirits of nature, the clan’s dead, ritual symbols, and votive objects, giving her spellcraft a form very different from western magical traditions.</p>\n<p>Among the Hillmen, each camp normally has a Wegech, literally a “knower of spirits.” The role is exclusively female and is the only hereditary office in their society. The Wegech acts as a matriarch whose authority extends far beyond religious questions.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Wegech’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the concentration required for prolonged ritual work.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Perception, Religion, ritual lore, Influence, natural knowledge, healing skills, Craft, and the social abilities required by a matriarchal role.</p>\n<h2>Ritual Magic</h2>\n<p>Wegech magic is strongly ritualized. Spells may require prepared stones, body paint, skins marked with signs, talismans, ancestral objects, and carved stones. Such practice makes her magic immediately recognizable. Losing ritual equipment can become a serious handicap, forcing the Wegech to improvise new tools from available resources.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Healing</h2>\n<p>When she has sufficient time and appropriate tools, the Wegech can perform especially powerful spiritual healing. She often acts after immediate danger has passed: preparing the patient, purifying the spirit, carrying out the necessary rites, and only then beginning the healing itself.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices precise and rapid magical medicine; the <strong>Wegech</strong> excels when treatment can be ritualized and the physical and spiritual dimensions of harm addressed together.</p>\n<h2>Ancestors and Spirits</h2>\n<p>The Hillmen interpret some illnesses and wounds through a spiritual worldview. The <strong>Boghain</strong>, ghosts of ancestors, are believed to protect certain places. The Wegech serves as intermediary with such presences and may interpret dreams, coincidences, or unusual phenomena as messages.</p>\n<h2>Personal Totems</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Ail-legan</strong>, “little jewels,” are pieces of copper chosen by the Wegech for the clan’s children. They become personal totems worn throughout life. Losing one is a major humiliation and may lead to a difficult quest to obtain a new totem.</p>\n<h2>Exorcism</h2>\n<p><strong>Exorcisms</strong> repels undead and certain entities. In Rhudaur and former Arnor, this may become essential when dealing with barrows, ruins, spectres, and manifestations inherited from Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Symbols and Summoning</h2>\n<p><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong> places spells into prepared stones and symbols. <strong>Gate Mastery</strong> can summon appropriate creatures or presences. Calling an entity associated with the Shadow immediately engages the rules for Corruption or the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Camp Healer.</strong> Protections, natural lore, and healing rites.</p>\n<p><strong>Exorcist.</strong> Exorcisms, spiritual lore, and Power Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of the Ancestors.</strong> Communal Ways, divination, and clan traditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Symbols.</strong> Symbolic Ways, Craft, and ritual lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Summoner.</strong> Gate Mastery and knowledge of spirits and creatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Matriarch.</strong> Influence, traditions, Leadership, and social skills.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Channels</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Protections</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exorcisms</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gate Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Wegech belongs essentially to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. A Wegech encountered far from her homeland should have a clear reason: a quest imposed by the ancestors, the search for a lost Ail-legan, exile, a mission against an influence of Angmar, or the need to obtain knowledge absent from her own tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>, with <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>, while preserving the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For RMU implementation, modern mechanics matching the function of these Lists are used where possible. The main specific adaptation concerns the <strong>ritualization of magic</strong>: extended preparation times, votive tools, and powerful spiritual healing are essential features of the Profession.</p>"}}},"compendiumAgeVariants":null,"compendiumFolderPath":{"fr":["Professions Hybrides","Essence + Théurgie"],"en":["Hybrid Spell-user Professions","Essence + Channeling"]},"compendiumFolderKeyPath":["professions-hybrides","professions-hybrides-essence-canalisation"],"compendiumResolverBacked":true}},"system":{"book":"MERP-RMU / RMU Spell Law","profession":"Wegech","spellCastingGroup":"Hybrid","numberOfBaseLists":6,"realms":"Channeling,Essence","description":"<h1>Wegech</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Wegech</strong> is a hybrid <strong>Channeling / Essence</strong> spell-user unique to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. She simultaneously serves as shaman, priestess, healer, exorcist, and guardian of the camp’s spiritual traditions. Her magic rests on spirits of nature, the clan’s dead, ritual symbols, and votive objects, giving her spellcraft a form very different from western magical traditions.</p>\n<p>Among the Hillmen, each camp normally has a Wegech, literally a “knower of spirits.” The role is exclusively female and is the only hereditary office in their society. The Wegech acts as a matriarch whose authority extends far beyond religious questions.</p>\n<h2>Prime Stats</h2>\n<p>The Wegech’s Prime Stats are <strong>Empathy (Em)</strong>, <strong>Intuition (In)</strong>, and <strong>Self Discipline (SD)</strong>. Empathy corresponds to Essence, Intuition to Channeling, and Self Discipline to the concentration required for prolonged ritual work.</p>\n<h2>Technical Orientation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech is a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>. Development favours Base Spell Lists, Power Manipulation, Power Perception, Religion, ritual lore, Influence, natural knowledge, healing skills, Craft, and the social abilities required by a matriarchal role.</p>\n<h2>Ritual Magic</h2>\n<p>Wegech magic is strongly ritualized. Spells may require prepared stones, body paint, skins marked with signs, talismans, ancestral objects, and carved stones. Such practice makes her magic immediately recognizable. Losing ritual equipment can become a serious handicap, forcing the Wegech to improvise new tools from available resources.</p>\n<h2>Spiritual Healing</h2>\n<p>When she has sufficient time and appropriate tools, the Wegech can perform especially powerful spiritual healing. She often acts after immediate danger has passed: preparing the patient, purifying the spirit, carrying out the necessary rites, and only then beginning the healing itself.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Lay Healer</strong> practices precise and rapid magical medicine; the <strong>Wegech</strong> excels when treatment can be ritualized and the physical and spiritual dimensions of harm addressed together.</p>\n<h2>Ancestors and Spirits</h2>\n<p>The Hillmen interpret some illnesses and wounds through a spiritual worldview. The <strong>Boghain</strong>, ghosts of ancestors, are believed to protect certain places. The Wegech serves as intermediary with such presences and may interpret dreams, coincidences, or unusual phenomena as messages.</p>\n<h2>Personal Totems</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Ail-legan</strong>, “little jewels,” are pieces of copper chosen by the Wegech for the clan’s children. They become personal totems worn throughout life. Losing one is a major humiliation and may lead to a difficult quest to obtain a new totem.</p>\n<h2>Exorcism</h2>\n<p><strong>Exorcisms</strong> repels undead and certain entities. In Rhudaur and former Arnor, this may become essential when dealing with barrows, ruins, spectres, and manifestations inherited from Angmar.</p>\n<h2>Symbols and Summoning</h2>\n<p><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong> places spells into prepared stones and symbols. <strong>Gate Mastery</strong> can summon appropriate creatures or presences. Calling an entity associated with the Shadow immediately engages the rules for Corruption or the Risk of Attracting the Shadow.</p>\n<h2>Possible Profiles</h2>\n<p><strong>Camp Healer.</strong> Protections, natural lore, and healing rites.</p>\n<p><strong>Exorcist.</strong> Exorcisms, spiritual lore, and Power Perception.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeper of the Ancestors.</strong> Communal Ways, divination, and clan traditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Mistress of Symbols.</strong> Symbolic Ways, Craft, and ritual lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Summoner.</strong> Gate Mastery and knowledge of spirits and creatures.</p>\n<p><strong>Matriarch.</strong> Influence, traditions, Leadership, and social skills.</p>\n<h2>Base Spell Lists</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has <strong>six Base Spell Lists</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Channels</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Communal Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Protections</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exorcisms</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symbolic Ways</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gate Mastery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Place in Middle-earth</h2>\n<p>The Wegech belongs essentially to the <strong>Hillmen of Rhudaur</strong>. A Wegech encountered far from her homeland should have a clear reason: a quest imposed by the ancestors, the search for a lost Ail-legan, exile, a mission against an influence of Angmar, or the need to obtain knowledge absent from her own tradition.</p>\n<h2>RMU / MERP Adaptation</h2>\n<p>The Wegech has no direct professional equivalent in RMU. We reconstruct her as a <strong>hybrid Channeling / Essence spell-user</strong>, with <strong>Empathy, Intuition, and Self Discipline</strong>, while preserving the six historical Lists.</p>\n<p>For RMU implementation, modern mechanics matching the function of these Lists are used where possible. The main specific adaptation concerns the <strong>ritualization of magic</strong>: extended preparation times, votive tools, and powerful spiritual healing are essential features of the Profession.</p>"},"type":"profession","img":"systems/rmu/icons/svg/iron_crown_w.svg","effects":[],"ownership":{"default":0},"_id":"uIs1WEyUPNgVYSRM"}
